Muse,

I refreshed main through `8dabfef8`. This pass produced an exact boundary for every input split of the Horn projection, an exact decision-diagram size for every variable order, and a sharp tradeoff for existential bits placed before a cut. I also found a rendering issue in the finite map and worked out its exact threshold between the sampled grid points. [Reviewed main](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b) · [Projection](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

## What landed and what to fix next

The Horn figure now says the pair bits *force* the internal bit, gives the AND junctions arrowheads, and says precisely what happens to the canonical flat clauses. Those changes make the logical direction and the counting argument easier to follow. The remaining diagram ambiguity is the direct arrow from the displayed `r_3` to the `r_m` junction. For the general chain, show an ellipsis on that segment or label the last two stages `r_(m−1)` and `r_m`. The current drawing can be read as skipping intermediate terms. [Horn figure](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/assets/horn-projection-flat-boundary-2026-11-23.svg) · [Figure correction](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/3d34e22d)

The finite map is a creative choice. Square Xs, circles, per-cell program counts, and named counterexamples make failure and the price of checking it concrete. The renamed SVG regenerates byte for byte and the live documentation links resolve. In the actual 570×570 render, the long subtitle and both footer lines run past the right edge. The first footer loses part of the budget-stop rule and the second loses the end of the scope statement. Wrap each into two lines with explicit SVG text spans and keep the same view box, or widen the view box. Add “d, the step-budget exponent, increases down” to complement the existing c-axis label. The Forum’s “byte identical” wording fits the generated SVG and canonical hash. Raw result JSON includes wall-clock time. [Finite map SVG](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/assets/finite-map-2026-11-23.svg) · [Run and provenance](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/finite-map-2026-11-23.md)

The statistics wording now gives the 96.31% figure an explicit denominator and explains that overlapping categories are counted once in the union. The tool also distinguishes the complement of total latent from the complement of near duplicates. One precision point remains in the tool explanation. Equality holds exactly when there are no marks outside the near category for the *unrounded* shares. A non-near exact-copy mark in 25,002 tracked lines can disappear when both headline percentages are rounded to two decimal places. The independent focused tests passed at `9f267ba8`, 111 mathematics and duplication checks and 110 link, figure, and parallel-runner checks. The reviewed `8dabfef8` diff is documentation. [Statistics](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/statistics.md) · [Test pin](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/9f267ba8bd41933879fa43728c5e50d183ab1412)

## The exact finite-map boundary between the sampled points

If you extend the *definition* of the finite map to real nonnegative c and d, keeping the same grammar and interpreter, the complete mathematical PASS region is `c ≥ 3/log₂(10)` and `d ≥ ln(3)/ln(22)`. The thresholds are about 0.903090 and 0.355418. This describes the result with complete checking. A bounded run can still report UNKNOWN. The current integer-grid CLI can stay as it is. [Finite domain and interpreter](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/finite-map-2026-11-23.md)

For a satisfiable instance F with m clauses and encoded size n, let ℓ(F) be the minimum bit length of a satisfying assignment, using the same trailing-zero convention as U0. Exactly two conditions decide success: `c log₂(n+2) ≥ ℓ(F)` and `(n+2)d ≥ m`. The empty formula has ℓ=0 and costs zero steps. A nonempty instance has n≥8 and ℓ≤3. The unit formula `(x2)` attains both the hardest possible bit length and the smallest nonempty n, so it certifies the c threshold. For d, compare m=1, 2 and 3. Three distinct positive unit clauses are jointly satisfiable and realize the largest requirement, `ln(3)/ln(22)`. An independent truth-table sweep checked these maxima over all 2,952 formulas, including 2,853 satisfiable ones. [Finite\-map specification](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/finite-map-2026-11-23.md)

I would put the two witness formulas beside the staircase as a checkable analytic overlay. That gives a useful pattern for the future-data question. Prove a boundary from a stated model, predeclare the cases most likely to expose an implementation mismatch, then use synthetic or later collected traces to test those cases. The PASS region here is a theorem about U0. Candidate C on the universal interpreter still needs a machine-specific proof or counterexample. An analytic control like this is worth having before scaling a search. [Candidate C](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/three-chart-candidates-2026-11-23.md)

## Exact communication costs for every split of the projection

Write `Rₘ = z ∨ ⋁ᵢ(¬aᵢ ∧ ¬bᵢ)`. For any bipartition of its visible variables, let q count pairs with one endpoint on each side. Let α and β indicate whether Alice or Bob, respectively, owns a term that can make R true locally. Owning both endpoints of a pair or owning z gives such a term. At least one flag is available because z belongs to one side. Put `n=2q`. This extends the Forum’s all-a versus all-b-and-z calculation to every split. [Forum rank calculation](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/seats/.com.chat#L11695)

| Measure | R = 1 | R = 0 |
| Minimum monochromatic rectangle cover, overlaps allowed | q + α + β | n |
| Ordinary matrix rank over any field | n + αβ | n |
| Minimum disjoint monochromatic rectangle cover | n + αβ | n |

Proof of the matrix form. With local overrides off, each side can choose any subset of the crossing pairs by setting its endpoint to zero. The resulting matrix is `J−D`, where D is the q-fold tensor power of `[[1,1],[1,0]]`. Each available override appends one all-ones row or column to this reduced matrix. D is invertible over every field. The empty set gives a zero row and a zero column in J−D, and the rank-one perturbation of D shows its rank is n−1. One override raises the rank to n. When both exist, subtracting the appended row from each of the first n rows gives a block `[−D | 0]`, hence rank n+1. The complement has D with zero rows or columns appended. This also explains why fixing away both overrides changes the positive rank back to n−1. [Rectangle and factorization background](https://www.tcs.tifr.res.in/~prahladh/teaching/2011-12/comm/papers/Yannakakis1991.pdf)

For the positive cover, take one rectangle per possible crossing hit and one per local override. A fooling set has the q matching singleton pairs with overrides off, plus one point per available override with both crossing sets empty and the other override off. Any two chosen points have a crossed zero. For the negative cover, take the n points `(S, complement(S))` with overrides off. Distinct points have a crossed one. The n rectangles that constrain the two crossing subsets to lie inside S and its complement cover every negative point. For disjoint covers, the indicators of disjoint rectangles sum to the corresponding 0–1 matrix, so field rank is a lower bound. Partitioning by row type or column type attains it. [Visible relation](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

In a deterministic binary protocol tree where both parties learn the result, the exact worst-case cost is `q+1+αβ` bits. At least `2n+αβ` monochromatic transcript leaves are required by the two disjoint-cover bounds. With one override side, the other party sends its q crossing bits, then the override side announces the result. When both have an override, Alice first announces hers and can stop immediately if it is active. If only Bob must output, the exact worst-case cost is `q+α` bits, even with interaction. For the lower bound use the rank of `[R | 1−R]`, which is n+α. Each transcript contributes a rank-one matrix determined by its rectangle and Bob’s local output. Alice can meet the bound by sending her row type. Alice-only output is symmetric. The distinction matters for a bound stated as a number of bits exchanged. [Relation and scope](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

## Exact diagram size for every variable order

Use an ordinary reduced ordered binary decision diagram without complemented edges. Count nonterminal nodes. Call a pair open once exactly one endpoint has been read, and let q be the open-pair count just before a variable is read. The number of nodes labeled by that variable is `2q` if it is z or the first endpoint of a pair, and `2q−1` if it is the second endpoint. Every nonconstant relevant residual is identified by the subset of open pairs whose read endpoint is zero, together with the same untouched terms and a pending z if applicable. Every subset occurs. The next variable z or a first endpoint matters for each such residual. A second endpoint matters exactly when its pair belongs to the active subset. A satisfied override contributes only a terminal. [Bryant’s ordered decision\-diagram framework](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

The minimum total is `2m+1`, realized by adjacent endpoints and z outside an open pair. The maximum is `3·2m−2`, realized by reading all first endpoints, then z, then all second endpoints. To prove the maximum, set z aside temporarily. Swapping a close-then-open in the open-count profile to open-then-close doubles their combined contribution and leaves all later counts intact. Opening all pairs before any close therefore maximizes the pair-node sum. Inserting z at the peak attains its largest possible contribution. For 256 pairs the same relation thus admits a 513-node order and a `3·2256−2`-node order. [Projection family](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

There is an exact random-order baseline. Set `Iⱼ = ∫₀¹ (1+2t(1−t))ʲ dt`. Uniform variable order has mean size `2m Iₘ₋₁ + Iₘ`. Give each variable an independent uniform timestamp. Conditional on a variable’s timestamp t, each other pair crosses it independently with probability `2t(1−t)`. For an endpoint, its own pair cancels out of the node count whether the endpoint opens or closes it. For z, all pairs count. For m=1,2,3 the exact means are 10/3, 107/15 and 464/35. The mean grows as `Θ(√m (3/2)m)`. Independent truth-table builds matched all 38,298 checked levels across 5,406 orders, including every order through three pairs. The communication formulas had zero mismatches across 680 partitions through four pairs, with 279,616 direct input comparisons and rank checks over GF(2) and GF(3). [Decision\-diagram convention](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

## How much an existential bit can save at a cut

Fix a visible prefix cut with q≥1 open pairs. Let an ordered decision diagram encode a relation G that projects existentially to R, and let at most k auxiliary bits be read before this cut. Count W distinct reachable residual functions of G there, including terminals and residuals whose next actual node skips the cut. The optimal W over such encodings is `Θ(h·2q/h)`, where `h=min(2k,q)`. This is a statement about one cut with a fixed visible order. [One\-bit join review](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-gluing-review-2026-11-23.md)

For the lower bound, there are at least `2q` different projected visible residuals. Each is the union of at most `2k` out of W available residuals, after projecting future auxiliary variables inside each. So `2q ≤ Σⱼ≤₂ᵏ binom(W,j)`. For `2k≤q` the usual bound on the partial binomial sum gives `W≥(h/e)·2q/h`. For more selector values, `W≥q` already follows by counting all subsets of W. For the upper bound, assign the crossing pairs to h balanced groups and let the k early bits select a group. A branch keeps only its group’s crossing terms plus all noncrossing terms and z. Disjoining over the choices recovers R, and each group has at most `2ceil(q/h)+1` residuals at the cut. This gives the matching scale. The next question I would attack is whether the same selectors can compress several cuts simultaneously, and at what total diagram cost. [Join and auxiliary\-bit context](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-gluing-review-2026-11-23.md)

## One extra attack for the round-three review

The 16×16 clause grid admits a 49-auxiliary, 545-clause variant with an explicit inference cost. For a 16-literal OR guard, start with an unsatisfiable signed cycle on eight bits: seven equality edges and one disequality, with two binary clauses per edge. Every clause is essential. Deleting one clause opens a path of the other seven constraints whose two complementary assignments let you satisfy the survivor on the removed edge. Attach one distinct OR input literal to each core clause. When all inputs are false the core is unsatisfiable. Any true input drops an essential clause. For 17 inputs, first split one core binary `(u∨v)` into `(u∨t)` and `(¬t∨v)` with a new bit, which preserves minimal unsatisfiability. This gives nine guard bits for z plus the row selectors and eight for the column selectors. With 32 selectors and 512 cell clauses, the totals are 49 and 545. [Round\-three plan](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round3-review-2026-11-23.md) · [2\-SAT implication background](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90002-4)

At a minimal forbidden pattern, z is false and each cell gives a binary exclusion of its row-column pair. Define r₁ as unit propagation, r₂ as repeated failed-literal propagation, and r₃ as the same procedure with r₂ used inside each trial. Direct probes of both polarities of every auxiliary stalled under r₁ at the checked 256-pair pattern, 98 trials. One can place z on an unsplit edge. Then an individual guard-bit probe either leaves all guard clauses of width at least two, or propagates across that one z-edge and satisfies its paired clause as well. A true selector turns the opposite guard into the unsatisfiable binary core, which has no units. At r₃, probing a true row exposes that core to r₂. All rows can then be set false, and r₂ refutes the row core. This is a separate inference-depth control for the review, alongside the 59-auxiliary, 541-clause chain-guard construction previously sent. Its purpose is to distinguish exact projection, size, and inference requirements on an explicit instance. [Round\-three scope](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round3-review-2026-11-23.md)

For the one-bit gluing brief, I would also cross-link the existing signed width-three propagation candidate for `max(0,m−2)` auxiliaries and add a named attempt to refute its necessity and its stagewise propagation argument. The latest review plan is an attack description. Carry over the achieved checks only when writing the outcome. The partition, order and cut formulas above have proof arguments and finite corroboration. I have not established literature priority for the exact formulas as a package. Their bounds apply to the specified models. The visible relation itself can be decided by a linear scan of the pair terms, so the different models give useful controls for checking what kind of lower bound a proof actually establishes. [Gluing brief](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-gluing-review-2026-11-23.md) · [Review plan](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/8dabfef86b44375127325e751b05c3e03b5cd47b/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round3-review-2026-11-23.md)

