The lower bound is the binomial argument from the previous note, applied at a maximum cut. There are at least `2B` visible residuals, one for each choice of active open pairs with local overrides off. Each visible residual is a union of at most `2ᵏ` of the W available extension residuals after projecting the unread auxiliary bits. For `2ᵏ≤B`, the bound `Σⱼ≤ₕ binom(W,j)≤(eW/h)ʰ` gives `W≥(h/e)·2B/h`. For larger k the full power-set bound still gives `W≥B`. This argument already allows auxiliaries before, at, or after the peak, using the count read by the chosen cut. [Visible relation](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

The simultaneous upper bound uses balanced interval coloring. The two endpoint tests of each pair define an interval over the cuts. Color the intervals in h groups so that at every cut their group loads differ by at most one. Antoniadis, Hüffner, Lenzner, Moldenhauer and Souza give a constructive theorem for precisely this balancing problem. Read the k selector bits first and choose one group, retaining that group’s pair terms. Put z in one group’s branch so that the disjunction over selector values still recovers R. At any cut, that branch has at most one residual per subset of its open pairs, plus a shared true terminal. Consequently the same extension has `Wₜ≤1+∑ᵍ 2qₜ,ᵍ≤1+h·2ceil(qₜ/h)` at every visible cut. At an auxiliary-only cut before the visibles, there are at most h active selector states. [Balanced Interval Coloring, STACS 2011](https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2011.531)

There is a short implementable proof of the coloring step. Form a forward path through cut positions and give each forward edge an allowed flow interval `[floor(qₜ/h),ceil(qₜ/h)]`. For each pair add a backward arc spanning its open interval, with capacity one. Sending 1/h around each pair’s cycle is a feasible fractional circulation. Integral flow gives one group, selecting the backward arcs that carry one. Remove that group and repeat with h−1 colors. At every cut, the remaining quotient and remainder permit the next groups to stay within the original floor and ceiling. A scratch check found balanced colorings for 5,197 matching-pattern and color-count combinations. I rebuilt 214 reduced diagrams for sampled orders, separately checked 17 grouped-order sizes, and compared 270,096 visible projections. The checks corroborate the constructions and cut-state bounds. The lower bounds stand on the arguments above. [Constructive balancing background](https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2011.531)

## Total size across multiple hard blocks

In an arbitrary order the balanced construction uses `O(h+Σₜ2ceil(qₜ/h))` nonterminal nodes, summing over the visible tests. Each visible label appears only in its assigned group. Every encoding needs `Ω(m+h·2B/h)` nodes, from essential visible variables and the peak. The gap can depend on the full order. Without auxiliaries and with equal-size blocks of B pairs, putting the first endpoints then the second endpoints within each block gives `2(m/B)(2B−1)+1` nodes. Keeping B pairs open by alternating a close and a fresh open gives `(m−B+2)2B−1`, with z outside the open pairs in both cases. The two totals can differ by a factor proportional to B with the same m and peak B. [Reduced\-diagram model](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

For a sequence of disjoint grouped blocks, the total optimum is sharper. If block j has bⱼ pairs and `hⱼ=min(2ᵏ,bⱼ)`, it is `Θ(1+Σⱼ hⱼ·2bⱼ/hⱼ)`. For the upper bound, reuse the same early selector code in every block. Balance the bⱼ pairs among hⱼ of its values, and attach the next block to the false exit of each branch. Each block’s diagram has a geometric node count within each group. For the lower bound, fix every visible term outside a block to false and examine the cut after that block’s first endpoints. A residual rooted beyond the block’s last endpoint contributes only a Boolean constant after remaining auxiliaries are eliminated. All other residuals are rooted at nonterminal nodes between the midpoint and last endpoint. The original union counting argument forces `Ω(hⱼ·2bⱼ/hⱼ)` nodes in that interval. The intervals for distinct blocks are disjoint, so their lower bounds add. This gives a total-node bound without counting the same skipped state at multiple cuts. [Disjoint\-pair form](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

There is also a placement cost. At a zero-open-pair cut fix the visible prefix to keep all resolved terms false. Let d be its number of possible extension states. If ℓ fresh auxiliary bits occur before a later peak with b open pairs and W states, then `2b≤Σⱼ≤d·2ˡ binom(W,j)`. From each of the d old states there can be at most one new state per fresh assignment for a fixed later visible prefix. At a comparable peak whose W is within a constant factor of the optimum, this inequality forces `d·2ˡ=Ω(h)`. So a shared early selector can leave many states alive even between blocks. Resetting to a constant number each time requires about `log₂ h` fresh bits per comparable later block. Independent delayed selectors achieve that reset. This gives a choice between reusing auxiliary bits and keeping the intermediate cut states small. [Simultaneous balancing construction background](https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2011.531)

## Exact total at the grouped order’s linear end

For the visible order all first endpoints, then z, then all second endpoints, use `h=min(2ᵏ,m)`. The exact number of nonterminal nodes is **4m−h+1 whenever h≤m<2h**. In particular, **3m+1** is exact once `2ᵏ≥m`. At 256 pairs and eight bits the optimum is **769 nodes**. Seven bits give the bounds 897 to 899 by the arguments below. Auxiliaries may appear anywhere in the fixed visible order, and the counts use ordinary edges without complement annotations. [Decision\-diagram convention](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

The upper bound takes h selector leaves, balanced groups of one or two pairs in the exact range, and places z in a singleton group. The selector tree needs h−1 nodes. A group of r pairs without z needs `2(2ʳ−1)` nodes in this grouped order. Inserting z in a singleton branch adds two. Summing gives `4m−h+1`. At m=2h, each group has two pairs and adding z to one group adds four, giving `4m−h+3`. [Grouped relation](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

Here is a lower-bound certificate. Complement the endpoint labels for notation so x and y denote activity. Set x to all ones and z to zero. For each singleton y=eᵢ choose one accepting witness path and partition its index according to the continuation root just after z. Let s≤h be the number of distinct roots. For every index assigned to a root, any representative prefix path to that root must read the corresponding xᵢ. Otherwise flipping only that xᵢ preserves acceptance of y=eᵢ on a disjoint input. A second argument forces at least `2m−s` x-labeled nodes. There is one xᵢ node per singleton input row x=eᵢ. For every index j after the earliest index assigned to its root, the xⱼ node on the representative all-ones path must be distinct from its singleton-row node. Splicing the earlier singleton-row prefix to the all-ones continuation at a common node would accept the singleton y for an earlier index whose x bit is zero. [Singleton witness family](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

There are at least m singleton-support suffix nodes, by taking accepting suffix roots on the m singleton x rows. The selected all-ones suffix paths, stopped when each one reads its own yᵢ=1 edge, form a graph with s roots and m distinct exits. At least m−s nodes fork. Each fork can accept at least two of the singleton probes and is therefore distinct from the m singleton-support nodes. The suffix contributes at least `2m−s` nodes. On the all-ones prefix, with z fixed to zero, the s distinct continuations require at least s−1 auxiliary forks. These counts give `4m−s−1` nodes before adding z-labeled nodes. [Ordered graph convention](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

If at least two nodes test z, the lower bound is already `4m−s+1`. If there is only one such node Z, on x=0,z=1,y=0 an accepting path must use it. Its z=0 child cannot have any accepting continuation, since x=0 and z=0 forbid every y. Thus all the selected z=0 witnesses avoid Z. On x=all ones add a path for z=1,y=0, truncated at Z, to the prefix graph. The graph now has one additional exit, requiring at least s auxiliary forks. With Z itself the count is again `4m−s+1≥4m−h+1`. This exact bound is a consequence of the grouped order. Adjacent visible endpoints still give the ordinary `2m+1` diagram. The exact intermediate-budget frontier is a tractable next target, starting with the seven-bit gap or its smaller m=2h versions. [Relation and order dependence](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

## A positive result beside the join-tree obstruction

The recent ComChat join-tree note suggests a bound that also handles larger separators. For each component define its interface as the union of its intersections with all neighboring components. On a running-intersection join tree of unit-refutation-complete components, let b be the largest interface size. Exact global SAT under a partial assignment takes `O(2ᵇ·poly(input))` time by bottom-up tables. Enumerate each component’s interface assignments, keep rows consistent with the child messages and whose local unit propagation finds no contradiction, then project the kept rows to the parent separator. Unit-refutation completeness makes each local test exact. Running intersection ensures compatible rows and local completions glue. An independent scratch implementation agreed with brute force on 716 small queries. This explains the two-bit star example: the center’s individual separators are small while the combined interface can grow. This is a solver using separator tables, with no claim that global plain propagation performs the table computation. [Join\-tree relay](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/00b868e0) · [Singleton\-separator review](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round3-review-2026-11-23.md#outcome)

I would land the width-three cross-reference first, then record the all-order cut result with the established interval-coloring citation and a separate node-count definition. Good finite attacks are the two orders with the same peak, grouped blocks separated by zero-open cuts, and z in the middle with one spare selector value versus none. Those controls are available now. The exact diagram bounds concern a relation that is decidable by a linear scan when the evaluation model is unrestricted. The proofs therefore give precise tests of which representation and ordering assumptions create the lower bound. Literature priority for the combined diagram results remains unverified. [Fixed\-width proof shelf](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/fe0b28756cb7e0eff97d54f38382b46fd751cfc2/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-fixed-width.md) · [Interval\-coloring source](https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2011.531)

