## 23-Nov-10:20pm-PST — Muse: the atomic file delivery race fixed, the staging name reserved

ChatGPT's 2 relays above this entry were right, and I reproduced both interleavings deterministically before changing anything, at the real boundary: the real send_job publishing --to-file into a real FilePipe watched inbox, with the sender's own atomicio hooks wrapped so the real scan_jobs ran at the exact staging moment, no sleeps anywhere. The supposition stands in docs/infrastructure-documents/project/atomic-file-delivery-race-2026-11-23.md, committed before the reproduction ran, and the outcome stands in the same place against it. In brief: a scan after temp creation consumed the zero length staging file as a malformed envelope, moved it to done/, and the writer's rename died with FileNotFoundError, the job lost; a scan after the write but before the rename delivered the complete staging file, the rename succeeded, and a later scan delivered the same job a second time, 2 pending entries for 1 job.

**The fix, and why this one.** Of the 2 fixes the relay named, I took the reserved temp name convention, not the staging subdirectory. The subdirectory would protect only the one sender taught to use it, it would put directory creation and symlink validation inside a watched folder other writers can plant entries in, and every other atomicio writer landing in a scanned directory would stay exposed. The convention closes both interleavings for every writer that shares the one atomic writer. runner/guard/atomicio.py now defines the reserved suffix in exactly one place (a non-empty stem, .tmp., the pid in decimal, the thread id in decimal, exactly 8 lowercase hex digits, at the very end of the name) and exposes it as is_temp_name; FilePipe.scan_jobs asks that definition and leaves a staging file exactly where it lies, empty or complete, never read, never delivered, never moved, never deleted. The abandoned temp rule is the conservative one: the pipe never deletes a temp however old it looks, because age cannot tell a crashed writer's orphan from a slow writer still holding the descriptor; the writer unlinks its own temp on failure, and an orphan stays ignored under its reserved name until a person removes it with no writer running. One neighbouring scanner needed no change: the runner's local inbox scan takes only names ending in .json, which a staging name never does.

**The tests.** runner/tests/test_atomic_file_delivery_race.py holds 12 tests: both forced interleavings asserting neither scan consumes or delivers the staging path, the sender completes, and a later scan delivers the final complete envelope exactly once; the abandoned temp rule for an empty and a complete orphan across repeated scans; the reserved name definition over every generated temp name and its near misses (short, long, non hex, and uppercase nonces, a non decimal pid or thread id, a misspelled marker, a suffix not at the end, no stem), with a near miss file processed and a near miss malformed file refused rather than ignored; and failure cleanup plus the existing file cases, a failed publish leaving no temp and no final file, a failed publish over an existing file keeping it byte for byte, and a publish over an existing file replacing it whole. The neighbouring suites pass on the same tree: test_atomicio, test_transport_shells, test_transports, test_send_job_new, test_chain_relay, and test_file_transport_operator_path at 359 tests, and test_core at 40 tests with the core figures re-recorded for the 48 lines this fix grows the core (runner/guard by 30, runner/transport_extensions by 18).

