Two small exact cases fell out of the support-pool argument after the main note. With one existential bit placed anywhere, the grouped visible order (all first endpoints, then z, then all second endpoints) has these exact ordinary OBDD nonterminal counts.

| Pairs | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
| Minimum nodes | 4 | 7 | 11 | 17 | 25 | 37 |

The first four follow from the earlier formulas. For five and six pairs, the lower proof uses a finite classification of minimum support pools and checks both alternatives for any candidate diagram. Let Q count nonempty-support continuation roots at the cut just after z, using z=0. The marked-support argument from the preceding note gives `N≥Q+B(m+1,2)`. I checked that the only weak 2-bases on five points with size at most 10 are the ten partitions into a pair and a triple, taking all nonempty subsets within each block. The only weak 2-bases on six points with size at most 14 are the ten unordered partitions into two triples. The separate seven-point search ruled out every weak 2-base of size at most 21. The partition into sizes three and four attains 22. The weak form allows the two sets in each union to overlap. [Bounded\-union background](https://doi.org/10.1017/S096354830800922X)

Consequently for five pairs, Q≥11 forces `N≥11+14=25`. For six pairs, Q≥15 forces `N≥15+22=37`. If Q is smaller, it must equal the corresponding minimum and the pool is one of the block partitions. With all blocks active there is exactly one reachable support per block, equal to that block’s actual active subset. For a block of size r, a path producing its exact support must test every one of its r first endpoints. At its j-th endpoint test, different prior local assignments require distinct nodes. Otherwise splicing one prefix and the other continuation produces the wrong support. This costs at least `2^r−1` prefix nodes, with another `2^r−1` in the suffix. At least one auxiliary fork is used on the full active row. On the product of nonempty local subsets, z witnesses must mark every nonempty support of at least one block. If each block had a missing mark, the row made of those missing subsets could not witness z. The empty row needs a different z node. Thus the canonical total is at least `2*sum_blocks(2^r−1)+1+2^min_r`, and the early-selector construction attains it. [Projection family](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/82605ec064ef89c2d171a50e28f2a9c7851d0746/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md)

The finite search is short enough to turn into a regression test. Include all singletons. Enumerate the possible families of two-element supports. Any triple with no chosen pair inside it must itself be included. For each larger target that is still uncovered, branch over every allowed single set or every two sets whose union is the target, dropping branches that exceed the size budget. Relabeling by nonincreasing pair-graph degree is a safe symmetry reduction for the seven-point decision check. My five- and six-point reruns returned all ten labeled partitions in each case, and the seven-point C++ search found no solution after 10,793 pair graphs and 163,065 completion nodes. The explicit 3+4 construction supplies the other half of that finite result. This gives a reproducible lower-bound target at the same sizes where exhaustive decision-diagram search is still conceivable. [Set\-family definitions](https://doi.org/10.1017/S096354830800922X)

