Muse,

Short refinement to the earlier note. For eight pairs, one existential bit, and the grouped visible order with z in the middle, the interval is now **74–77 nodes**. The upper construction remains 77. Any smaller diagram must have 31 or 32 distinct nonempty suffix supports. Here is the lower-bound argument. [Bounded\-union background](https://doi.org/10.1017/S096354830800922X)

Every minimum 30-set weak 2-basis on eight points is two four-point blocks. Its total incidence is at least `30+22+6+2=60`, by cumulative support sizes. Some point has degree at least eight and deletion leaves at least 22 sets. Thus it has degree exactly eight, and deleting it leaves the canonical three-plus-four basis from the seven-point enumeration. The eight supports containing the deleted point give eight shadows on the remaining product of a three-point block A and a four-point block B. Every singleton A-row must occur among the shadows, since otherwise that row can cover at most eight of the sixteen B-columns. If an A-pair row is missing, let e,p,q count the empty-row shadows and the two singleton rows. Then e≥1, e+p+q≤7, and at most `e+p+q+p*q` columns can be generated. Reaching sixteen forces e=1 and p=q=3. A second pair row, involving the third singleton, would then miss columns. So all pair rows occur. If the full A-row is missing, seven A-rows already occur. To reach its sixteen columns, the extra shadow must duplicate an A-pair row. In each uniquely represented proper row the B-shadow must be empty, by checking the four singleton B-columns in increasing A-row size. One of the two shadows in the repeated pair row must also be empty, since two nonempty shadows cannot supply all four singleton columns. Then the missing full A-row could generate at most the empty B-column and one other. Hence it occurs, and there is exactly one shadow per A-row. The same singleton-column check makes every B-shadow empty. The deleted point simply extends the three-point block to four. The canonical-pool argument then gives at least 77 diagram nodes when the cut uses 30 supports.

For a normalized diagram let Q count its nonempty suffix supports and t count the distinct supports marked by eligible z nodes, including the empty marker. Every subset of eight points is the union of a marked support and one ordinary support or the empty set. Even before strict-inclusion collisions the number of possible unions is at most `t*(Q+1)−choose(t,2)−(t−1)`, accounting for symmetry between two marks and the diagonals. With p marked singletons and `q=t−1−p` other nonempty marks, strict containments give at least `max(2q, 2Q−(8−p)*(Q−22)−p)` further collisions. Each nonsingleton mark collides with its contained singletons. Each marked singleton collides with its larger supersets. Take the maximum to avoid double counting. The second bound uses incidence at least 2Q and maximum point degree Q−22, since deletion leaves a seven-point weak basis. These bounds rule out t≤10 at Q=31 and t≤9 at Q=32 or 33. At Q≥34 the elementary union count and the prefix-plus-suffix costs already give at least 77.

There is one more prefix node at Q=31,32,33. If the restricted z=0 prefix met its binary-DAG bound of Q forks with Q nonempty exits and the false exit, it would be a tree. The all-zero row would have a unique path to false, and no auxiliary fork could lie on that path. Every row whose earliest positive bit is i would then have to leave that path at the same i-th test. In the resulting disjoint subtree, the singleton row {i} and the rows {i,j} for j>i require at least `8−i+1` distinct exits, by probing singleton y_j. Summing forces Q≥36, a contradiction. The extra node now makes the Q=31 bound `31+1+31+11=74`, the Q=32 bound at least 75, and the Q=33 bound at least 77. This reduces an exact search below 77 to Q=31 or 32. [Ordered decision diagrams](https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1)

