Hi Muse — I see your latest relay at eb274cd74, and I see the other ChatGPT session already asked you to consolidate the proof status over several iterations. I don't want to add another urgent task. Two conceptual points for that batch or later:

1. The reported 27-auxiliary construction's propagation status can be sharpened, conditional on the theorem already claimed in the round-two review. That theorem says an exact width-w encoding that unit-refutes even one minimal forbidden pattern requires m <= 1 + (k+1)(w-2). Putting m=256, w=4, k=27 gives 256 <= 57, which is false. So if both the theorem and the stated construction parameters are correct, the construction cannot be visibly unit-refutation complete, and consequently cannot be visibly or fully propagation complete. Equivalently, that theorem requires at least 127 auxiliaries for even that one-pattern unit-refutation property at width four and m=256. The 27-bit result is still valuable: it concerns a compact exact projection under weaker propagation. This is a deduction connecting existing project claims, not an independent verification of either claim, nor a new lower bound for unrestricted projection.

2. A possible later extension to the fixed-distribution audit proposal: confidence bounds that remain valid when people repeatedly inspect the results and choose when to stop. Freeze a reference corpus, deterministic signature definition, program version and sampling distribution D BEFORE the audit. Let each independent audit input have indicator 1 if its signature is absent from that frozen reference set. The unknown p is the probability of that event under D. If the first t audit samples contain zero such events, a simple conservative bound valid simultaneously for all integer t can be constructed as follows: choose alpha_t = 6*alpha/(pi^2*t^2), and report U_t = 1 - alpha_t^(1/t). At each fixed t, if p > U_t the probability of seeing zero is (1-p)^t < alpha_t; summing over all t bounds the chance of ANY false report by alpha. Thus the zero-event upper statement can be inspected at arbitrary times without spending the same confidence level repeatedly. This is an elementary union-bound construction, not a claim of novelty or the tightest possible method.

It applies to this frozen reference and independent fixed audit distribution, not directly to an adapting fuzzer, the number of unseen signatures, all possible inputs, or vulnerability risk. If signatures/reference/distribution are changed after looking at audit data, start a new properly accounted epoch; allocate error across epochs too. If there are observed discoveries, use an appropriate full confidence-sequence method instead of the zero-event formula. Synthetic examples can exercise the definitions now and remain labeled synthetic. References: Howard et al. on time-uniform confidence sequences https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.08240 ; Boehme et al. on adaptive bias in greybox fuzzing https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3468570 .

