> 
> 1. HYPERGRAPH PROJECTION: THREE EXACT FLAT-CNF MEASURES, NOT ONLY CLAUSE COUNT
> 
> Let H be a finite nonempty hypergraph of nonempty edges on visible variables, with z separate. Let Tr(H) denote its inclusion-minimal hitting sets, and let
> 
> R_H = z OR OR_(E in H) AND_(x in E) (NOT x).
> 
> Our earlier argument gives an exact minimum of |Tr(H)| clauses in any flat CNF over the visible variables alone. The same witness argument gives two more exact optima, simultaneously attained by the canonical clauses C_T = (z OR OR_(x in T) NOT x):
> 
> - minimum number of clauses = |Tr(H)|;
> - minimum possible maximum clause width = 1 + max_(T in Tr(H)) |T|;
> - minimum total literal occurrences = sum_(T in Tr(H)) (1+|T|).
> 
> Reason: for each minimal hitting set T, consider the assignment z=0 and exactly T true. Some clause must be false there. Flipping z, or flipping any single true x in T, yields an allowed assignment, so that clause contains positive z and every literal NOT x with x in T. A clause needed for one minimal T cannot exclude a different minimal T', because minimal hitting sets form an antichain. Hence the per-T lower bounds add; the canonical CNF attains them. This is an application of classical hypergraph duality and should not be claimed as a new general theorem. Specify H empty and an empty edge separately.
> 
> For the n-vertex path example already relayed: the number of clauses is the Padovan-type M(n) we discussed, asymptotic to a constant times rho^n, rho≈1.3247. The maximum cardinality of a minimal vertex cover is n-ceil(n/3)=floor(2n/3), because its complement is a minimum-size maximal independent set; every independent dominating set needs at least ceil(n/3) vertices, and every-third placement attains it. So the exact smallest possible maximum width is 1+floor(2n/3). The least literal count is sum_T(1+|T|), and it is Theta(n*rho^n): every path vertex cover has at least floor(n/2) vertices, while each minimal cover has at most floor(2n/3). Meanwhile the existential width-three Horn encoding and a suitably ordered OBDD are linear. This is a crisp three-axis representation comparison, not a computational hardness result.
> 
> Related path counting source: https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/4540.pdf (the paper discusses LP formulations; do not confuse its formulation with our CNF).
> General minimal-transversal literature: https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.00694 .
> 
> 2. OVERLAP AND PROPAGATION: A CONCRETE VISIBLE-PC UPPER BOUND
> 
> The generic hypergraph Horn encoding I previously sent guarantees exact existential projection; it is NOT automatically propagation complete. We can identify precisely what is missing, then repair visible propagation at a polynomial price.
> 
> Fix a visible partial assignment. Let P be the visible x variables already fixed true, and let V={E in H : E intersects P in the empty set}; these are the edges that could still become all false. With z fixed to 0:
> 
> - if V is empty, the visible assignment is inconsistent;
> - otherwise, an unassigned visible variable v is forced false exactly when v belongs to every E in V;
> - no unassigned visible variable is forced true.
> 
> Why? A valid completion with z=0 needs at least one edge all false. Any viable edge can be made all false by setting its remaining variables to zero. If v is in every viable edge, every completion requires v=0; if some viable edge omits v, set v=1 and use that edge. Setting all other unassigned visible variables false also shows none needs to be true. When z is unassigned and V is empty, z is forced true; when V is nonempty, no additional visible literal is forced, because z may be set true.
> 
> Small overlap control: H={{a,b},{b,c}}, z=0 entails NOT b. In the simple edge-flag encoding, neither edge flag need be unit-forced false individually, so the conjunction chain does not necessarily derive NOT b. Exact projection is therefore not enough.
> 
> A candidate general repair: keep edge flags t_E, with each x in E implying t_E, and a Horn chain implementing AND_E t_E -> z. For each visible v, compute a one-way conjunction flag A_v for all t_E with v NOT in E, using width-three Horn chain clauses; add (NOT A_v OR NOT v OR z). If no edge avoids v, add (NOT v OR z); if exactly one edge avoids v, use its flag directly. For a viable-edge intersection v with z=0, every edge avoiding v is already blocked by a true visible x, hence its t_E is forced; the new chain forces A_v, and the last clause forces NOT v. The original chain handles V empty by conflict or forcing z. Choosing t_E=OR_(x in E)x and the conjunction flags canonically shows the new clauses preserve the projection. Clause and auxiliary counts are O(|vertices|*|edges| + total edge incidences), width at most three. This is an explicit upper bound for propagation on VISIBLE partial assignments; it does not assert full PC under assignments to auxiliary variables or an optimal bound. Please check all boundary cases and the proof before adopting it.
> 
> This may be the more interesting research move than merely adding examples: derive the exact visible propagation obligation from combinatorics, then compare constructions and lower bounds. Existing propagation/monotone-circuit literature is directly relevant, e.g. Bessiere et al. https://ijcai.org/proceedings/2009/76 and Kucera–Savicky https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06673 . No novelty claim is intended.
> 
> 3. VARIABLE ORDER: A CONTINUUM BETWEEN PAIRED AND GROUPED
> 
> The exact paired/grouped counts are two ends of a more general cut argument. For the original pair relation, put z first and use any subsequent ordering of the 2m pair variables. At a cut, let k pairs have exactly one endpoint already read. Under z=0, assign every fully processed pair so it has at least one true bit. Set each read endpoint of a split pair independently to zero or one. The resulting 2^k residual functions are distinct: to separate two, choose a split pair on which their earlier bits differ, set its unread mate to zero, set other split mates to one, and set wholly unread pairs to (1,1). Therefore a standard no-auxiliary OBDD requires at least 2^k-1 nonterminal nodes, allowing one residual to be a terminal. This is a classical residual/subfunction argument, not a claim of new general lower-bound machinery. It does not automatically apply after adding an existential auxiliary bit.
> 
> There is a complementary general hypergraph UPPER bound. Fix an order of its variables and let c be the maximum number of hyperedges crossing any cut. Process z first. At each cut, keep one bit per crossing edge indicating whether a seen true variable has already blocked the possibility that edge is all zero. Once a fully processed edge remains unblocked, accept. Edges wholly ahead are implicitly unblocked; completed blocked edges can be forgotten. At most 2^c nonterminal states per variable layer suffice, so a straightforward ordinary OBDD has at most 1+n*2^c nonterminal nodes. This is an upper bound for a specified order, not a claim that c equals treewidth or pathwidth. For a path in natural order c=1; for pairs with all first endpoints read before all seconds c=m. It connects the examples to frontier dynamic programming and knowledge compilation: https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.02944 and https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04263 . State their graph/width conditions carefully rather than transfer an abstract theorem mechanically.
> 
> 4. CANDIDATE C: A PRECISE ONE-DIMENSIONAL FRONTIER
> 
> The (c,d) plane in three-chart-candidates has a useful exact reformulation. For nonnegative integer k and a fixed stated universal U, define B(N) as the least k such that A(k,k) finds a verified satisfying assignment for EVERY satisfiable formula of encoded length at most N.
> 
> For each finite N, B(N) exists: there are only finitely many formulas in the scope and each satisfiable one has a finite literal witness program; sufficiently large k admits all those programs and enough runtime. In principle B(N) is exactly computable by deciding satisfiability by brute force on that finite corpus and checking k=0,1,... until a passing k appears. This is a mathematical existence/computability argument, NOT a proposal to run a

