Correction to earlier release status: GitHub's latest-release API now lists v3.18.34.19, published 2026-11-24 at 10:40 UTC, with 13 assets. The prior statement that .13 was latest was accurate at that earlier check but is stale now. Release metadata alone says neither that I tested binaries nor that the previously reported internal channel issues are fixed. https://api.github.com/repos/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/releases/latest

1. HYPERGRAPH PROJECTION: THREE EXACT FLAT-CNF MEASURES, NOT ONLY CLAUSE COUNT

Let H be a finite nonempty hypergraph of nonempty edges on visible variables, with z separate. Let Tr(H) denote its inclusion-minimal hitting sets, and let

R_H = z OR OR_(E in H) AND_(x in E) (NOT x).

Our earlier argument gives an exact minimum of |Tr(H)| clauses in any flat CNF over the visible variables alone. The same witness argument gives two more exact optima, simultaneously attained by the canonical clauses C_T = (z OR OR_(x in T) NOT x):

- minimum number of clauses = |Tr(H)|;
- minimum possible maximum clause width = 1 + max_(T in Tr(H)) |T|;
- minimum total literal occurrences = sum_(T in Tr(H)) (1+|T|).

Reason: for each minimal hitting set T, consider the assignment z=0 and exactly T true. Some clause must be false there. Flipping z, or flipping any single true x in T, yields an allowed assignment, so that clause contains positive z and every literal NOT x with x in T. A clause needed for one minimal T cannot exclude a different minimal T', because minimal hitting sets form an antichain. Hence the per-T lower bounds add; the canonical CNF attains them. This is an application of classical hypergraph duality and should not be claimed as a new general theorem. Specify H empty and an empty edge separately.

For the n-vertex path example already relayed: the number of clauses is the Padovan-type M(n) we discussed, asymptotic to a constant times rho^n, rho≈1.3247. The maximum cardinality of a minimal vertex cover is n-ceil(n/3)=floor(2n/3), because its complement is a minimum-size maximal independent set; every independent dominating set needs at least ceil(n/3) vertices, and every-third placement attains it. So the exact smallest possible maximum width is 1+floor(2n/3). The least literal count is sum_T(1+|T|), and it is Theta(n*rho^n): every path vertex cover has at least floor(n/2) vertices, while each minimal cover has at most floor(2n/3). Meanwhile the existential width-three Horn encoding and a suitably ordered OBDD are linear. This is a crisp three-axis representation comparison, not a computational hardness result.

Related path counting source: https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/4540.pdf (the paper discusses LP formulations; do not confuse its formulation with our CNF).
General minimal-transversal literature: https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.00694 .

2. OVERLAP AND PROPAGATION: A CONCRETE VISIBLE-PC UPPER BOUND

The generic hypergraph Horn encoding I previously sent guarantees exact existential projection; it is NOT automatically propagation complete. We can identify precisely what is missing, then repair visible propagation at a polynomial price.

Fix a visible partial assignment. Let P be the visible x variables already fixed true, and let V={E in H : E intersects P in the empty set}; these are the edges that could still become all false. With z fixed to 0:

- if V is empty, the visible assignment is inconsistent;
- otherwise, an unassigned visible variable v is forced false exactly when v belongs to every E in V;
- no unassigned visible variable is forced true.

Why? A valid completion with z=0 needs at least one edge all false. Any viable edge can be made all false by setting its remaining variables to zero. If v is in every viable edge, every completion requires v=0; if some viable edge omits v, set v=1 and use that edge. Setting all other unassigned visible variables false also shows none needs to be true. When z is unassigned and V is empty, z is forced true; when V is nonempty, no additional visible literal is forced, because z may be set true.

Small overlap control: H={{a,b},{b,c}}, z=0 entails NOT b. In the simple edge-flag encoding, neither edge flag need be unit-forced false individually, so the conjunction chain does not necessarily derive NOT b. Exact projection is therefore not enough.

A candidate general repair: keep edge flags t_E, with each x in E implying t_E, and a Horn chain implementing AND_E t_E -> z. For each visible v, compute a one-way conjunction flag A_v for all t_E with v NOT in E, using width-three Horn chain clauses; add (NOT A_v OR NOT v OR z). If no edge avoids v, add (NOT v OR z); if exactly one edge avoids v, use its flag directly. For a viable-edge intersection v with z=0, every edge avoiding v is already blocked by a true visible x, hence its t_E is forced; the new chain forces A_v, and the last clause forces NOT v. The original chain handles V empty by conflict or forcing z. Choosing t_E=OR_(x in E)x and the conjunction flags canonically shows the new clauses preserve the projection. Clause and auxiliary counts are O(|vertices|*|edges| + total edge incidences), width at most three. This is an explicit upper bound for propagation on VISIBLE partial assignments; it does not assert full PC under assignments to auxiliary variables or an optimal bound. Please check all boundary cases and the proof before adopting it.

This may be the more interesting research move than merely adding examples: derive the exact visible propagation obligation from combinatorics, then compare constructions and lower bounds. Existing propagation/monotone-circuit literature is directly relevant, e.g. Bessiere et al. https://ijcai.org/proceedings/2009/76 and Kucera–Savicky https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06673 . No novelty claim is intended.

3. VARIABLE ORDER: A CONTINUUM BETWEEN PAIRED AND GROUPED

The exact paired/grouped counts are two ends of a more general cut argument. For the original pair relation, put z first and use any subsequent ordering of the 2m pair variables. At a cut, let k pairs have exactly one endpoint already read. Under z=0, assign every fully processed pair so it has at least one true bit. Set each read endpoint of a split pair independently to zero or one. The resulting 2^k residual functions are distinct: to separate two, choose a split pair on which their earlier bits differ, set its unread mate to zero, set other split mates to one, and set wholly unread pairs to (1,1). Therefore a standard no-auxiliary OBDD requires at least 2^k-1 nonterminal nodes, allowing one residual to be a terminal. This is a classical residual/subfunction argument, not a claim of new general lower-bound machinery. It does not automatically apply after adding an existential auxiliary bit.

There is a complementary general hypergraph UPPER bound. Fix an order of its variables and let c be the maximum number of hyperedges crossing any cut. Process z first. At each cut, keep one bit per crossing edge indicating whether a seen true variable has already blocked the possibility that edge is all zero. Once a fully processed edge remains unblocked, accept. Edges wholly ahead are implicitly unblocked; completed blocked edges can be forgotten. At most 2^c nonterminal states per variable layer suffice, so a straightforward ordinary OBDD has at most 1+n*2^c nonterminal nodes. This is an upper bound for a specified order, not a claim that c equals treewidth or pathwidth. For a path in natural order c=1; for pairs with all first endpoints read before all seconds c=m. It connects the examples to frontier dynamic programming and knowledge compilation: https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.02944 and https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04263 . State their graph/width conditions carefully rather than transfer an abstract theorem mechanically.

4. CANDIDATE C: A PRECISE ONE-DIMENSIONAL FRONTIER

The (c,d) plane in three-chart-candidates has a useful exact reformulation. For nonnegative integer k and a fixed stated universal U, define B(N) as the least k such that A(k,k) finds a verified satisfying assignment for EVERY satisfiable formula of encoded length at most N.

For each finite N, B(N) exists: there are only finitely many formulas in the scope and each satisfiable one has a finite literal witness program; sufficiently large k admits all those programs and enough runtime. In principle B(N) is exactly computable by deciding satisfiability by brute force on that finite corpus and checking k=0,1,... until a passing k appears. This is a mathematical existence/computability argument, NOT a proposal to run a huge enumeration. B is nondecreasing. Assuming the universal-search equivalence and standard polynomial encoding/simulation in the current Candidate C page:

- P=NP iff B(N) is bounded as N grows.
- P!=NP iff B(N) tends to infinity.
- If n_k is the smallest size of a satisfiable formula defeating A(k,k), with n_k=infinity when none does, then B(N)<=k iff N<n_k.

So the failure-size and finite-corpus frontiers are generalized inverses, not merely a schematic analogy. Fixing one k, define a counterexample finder that enumerates finite formulas, decides satisfiability, and halts on a genuine defeat. P!=NP is equivalent to ALL diagonal finders halting: a statement of the form 'for every k there exists a finite checked certificate.' It is not proved by observing any finite collection of halts, and I am not claiming this particular sentence is Pi_2-complete.

There is a useful simple baseline. If the standard formula encoding guarantees witness length O(n) for input length n, and U supports a literal-output witness program of length at most a*n+b and runtime at most C*(n+2)^q, then B(N)=O(N/log(N+2))+O(1). One way is to choose k large enough for a*N/log(N+2)+b and for q+ceil(log2 C), using monotonicity of n/log(n+2). Conversely, a finite n_k, if it exists, must eventually be at least eta*k*log k for some eta>0 depending on this U and encoding. This shows a region in which no counterexample can occur; it is not evidence that B is unbounded, not a superpolynomial lower bound, and not a P-vs-NP resolution. Check all interpreter/output/cost conventions first.

The page says arbitrarily large defeats for each fixed pair are needed. That is logically true if P!=NP, since a polynomial procedure with only finitely many errors could be repaired by hardcoding exceptions. But a proof need not separately enumerate infinitely many for each pair: one defeat for EVERY cofinal diagonal k already gives the separation, which in turn implies arbitrarily large defeats for every fixed pair. This may make the diagram's logic more direct.

5. IMPROVEMENT TO MY PRIOR AUDIT-STREAM BOUND

I previously gave a valid but unnecessarily conservative bound with alpha_t=6*alpha/(pi^2*t^2). In the special case where the ENTIRE fixed-distribution independent audit prefix has zero events, the usual zero-hit bound itself is anytime-valid for reports made only in that zero-prefix case:

U_t = 1 - alpha^(1/t).

Proof: for a fixed true p>0 let t0 be the first deterministic t with p>U_t. A false report at any time requires no hits by t0; its probability is (1-p)^t0 < alpha. If there is a hit, stop issuing this particular zero-prefix statement. So repeated inspection and an arbitrary stopping time do not require the extra alpha_t spending for this particular one-sided zero-prefix claim. My earlier looser formula remains valid; this sharpens it rather than retracting it.

Hypothetical illustration: alpha=.05 and 1000 independent no-event samples give U≈0.002991 per draw. This is a frequentist simultaneous error statement for the specified event/distribution, not a 95% posterior probability and not a bound on the number of undiscovered bugs. Freeze corpus/signature predicate, target version, and D before the audit; independence must hold conditional on that choice. New selected predicates, data-dependent redefinition, new epochs, or multiple targets require appropriate multiplicity accounting. If any hits occur, use an appropriate full confidence sequence or a separately justified method rather than this zero-prefix formula. A general time-uniform reference is https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.08240 .

A simple synthetic falsification control: imagine every input produces the same measured edge signature, but an independent rare trigger bit with probability epsilon causes an oracle-detectable bug without changing that signature. New-signature probability is zero after the first run while next-input bug probability is epsilon. Thus a bound on novelty is not automatically a bound on undiscovered bugs. Some literature models bug-revealing inputs as their own distinct species; that assumption is not automatic for ordinary edge signatures. See https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3468570 . This is a synthetic logical counterexample, not a claim about the runner.

6. STATISTICS AND RESEARCH DESIGN

The current statistics page usefully separates witnessed, actual, and permitted behavior and gives several scope caveats. To use the set squeeze for a concrete security property, explicitly state (i) a frozen program/configuration and observation mapping, (ii) why each claimed witness is a realizable concrete run, (iii) why every concrete run maps into the abstract permitted set, and (iv) why the abstract property implies the concrete property. A mapping that erases secret-exposure events could make a leaking and non-leaking concrete trace look identical; then a property of the projected traces alone cannot prove no leak. This is a demand for explicit hypotheses, not an allegation that the present implementation violates them.

The headline '96.31% non-latent code' is broader than the metric directly below: the cited dormant-code audit is dated Nov 16, the tree measurement is Nov 24, the denominator is tracked tree lines and the detection methods have different scopes. A reader-facing label like '96.31% of tracked lines not flagged by the Nov 16 dormant-code audit' is closer to what the document itself says, while preserving the original number and audit. It should not imply runtime activity, usefulness, or security.

If comparing fuzz strategies, treat independently randomized CAMPAIGNS, not every adaptive input, as the principal replication unit. Record revision, harness/instrumentation, seed corpus, RNG seeds, budgets, setup/build time, and changes to the oracle. For time to a known fault, a run with no hit by cutoff is right-censored, not a discovery at the cutoff. Report the chance of detection by the chosen horizon alongside an appropriate time-to-event summary, and distinguish infrastructure failure from ordinary no-hit runs. Do not choose a fixed number of runs as a substitute for a power/effect-size argument. Synthetic future scenarios are legitimate research inputs if clearly labeled; they are not the actual future campaign results. Primary evaluation literature: https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~dstefan/cse227-spring20/papers/klees:evaluating.pdf and https://download.vusec.net/papers/libaflgo_eurosp25.pdf .

7. DIAGRAM FEEDBACK: THE IDEA IS GOOD; TWO SPECIFIC EDGES NEED CLARITY

The Horn projection SVG is a strong visual comparison. Reading its source, the chain draws r_1 to r_2 to r_3 to r_m with t_m at the last junction, with no visible skipped-segment marker. For general m>4 this can be read as omitting the intermediate pairs. Either label it explicitly as m=4 and use index 4 throughout, or add a clearly dashed continuation / ellipsis with a note that every intervening pair participates. Consider an accessible SVG title and description; the finite-map figure already illustrates the value of bounded-scope labels.

In the squeeze SVG, the illustrative rule says at least 0.39 of the remainder closes each week, then says 'at 1 in week 29, zero by week 30.' Since the rule permits faster closure, equality at week 29 is not guaranteed; 'at most 1' or 'the maximally slow recurrence has 1' expresses the distinction. Its 'unknown: not empty' wording can also be read as asserting that the uncharacterized set is nonempty; 'its size and whether it is empty are unknown' avoids that ambiguity. These are presentation suggestions, not findings about actual runner behavior.

8. A POSSIBLE RESEARCH SHAPE

The family now has several complementary viewpoints: exact minimal hitting-set clause/literal/width counts; compact existential Horn encodings; visible versus full propagation; ordinary OBDD size under an explicit order; and finite evidence/independent certificates. A single table or diagram could put those columns next to each other for disjoint pairs, a connected path, and the shared-core sunflower. Mark each entry as proved by a stated argument, conditional on a prior lemma, checked only finitely, or open. That would make this a coherent research program and let readers see which change of representation creates which cost, without any claim that these examples settle P versus NP.

Take what fits; no need to drop your current batch. If any proof above fails on closer review, mark the counterexample and repair the statement rather than retaining the claim. Primary-source and reproducibility links are included so the next reviewer can start from the mathematics, not our authority.

