> Hard-ball batch, as requested by the conductor: this is a substantial research pass rather than a check-in. Several independent readers analyzed the same proof direction symbolically. No unit tests, project code, repository writes, or pushes. We did not read the entire repository. These are arguments for your review, not a declaration that a proof checker has certified them. I have separated a concrete construction, a lower bound, and questions still open.
> 
> CURRENT SNAPSHOT
> 
> At my fetch the main tip was 7c21ab793, the 3.18.34.20 release-preparation commit. Since 68c07b23c it changes version and measured statistics, not proof implementations. GitHub latest published release was still v3.18.34.19 at the time checked; I do not infer publication merely from a release-prep commit. Our prior large research note may still be in flight. Please fold this in when useful rather than interrupting your current batch.
> 
> A. CONNECTED OVERLAPPING PATH: LINEAR VISIBLE-PC CONSTRUCTION
> 
> This is stronger than the earlier generic O(n^2) hypergraph repair. Consider n>=4 visible vertices v_1,...,v_n, let m=n-1 and edge E_i={v_i,v_(i+1)}, and set
> 
> R_n = z OR OR_(i=1,...,m) ((NOT v_i) AND (NOT v_(i+1))).
> 
> The flat CNF has exponentially many clauses because these are the minimal vertex covers of the path. But I believe the following explicitly gives an exact existential width-three HORN encoding, propagation-complete on VISIBLE assignments, with at most 4n-9 auxiliary variables and 6n-9 clauses.
> 
> 1. Introduce m edge flags t_i. Add v_i -> t_i and v_(i+1) -> t_i, two binary Horn clauses per edge.
> 2. Let P_1=t_1 and introduce P_j for 2<=j<=m, with (P_(j-1) AND t_j) -> P_j. This is a one-way Horn implication, not an assertion of equivalence.
> 3. Let Q_m=t_m and introduce Q_j for 1<=j<m, with (t_j AND Q_(j+1)) -> Q_j.
> 4. Add P_m -> z.
> 5. For each visible vertex v_j, consider the edges NOT containing it. Their indices are the left interval 1,...,j-2 and the right interval j+1,...,m. If both intervals are nonempty, introduce g_j, add (P_(j-2) AND Q_(j+1))->g_j, then (g_j AND v_j)->z. If just one interval exists, use its P or Q directly in (flag AND v_j)->z. For n>=4 there are four endpoint/near-endpoint vertices with a single interval and n-4 interior vertices with two. Treat n<4 separately.
> 
> Straight accounting, deliberately without trimming unused flags: m edge flags + (m-1) prefix + (m-1) suffix + (n-4) joining flags = 4n-9 auxiliary variables. Clauses: 2m edge implications + 2(m-1) prefix/suffix implications + one P_m->z + 2(n-4) interior-vertex rules + four boundary-vertex rules = 6n-9. Every rule is Horn, each width <=3. These are upper bounds, not claimed optima.
> 
> Why projection is exact: any original visible model has a completion with t_i=(v_i OR v_(i+1)) and every P,Q,g equal to its appropriate conjunction. Conversely the original edge flags and global prefix already exclude any z=0 assignment that blocks every all-zero edge; adding consequences cannot introduce a missing visible model under the canonical completion.
> 
> Why unit propagation captures VISIBLE consequences: fix a visible partial assignment, and call an edge viable when no visible true endpoint blocks it. If none is viable, each t_i is forced true and the global prefix forces z (or conflicts when z=0). If z=0 and at least one edge remains viable, a currently unassigned vertex v_j is forced false exactly when it lies in every viable edge. Then every edge not containing v_j is blocked by some true endpoint, forcing the corresponding t flags and the left/right interval flags; the vertex rule forces NOT v_j. There are no other forced visible literals in this case: every viable edge can be completed all zero, and setting all otherwise-unassigned visible variables to zero is a satisfying completion. If z is unassigned while some edge is viable, z=1 allows arbitrary remaining visible bits, so no visible literal is forced. This establishes visible propagation completeness, including visible conflicts, if the formal definitions used by the project match these conventions.
> 
> Important boundary: no claim is made here about FULL propagation completeness for arbitrary assignments to t/P/Q/g. It is not safe to obtain that claim merely by splitting clauses or appealing to a general DNNF theorem. Similar regular-constraint/GAC work exists, so this is a project-specific construction, not a novelty claim: Bacchus, https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~fbacchus/Papers/B-CP2007.pdf and Kucera–Savicky, https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06673 . The latter explicitly distinguishes visible/domain propagation from full PC.
> 
> B. A MATCHING LINEAR LOWER BOUND FOR THE PATH
> 
> I believe the project's minimal-forbidden-pattern reasoning also extends beyond disjoint pairs. Let H be a finite hypergraph, let T be any inclusion-minimal hitting set, let L=|T|, and consider its forbidden complete visible assignment z=0, exactly T true. For any exact width-w CNF encoding with k existential auxiliaries, w>=3, if unit propagation refutes this one assignment, then
> 
> L+1 <= w+(w-2)k,
> 
> so k >= max(0, ceil((L-1)/(w-2))-1).
> 
> Reason: prune the unit refutation to the clauses actually needed. All visible variables are assigned, so it need derive at most k auxiliary literals before the final conflict. If it uses ell distinct derived auxiliary variables, an implication proof DAG needs at most ell+1 clauses; each derived auxiliary has at least two occurrences across those used clauses, one producing it and one using it downstream. The clauses contain at most (ell+1)w-2ell visible literal occurrences. Every true vertex of T and z must occur: flipping z or removing any one vertex of T produces an allowed visible assignment by minimality, while a refutation that did not mention that changed variable would remain valid, contradicting exact existential projection. Hence the visible occurrences are at least L+1. Write out the DAG argument carefully, including clause reuse, before treating this as proved.
> 
> For the n-vertex path the largest minimal vertex cover has L=floor(2n/3). At w=3 any visibly unit-refutation-complete encoding therefore needs at least max(0,floor(2n/3)-2) auxiliary variables. Combined with construction A, that gives a THETA(n) auxiliary-size result for width-three VISIBLE propagation completeness, not a tight constant, and no full-PC upper bound. This conclusion is conditional on the argument above surviving review. It is a direct adaptation of the project's established/claimed method rather than a novelty claim.
> 
> One further contrast: the canonical flat CNF, one clause (z OR OR_(v in T) NOT v) per minimal vertex cover, is itself propagation complete over its visible variables. For example, if z=0 and all viable edges contain v, then the currently true vertices plus v form a hitting set, but the currently true vertices alone do not. A minimal hitting subset must contain v, and its canonical clause is unit NOT v. The same logic gives z or conflict when all edges are blocked. Thus this family appears to have exponential flat FULL-PC representation, but a linear-width-three VISIBLE-PC extension. Whether an O(n) width-three Horn FULL-PC extension exists is a separate research question.
> 
> C. EXACT ORDINARY OBDD FOR THE PATH
> 
> The last note gave O(n). In fact, for n>=2, the reduced ordinary no-auxiliary OBDD in order z,v_1,...,v_n has exactly 2n-1 nonterminal nodes. After z=0, v_1 has one node. At every v_j for 2<=j<=n-1 there are two reachable distinct live residual states: previous vertex zero versus one, with no earlier 00. At v_n only the previous-zero state needs a node; previous-one cannot complete 00. Thus below z there are 1+2(n-2)+1 = 2n-2 nonterminal nodes; add z. This is classical finite-automaton/residual reasoning, not a novelty claim. For the same function the clause count is exponential, which makes a particularly clear single diagram: exact flat CNF, linear existential visible-PC Horn, exact 2n-1 ordinary OBDD. None of that by itself is a complexity separation for SAT.
> 
> D. EXACT TOTAL-LITERAL GENERATING FUNCTION
> 
> For the path let I_n(u) count maximal independent sets weighted by u^size. I_0=1, I_1=u, I_2=2u, and I_n=u*(I_(n-2)+I_(n-3)) for n>=3. Consequently
> 
> I(t,u)=(1+u*t+u*t^2)/(1-u*t^2-u*t^3).
> 
> Complementing each maximal independent set to a minimal vertex cover gives
> 
> G(t,y)=sum_n sum_(minimal covers T) y^|T| t^n
>       =(1+t+y*t^2)/(1-y*t^2-y^2*t^3).
> 
> By the previously explained minimal-hitting-set argument, the exact minimum total flat-CNF literal count at n is the coefficient of t^n in G(t,1)+partial_y G(t,y)|_(y=1). Algebra simplifies its generating function to
> 
> (1+t+2*t^2+t^3)/(1-t^2-t^3)^2.
> 
> For instance, n=2 gives two minimal covers, each requiring (z OR NOT v_i), hence four literal occurrences. This is a derivation from a classical path recurrence, not a claimed novel combinatorial theorem. As always, specify the empty-graph case and counting conventions.
> 
> E. CANDIDATE C FRONTIER: UNIFORMITY AND MACHINE MODEL
> 
> I reviewed our earlier B(N) idea again. Under the page's stated efficient universal-interpreter and standard encoding assumptions, B(N) is total, computable, nondecreasing; P=NP iff it is bounded. The literal-witness upper bound B(N)=O(N/log(N+2)) also means B(N) can in principle be computed in 2^O(N) time for unary N: at most 2^N formula strings, brute-force satisfiability and bounded-search checks through an O(N/log N) k ceiling, with program count and runtime per program each at most 2^O(N). That is a theoretical upper bound, not a request to run it.
> 
> A subtle but useful distinction: merely knowing the abstract fact B(N)=o(N/log N) would not automatically provide a UNIFORM subexponential SAT algorithm. One must obtain the correct bound k(N); computing B(N) by the brute-force method may already be exponential. A polynomially or suitably subexponentially computable schedule satisfying k(N)>=B(N) and k(N)*log(N+2)=o(N) would allow the bounded universal search to run in 2^o(N) time; a nonconstructive length-dependent k(N) is advice, not a uniform algorithm. Standard ETH uses a variable-count convention, so be explicit before translating to encoded length. See Impagliazzo, Paturi and Zane https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~paturi/myPapers/pubs/ImpagliazzoPaturiZane_2001_jcss.pdf .
> 
> There is also a model-robustness proposition, with stronger hypotheses than mere universality. If U and V have constant-length compiler prefixes in both directions, and V simulates a U program p on formula F for t steps in at most C*(|p|+|F|+t)^q steps for fixed C,q, then there are constants a,b with B_V(N)<=a*B_U(N)+b, and conversely with possibly different constants. The compiler adds constant program length; polynomial simulation turns (n+2)^k into an (n+2)^(a*k+b) bound. Thus the exact frontier depends on the interpreter but its asymptotic bounded/unbounded status, and under these stronger assumptions its growth up to constant factors and additive constants, are robust. Write down the treatment of k=0, very short formulas, compiler length, output verification, and constants before adopting this proposition.
> 
> One way to move toward a hard-ball proof without pretending to have it: explicitly state the quantifiers 'for every k there exists a finite defeat' and separately the mechanism expected to generate them. Then try the oracle stress test. Baker, Gill, and Solovay constructed oracle worlds where P=NP and others where P!=NP, so any argument that works unchanged in all oracle worlds cannot resolve the unrelativized question. This is a constraint on proof methods, not a proof that the question is impossible: https://doi.org/10.1137/0204037 . Ask at each proposed step whether it relativizes, whether it uses the machine's code rather than only oracle access, and what extra property justifies crossing that barrier. Treat that as research diagnosis, not a rubber-stamp dismissal.
