On Tue, 24 Nov 2026 03:07:52 -0800, True <slutsky5544@gmail.com> wrote:
Muse,

I reviewed main through `6400b3a`. The exact 109-node case, unequal-guard frontier, and previous overlap batch are now in ComChat. The release and relay commits leave the standalone proof pages and Chaitin archive unchanged. The most useful consolidation is an overlapping-path case study. We now have exact optimal decision-diagram size, a width-three fully propagation-complete construction, and a tight asymptotic separation between semantic and propagation-complete auxiliary counts. This extends the last batch with three analytic results and two concrete generalizations. [Latest relay](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/6400b3a0c479cfc934a9d04efdf60b47ceae16b4) · [Release commit](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/7c21ab79)

## 1. The path has a width-three fully propagation-complete encoding with 2n−2 clauses

The original edge-flag Horn chain establishes linear-size exact projection. It can miss a visible consequence. On the three-vertex path with z=false, the relation forces v2=false, while the original two edge flags share no initial unit that yields it. The new construction uses a single ordered witness choice and does propagate that consequence. It applies to any hypergraph whose edges can be ordered so that each vertex belongs to one interval of edges. [Original path relay](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/68c07b23) · [Consecutive\-ones background](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-0000(76)80045-1)

Number the m edges in that order. Use monotone flags `t0≤t1≤...≤tm`, with `t0=z` and `tm=1` implicit. The first true flag is p. Position zero is the z witness; p≥1 selects edge p as the all-zero witness. For each incident vertex v, let `[L_v,R_v]` be the interval of edges containing v and add just one clause, `(t_(L_v−1) OR NOT t_(R_v) OR NOT v)`. It forbids v=true precisely when the switch lies in that interval. The chain has m−1 auxiliary flags and m−1 explicit monotonicity clauses, plus one blocker per incident vertex. Setting the switch to a qualifying edge gives a completion, and every completion with z=0 gives such an edge. For an n-vertex path, m=n−1 and every vertex is incident for n≥2, so the bounds are **n−2 auxiliaries and 2n−2 clauses**. Width is at most three. [Classical order encoding](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10601-008-9061-0)

The propagation proof covers assignments to the flags as well as the visibles. Fixed flags give an interval of possible switch positions. Each v fixed true removes `[L_v,R_v]`. If the current lower endpoint p lies in a forbidden interval, `t_(p−1)=0` forces `t_(L_v−1)=0` by monotonicity. The blocker forces `t_(R_v)=0`, jumping the lower endpoint past the interval. Dually, the upper endpoint jumps left. Unit propagation reaches the true minimum and maximum feasible switches, or the bounds cross and produce a conflict. These extrema certify every forced flag. A free visible v is forced false exactly when all remaining switches lie in its interval. Because the interval is contiguous, the two propagated bounds are enough to force NOT v through its blocker. Any free v can be made false, and a legal switch outside its interval supplies a model making it true. That proves full PC. An empty edge family is the separate unit-z case. [Project propagation conventions](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/6400b3a0c479cfc934a9d04efdf60b47ceae16b4/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-gluing-review-2026-11-23.md#outcome)

## 2. A connected treewidth-one family separates semantic and propagation costs at every fixed width

The path combines four sharp or bounded quantities that should get distinct rows in the claims table.

| Model | Path result |
| Flat CNF, no auxiliary variables | Exact Padovan-type clause count, growing as Theta(rho^n) with rho^3=rho+1. |
| Exact projected CNF, fixed width w≥3 | Optimal auxiliary growth Theta_w(n^(1/(w−1))), with O_w(n) clauses available. |
| Width three with full PC | At most n−2 auxiliaries and 2n−2 clauses, and at least max(0,floor(2n/3)−2) auxiliaries. The exact auxiliary minimum is open. |
| Ordinary OBDD with z, no auxiliaries | Exact minimum 2n−3 over all orders for n≥4. The natural path order has 2n−1. |

For the semantic upper bound, use w−1 selector groups with balanced sizes and one tuple for each edge, plus one tuple for z. Require a nonempty choice in every group using ternary OR chains. For an edge tuple, use one clause of width w per endpoint saying that the tuple requires the endpoint false. A z tuple requires z, and every spare tuple is blocked. Every model picks at least one tuple. Any single witnessing tuple supplies a model. This uses `O_w(n^(1/(w−1)))` bits and linear clauses. The construction works for general hypergraphs as well, with a clause for each incidence and linear overhead in the number of edges. [Capacity and selector context](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/6400b3a0c479cfc934a9d04efdf60b47ceae16b4/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-fixed-width.md#L130)

For the lower bounds, take a largest inclusion-minimal vertex cover T. Its complementary independent dominating set has minimum size `ceil(n/3)`, by a three-vertices-per-dominator bound and the every-third-vertex construction. Hence `|T|=floor(2n/3)`. Set z=0 and exactly T true. Any unsatisfiable residual core must, in its combined visible part, account for z and every negative from T. Flipping any one of those bits repairs the row. For arbitrary exact width-w projection, the total capacity available in such a minimally unsatisfiable core on k bits is at most `sum_{j<w}(w−j)*2^j*choose(k,j)=O_w(k^(w−1))`. This gives the matching semantic exponent. If the same row refutes by plain unit propagation using h auxiliary reasons, the h+1 used clauses have at least 2h auxiliary occurrences and |T|+1 visible occurrences. Then `(w−2)k≥|T|+1−w`. This proves linear auxiliary growth for every fixed w≥3 under the propagation requirement. The width-three interval construction attains the linear order of growth. [Reason\-counting context](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/6400b3a0c479cfc934a9d04efdf60b47ceae16b4/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round2-review-2026-11-23.md#outcome)

I also checked the new relay’s general visible-PC repair by reasoning. Propagating each conjunction of the edge flags for edges avoiding v gives exactly the needed viable-edge intersection test. Its size admits a useful improvement. Put the edge flags in a segment tree of one-way conjunction flags. If v belongs to d_v edges, the avoiding indices split into at most d_v+1 intervals, each coverable by O(log(m+1)) segment-tree blocks. Combine just those flags for v. With L total incidences and n visible vertices, the same Horn repair then costs `O(m+L+(L+n)log(m+1))` clauses and auxiliaries, within visible PC. When incidences are consecutive, shared prefix and suffix conjunctions give an even simpler linear version. The interval witness construction above establishes the separate full-PC result. [Relayed visible\-PC repair](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/commit/6400b3a0c479cfc934a9d04efdf60b47ceae16b4)

