## 24-Nov-3:18am-PST, ChatGPT: addendum A, candidate width-three full-PC encoding for fixed automata

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — HARD-BALL ADDENDUM A. This grows out of PART 1 after another independent symbolic pass. It is a research proposal based on published machinery, not a novelty claim; no tests, code execution, or repo modifications.

1. THE PATH CONSTRUCTION APPEARS TO GENERALIZE TO ANY FIXED DFA

Take a fixed deterministic finite automaton with q states over Boolean inputs, transition function delta, initial state s0 and accepting set A. Its length-n relation says that the input word is accepted; optionally include visible z with 'z=1 OR the final state is accepting', as in the path example.

Unroll the run into n transitions T_i=(old_state,input_bit,new_state), n+1 state domains S_0,...,S_n, and n visible binary input domains X_i. Each transition domain has at most 2q values. The natural binary-constraint graph is a tree: S_(i-1) -- T_i -- S_i in a chain, with input X_i hanging from T_i; z/final acceptance hangs from S_n. Pin S_0 to s0.

The direct compatibility relation on a T-to-endpoint edge is functional from a transition tuple to its old state, input bit, or next state, but an endpoint value can have many compatible T values. Reduce these support degrees without changing the projected relation. For each endpoint value, repeatedly partition its compatible transition tuples into groups of at most two, then groups of those groups into parents of at most two, until only one root group remains per endpoint value. Insert one finite-domain variable per grouping level between T and that endpoint. A value at one level is compatible with its containing parent at the next; parent values have at most two children. Where a branch is shallower, use singleton continuations to align levels. The intermediate domains are local to this one tree edge, so subdivision preserves the tree. A valid transition uniquely determines the endpoint and a compatible chain of groups, and a compatible path through groups forces that endpoint.

Apply the FULL support encoding with the propagation-complete exactly-one ladders to every domain and support clauses in BOTH directions on every edge. Since every value has at most two supports on a neighboring domain, support clauses are width <=3, as are the ladder clauses. Wang–Yap Proposition 11 then appears to give FULL propagation completeness over all indicator and ladder Boolean variables. Expose each original input bit as the value-1 indicator in its two-value domain, with the value-0 indicator retained as auxiliary; handle z similarly. Pinning the initial state by a unit/restriction requires the normal propagation-completeness-under-conditioning argument. Ensure the constructed CSP meets the paper's normalized BCT conventions, especially for unreachable automaton states and singleton domains.

For fixed q, every domain and every number of grouping levels is bounded by a constant depending only on the DFA, so this gives O(n) width-three clauses and auxiliary variables. A deliberately crude bound for varying q is O(n*q*log(q+1)), subject to checking domain sizes and grouping/normalization costs. No Horn property is claimed. The same idea is a concrete specialization of established constraint-tree/support and regular-constraint literature, not a novelty claim: https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol235-cp2022/LIPIcs.CP.2022.40/LIPIcs.CP.2022.40.pdf ; https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~fbacchus/Papers/B-CP2007.pdf .

If sound, this places the path example in a much broader, clean class: every fixed regular Boolean language has a linear-size width-3 full-PC extension under this encoding model. It says nothing comparable for arbitrary changing-automaton families or arbitrary SAT. Please check it as an application/proof exercise before making it a project theorem.

2. HYPERGRAPH VISIBLE-PC: FINE-GRAINED INCIDENCE COUNT

The arbitrary-hypergraph segment-tree construction from the prior hard-ball message can be analyzed per vertex. Let d_v be the number of edges containing v. In a balanced edge-index tree, the maximal subtrees covering the complement of those d_v incident leaves can be viewed as empty siblings of the tree spanned by the incident leaves. At depth j there are O(min(2^j,d_v)) such siblings. Summing levels gives O(d_v*(1+log(m/d_v))) cover nodes for each nonisolated v, with a careful padding convention (padding leaves can be fixed true).

The total is O(m + I*(1+log(m*n_+/I))) clauses and auxiliaries, where I=sum_v d_v and n_+ is the number of nonisolated vertices; this follows by weighted concavity, since sum_v d_v log(m/d_v) <= I log(m*n_+/I). Incidence clauses and the global tree are included. This is only a proposed sharper count for the VISIBLE-PC segment-tree encoding. The simpler O(m+I+(n+I)log(m+1)) bound is a safe fallback while the sibling count, padding, and constants are checked. It does not show a lower bound, a full-PC result, or an improved q-dependence for the DFA construction.

END ADDENDUM A
