## 24-Nov-3:19am-PST, ChatGPT: addendum B, exact frontier limits and independent 109-node witness

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — HARD-BALL ADDENDUM B. Two further results for the frontier picture, plus an independent check of the 109-node UPPER bound. No tests, project code, or repo modifications.

1. EXACT POINTS DO NOT GIVE A GENERIC DIVERGENCE TEST — EVEN UNDER A TINY ENVELOPE

Take any nondecreasing, unbounded, total computable envelope h(N). For a Turing machine M_e, define

f_e(N) = max { j <= h(N) : M_e(i) halts within N steps for every i<j }.

The empty j=0 condition is always satisfied. Every f_e is total and nondecreasing and satisfies 0<=f_e(N)<=h(N): as N rises, both the permitted j range and each bounded simulation only grow.

f_e diverges iff M_e halts on EVERY input. If M_e is total, for each j a sufficiently large N exceeds both the first j halt times and an N for which h(N)>=j. If some input i never halts, then f_e(N)<=i forever.

For a very slow explicit h, e.g. floor(log_2(log_2(N+4))) with a fixed integer-rounding convention, each point f_e(N) can be computed in polynomial time in UNARY N: simulate finitely many inputs, each for N steps. Thus any general algorithm that takes e and decides whether every such exact, monotone, slowly bounded frontier diverges would decide the classical totality problem. The latter is Pi^0_2-complete: Andrew Marks's notes, Proposition 17.4, https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf .

This does NOT show that our specific B(N) is undecidable, Pi^0_2-complete, independent, or incapable of an analytic proof. It says that accurate finite data, monotonicity, pointwise computability and even a slow known upper envelope are not by themselves enough for a UNIVERSAL extrapolation algorithm. B may have additional structure; identifying and exploiting it is exactly the mathematical task.

A related modulus warning: there is no total computable F(e,k) that returns a guaranteed N with f_e(N)>=k for EVERY e whose f_e is unbounded. To see this, turn an arbitrary program P into M_e which simulates P on every input. M_e is total iff P halts. If such F existed, compute F(e,1), run P for that many steps, and decide whether P halts. For any ONE fixed unbounded computable f, the inverse can be computed by searching; the impossibility concerns one uniform total procedure for the whole family.

2. A DIRECT 109-NODE UPPER BOUND, INDEPENDENT OF FINITE BASIS SEARCHES

The other ChatGPT session's proposed matching lower bound for the grouped nine-pair model still depends on the finite basis results and normalization/one-merge lemmas. But its 109-node UPPER bound has a short direct derivation.

Complement the visible pair literals and write the target as z OR OR_(i=1..9)(x_i AND y_i). The visible order is x1,...,x9,z,y1,...,y9; put the one existential selector at the root. One selector branch represents

f0 = z OR OR_(i=1..4)(x_i AND y_i),

the other represents

f1 = OR_(i=5..9)(x_i AND y_i).

Existentially quantifying the selector yields f0 OR f1, exactly the target. In a standard reduced OBDD with ordinary edges and nonterminal nodes counted:
- f0 has 15 x-nodes, 16 z-nodes corresponding to the active subset, and 15 y-nodes.
- f1 has 31 x-nodes, skips z, and has 31 y-nodes.
- the selector root adds one.

Total: 15+16+15+31+31+1=109. The branches use disjoint pair variables; z is read only by f0. This is an upper-bound construction under exactly these ordering, selector and node-count conventions. I checked it by symbolic reasoning, not by generating a DAG. It does NOT prove 108 impossible. The r=2,3,4,5 arithmetic in the proposed lower-bound table is consistent with its stated outside-exit model, but I have not verified the finite basis searches, symmetry completeness, suffix normalization, or root-selector/one-merge reduction. Please keep those dependencies visible.

3. HOW THIS CAN HELP THE USER'S BIG QUESTION

The project now has three useful logically distinct things to put beside a chart:
- exact or conditional finite structures, such as the explicit 109-node witness;
- general model-dependent theorems, such as fixed-DFA full-PC encodings and hypergraph hitting-set formulas;
- a specific computable frontier B(N) whose asymptotic boundedness is equivalent, under stated assumptions, to P=NP.

The first two can be genuine progress without the third being settled. The family-of-frontiers reduction explains why 'all calculated points look this way' alone cannot settle the third. It does not discourage searching for an analytic invariant particular to B. The key test is whether a proposed invariant can be stated for all k, survives the oracle/model diagnostics in part 2/3, and supplies a proof rather than a hypothetical continuation of a curve.

END ADDENDUM B
— ChatGPT

