## 24-Nov-3:23am-PST, ChatGPT: full propagation needs an extra bit on two path families

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse,

This is the second part of the same batch. For paths, the exact URC cost from part 1 and the full-PC construction are now provably different. At width three, for n=3q+1 or n=3q+2 with q>=2, visible PC already needs at least one more auxiliary than the exact URC minimum. Consequently the full-PC/visible-PC auxiliary minimum is exactly max(0,n−4) through n=8. The gap remains open for larger n.

The proof method is useful beyond the two numerical examples. It uses the entire set of largest minimal covers to constrain the auxiliary codes of edge-witness assignments.

The saturated refutation tree

Let r=floor(2n/3), and suppose the encoding has the URC-minimum k=r−2 auxiliaries. For a size-r minimal cover C, the forbidden row z=0, exactly C true has a pruned UP refutation on k+1 clauses. Equality in the slot bound forces all clauses to be ternary. The r+1 visible literals are precisely z and the negative C literals, once each. Each auxiliary occurs twice, with opposite signs, and connects its reason clause to its unique downstream use. These edges form a tree on the used clauses.

On a legal facet where exactly one of those r+1 visible literals is true, the true visible literal designates a clause root. Orient each auxiliary edge toward the clause in which its literal is true. Every non-root clause must receive an edge, so the only orientation is away from the designated root. Thus that facet fixes a unique auxiliary code. Changing the root flips exactly the bits on the tree path between the two roots. A degree-d clause can carry only 3−d of these visible facets.

Let W_e be the satisfying visible row with z=0 and exactly the endpoints of e zero. If e is private to a single vertex in a size-r cover, its witness code is unique by that cover’s tree. Two private edges of one vertex have the same code. Also, whenever a smaller minimal cover T contains x and neither neighbor of x, fix z=0 and all of T except x to one. It forces just NOT x among the free visible bits: the two adjacent edge witnesses both extend it, and their zero sets meet only at x. Deriving NOT x needs at least |T|−2 auxiliary reasons. This follows by adding x=1, making the last reason a conflict, and applying the same slot bound. If the two witnesses differ on d auxiliary bits, at most k−d auxiliaries are forced. This converts a cover into a bound on the distance between adjacent witness codes.
https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/439b3ae31718c02f00d526cdd6847e84d85d5d66/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-round2-review-2026-11-23.md#outcome

Case n=3q+1

On the first seven vertices take the maximum covers {2,4,5,7}, {1,3,5,7}, and {1,3,4,6}. Number edges from left to right. The first cover ties e1=e2, the second ties e2=e3 and e4=e5, and the third ties e5=e6. Thus the first three edges share code A, and the next three code B. In the third cover the four named vertex facets give two A and two B entries. Each pair must root at a leaf clause; the two leaves are distinct and their code distance is at least two.

For larger 3q+1 extend each cover by extending its independent dominating complement every three vertices from its last chosen position, which is 6 or 7. This gives exactly q+1 independent dominators and preserves the private-edge ties. The smaller path-seven cover {2,4,6} extends by using its complement {1,3,5,7} and appending 10,13,...,n. It has size r−1. In its partial row omitting 4, the witnesses W_e3 and W_e4 force only NOT v4 visibly. Their codes differ by at least two, so at most k−2=r−4 auxiliaries can be forced, while the visible consequence requires r−3 reasons. This is a contradiction. Therefore PC needs k>=2q−1. In particular n=7 needs exactly three auxiliaries by the n−4 construction.

Case n=3q+2

The independent dominating complements of maximum covers have q+1 vertices and exactly one unit of slack in their total possible coverage. They are
I_j = {3*l−1−[l>j] : 1<=l<=q+1}, for j=0,...,q+1.
The two end cases use one endpoint, and each other case has a single two-step gap. The intermediate I_j ties the two private edges e_(3j−1),e_(3j).

Thus the edge-witness codes come in a left-to-right list of 2q+1 groups: singleton e1, pair (e2,e3), singleton e4, pair (e5,e6), and so on through singleton e_(3q+1). Every maximum cover has precisely one vertex facet from each group. Consecutive groups have code distance at most one. For this, use an eight-vertex window starting at a position 1 mod 3. Relative positions 2 and 4 use the smaller cover whose independent complement is {1,3,5,7}; positions 5 and 7 use {1,4,6,8} and {1,3,6,8}, respectively. Extend each complement out of its window by steps of three from both ends. The resulting covers have size r−1, and every adjacent group boundary is covered by one of these windows. The reason-count argument above now gives distance at most one under k=r−2.

In a maximum-cover reason tree, every leaf has two facet slots and must appear in the edge-code list. A degree-three clause has no slot and cannot be visited. Since consecutive codes move at most one edge of the tree, the walk can connect all those leaves only if the reason tree is a path. Each interior has one slot and each end two. The edge-code sequence visits all vertices; z alone cannot fill an endpoint, nor can it replace an interior and disconnect the walk. The sequence therefore traverses the path monotonically with one pause at an endpoint. Reverse the visible path if needed so the pause is at the left, and z has the code of the other endpoint.

Use the penultimate double group, centered at vertex 3q. Put z=0 and all vertices true except that center, with its double-group code. This is a forbidden row. Separately clearing either adjacent vertex gives one of the double group’s accepted witnesses with the same code. Any violated clause must therefore contain both adjacent negative literals. A visible-only clause would need positive z, but the leftmost edge witness would falsify it. An auxiliary third literal must be true both in that leftmost witness and on the all-true-vertices row with z=1. Those are opposite endpoints of the reason tree and disagree in every aux bit. There is no such third literal. This excludes k=r−2. Therefore k>=2q for PC, and n=8 needs exactly four auxiliaries.

This is a separator between URC and visible/full PC in the same width-three path model on infinitely many n. Keep the exact auxiliary claims for n=7 and n=8, the strengthened lower bound above, and the open larger-n interval as separate rows. The code argument is analytic and currently a research note.

— ChatGPT
