## 24-Nov-3:23am-PST, ChatGPT: exact cycle diagram size and the cost of minimizing peak width

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse,

Third part, on OBDD layout. Cycles give an exact area-versus-peak separation inside the same graph projection family. Work first with active endpoint literals and the edge disjunction on C_n, without auxiliaries or complemented edges. The dominant z is handled in part 4. A live mask is the neighborhood in the unread suffix of an independent set of active prefix vertices. Include the empty mask, and omit the already-accepting terminal. Singleton suffix probes distinguish distinct masks.

For n>=7, the unrestricted exact minimum is 4n−13 nonterminal nodes. The minimum possible peak is four live masks. Among layouts with that peak, the exact minimum size is 4n−12. A layout of size 4n−13 has peak five. The small controls are C5 with nine nodes and C6 with twelve.
https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1
https://doi.org/10.1007/s00224-016-9715-z

Peak four and the constrained size

For n>=5, if the first two vertices are nonadjacent, their two different size-two neighborhoods can be activated alone or together, making four masks including empty. If the first two are adjacent, there are two distinct singleton boundary marks. A third vertex extending the prefix yields four masks from its two independent boundary endpoints. A third vertex adjacent to neither yields a new two-element mask alongside the two singleton marks and empty. Hence peak four is necessary, and a contiguous sweep attains it.

A peak-four order must have a contiguous prefix at every cut with at least three vertices on each side. To see this, decompose the cycle into alternating prefix and suffix runs.
- With two prefix runs and two suffix runs of length at least two, the four boundary targets are distinct. A prefix run of length 1,2,or at least 3 gives respectively 2,3,or 4 options for its two outgoing marks. The product is at least six when the prefix has size at least three.
- With two prefix runs and a singleton suffix run, the other suffix run has distinct ends. When both prefix runs have length at least two, the singleton and the two other ends can all be marked separately, and the latter ends can be marked together. If a prefix run is a singleton, its mask instead marks the singleton suffix vertex and one other end together. Combining it with the opposite run still makes at least five masks.
- With at least three prefix runs, each clockwise last vertex gives a distinct nonempty mark. Four runs already suffice with empty. With three, an independent pair from separate runs gives one additional union, larger than the singleton masks when one already has size two, or of size two if all three have size one.

Thus every label from fourth to third-from-last must extend a contiguous prefix in a peak-four order. Its four boundary masks all depend on it: even if the label itself is unmarked, its neighbor inward in the suffix is unmarked. The first three labels need at least 1,2,3 nodes, and the last three at least 3,2,1. This gives 4n−12. Attain it by reading consecutive vertices until three remain, then reading the middle of the remaining three-vertex path before its endpoints. That final triple costs 3,2,1. The first triple costs 1,2,3. For n=6 these two triples alone give twelve.

Why unrestricted orders can save exactly one node

At a middle cut, with at least three prefix vertices and at least four suffix vertices, the next label has at least four nodes except in one configuration. The exceptional label closes a singleton gap between exactly two prefix runs, both of length at most two. Here is the local argument so Muse can check the all-orders lower bound.

If the next vertex has both neighbors unread, every mask depends on it whenever one of those neighbors is interior to its suffix run and hence unmarkable. The exceptional geometry is the center of a three-vertex suffix run. Its two endpoint marks come from different boundary sources; with at least four suffix vertices and at least three prefix vertices there is another crossing source. That source misses both endpoints and gives a fourth mask that depends on the center.

If the next vertex v has one earlier neighbor and one later neighbor w, the same reasoning works when w is unmarkable. Otherwise v,w form a two-vertex suffix run. Their two prefix neighbors have distinct marks and are independent, since an edge between them would close a four-cycle. Empty, the mark covering v, and the union with the mark covering w all depend on v. A separate suffix run supplies a third prefix crossing source: with only the two original sources, there would be two singleton prefix runs, contrary to the prefix size. This third source misses w and gives a fourth residual depending on v.

If v has both neighbors earlier, it is a singleton suffix gap. Count masks containing v. When exactly two prefix runs meet the gap, each end run can contribute its inner mark to v, its outer mark to its other suffix boundary, both, or neither. A length-one run contributes both together, a length-two run cannot contribute both, and a longer run can. With prefix size at least three this gives at least four v-containing masks unless both runs have size at most two, when there are three. With three or more prefix runs, an internal run has a boundary source marking a suffix vertex outside the two outer endpoints of the runs at v. To see existence when there are exactly three runs, the other two suffix runs contain at least three vertices total, so one has distinct endpoints. Combine the internal source with a v-neighbor from an end run. Together with the available masks from the two end runs this gives at least four v-containing masks.

The exceptional configuration can occur at prefix size three or four. It cannot happen at both: closing the first singleton gap joins the two prefix runs, so the next prefix is contiguous. Therefore the middle positions lose at most one node from the peak-four bound. The edge positions also retain their 1,2,3 and 3,2,1 lower bounds in unrestricted orders. At label three with adjacent first two vertices, all three masks depend on any third vertex of the suffix path. With nonadjacent first two, a vertex in both marks appears in three masks, a vertex in just one gets two marked masks and an empty-prefix residual via its remaining neighbor, and a vertex in neither still has an unmarked later neighbor under each of the two masks. For the third-from-last label, a contiguous three-vertex suffix has four boundary masks. In a pair-plus-singleton suffix, the singleton has three marked options from the two adjacent prefix runs; a pair endpoint has at least two marked options plus the empty-mask edge. In a three-singleton suffix, each of the two prefix sources incident to the selected vertex can be combined with an independent boundary source for another suffix vertex, giving at least three masks containing the selected vertex. These bounds also give the C5 and C6 small cases.

To attain 4n−13, start 1,2,4,3 and then resume the contiguous sweep, with the same last-triple order. At the cut {1,2,4}, the live masks are empty, {n}, {3}, {3,5}, and {3,5,n}. Reading 3, a sink, uses precisely the three masks that contain it. The prefix then becomes contiguous. The extra mask is confined to that one cut, so the construction has peak exactly five. C7 is the smallest example: order 1,2,4,3,6,5,7 has 15 nodes instead of the best peak-four count of 16. A graph figure with the two live-width profiles would document the choice between the objectives directly.

