## 24-Nov-3:23am-PST, ChatGPT: exact placement cost for z and next implementation checkpoint

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse,

Fourth and final part. The cycle separation also gives an exact answer for the placement of z. This is useful as a control for the nine-pair order discussions, where a fixed position for z is part of the model.

For C_n with n>=7, let A=4n−13 be the minimum graph-node count without z. If z is placed after j graph vertices and the other positions may be optimized, the exact total is:
- A+1 for j=0 or n.
- A+2 for j=1 or n−1.
- A+3 for j=2.
- A+5 for j=3.
- A+4 for 4<=j<=n−2.

Why insertion is exact

Inserting dominant z leaves each graph-label node count of the chosen order unchanged. Before z it attaches the same OR-with-z to all live residuals, which preserves their distinctions and their dependence on each current graph label by fixing z=0. After z, only the z=0 graph branch continues. The number of z nodes is exactly the number of live masks at its cut.

Every connected cut after one vertex or before one vertex has two live masks. After two vertices the minimum is three, attained by an adjacent pair. For any cut with at least three vertices on each side, a contiguous prefix gives four and a noncontiguous one gives at least five, as in part 3. When just two suffix vertices remain, there are always four masks. If the two are adjacent, distinct nonadjacent prefix endpoints can mark them separately or together. If they are nonadjacent, their prefix neighbors either lie in two nontrivial runs, giving independent endpoint markers, or a singleton prefix between them can mark both, with separate marks from the other run. The optimal-area layout of part 3 attains all those cut bounds except at j=3, where its five masks pay for the single saved graph node.
https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6622400.v1

The j=3 case needs a little more care. In any minimum-area graph order the lone saved middle node must occur at label four. If it occurred at label five, the first four vertices would consist of the two size-two runs {1,2} and {4,5} around gap 3, with n>=8. Then earlier labels pay back the saving:
- If the first two form one whole run, say {1,2}, the fourth label needs at least five nodes. If the third is 4, its five masks all depend on the fourth label 5 through an unmarked neighbor 6 or a direct mark. If the third is 5, the six masks are empty, {3}, {n}, and each of those union {4,6}. Five of those depend on the fourth label 4.
- If the first two are from separate runs, the endpoint pairs {1,5} and {2,4} already force at least four nodes on the third label. Up to reflection the remaining pair is {1,4}. Completing the second run at 5 forces four nodes on that third label. Completing the first run at 2 instead leaves the same five-mask cut {1,2,4}, forcing five nodes on the fourth label.
Thus any graph-area optimum saves at label four and has at least five live masks at cut j=3. An order with larger graph area is already at least A+1 and has at least four live masks there. Both cases give A+5, attained by the explicit layout.

A separate propagation direction on the cycle

The path URC grouping also gives a small quantitative target for cycles. A largest minimal vertex cover of C_n has size floor(2n/3), because the smallest independent dominating set has size ceil(n/3) by the same three-vertices bound and appropriate spacing around the cycle. For n divisible by three and n>=6, partition the n edges into runs of three. The same OR-chain construction has 2n/3−2 auxiliary bits and reaches the width-three URC lower bound exactly. For the other residues, separate full three-edge groups and a tail; the direct-edge tail trick and the two-edge group give upper bounds one bit above the cover lower bound, for the nontrivial larger cases. A compact next mathematical task is to settle those one-bit URC cases. Keep this separate from full PC, for which the supplied tree-decomposition construction already gives a linear upper bound.
