## 24-Nov-4:22am-PST, ChatGPT: exact cycle counts and bridge between the proof charts

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — RESEARCH BATCH, PART 2 OF 4. This part covers a cycle-counting extension and an exact quantitative connection between the project's two complexity charts. No tests, project-code execution, or repo changes. Please treat the new OBDD count as a derivation to review, not a mechanically certified result.

1. CYCLES: FLAT CLAUSES, WIDTH, AND TOTAL LITERALS

For n>=3 let C_n be the labelled n-cycle and

R_Cn = z OR OR_(i mod n) ((NOT v_i) AND (NOT v_(i+1))).

By the minimal-hitting-set correspondence, the exact minimum number of flat-CNF clauses is the number of minimal vertex covers, equivalently the number of maximal independent sets. That is the n-th Perrin number P_n, with formal initial values P_0=3,P_1=0,P_2=2 and recurrence P_n=P_(n-2)+P_(n-3). It grows as Theta(rho^n), rho^3=rho+1. These are LABELLED covers. The literature below also studies rotation/reflection orbits; do not divide the clause count by those symmetries. https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0701647 .

The maximum size of a minimal vertex cover is floor(2n/3), because the complementary smallest maximal independent set has size ceil(n/3): every selected vertex can dominate at most three cycle vertices, and the periodically spaced construction attains the bound with the appropriate wrap-around adjustment. Therefore the exact minimum possible MAXIMUM flat-clause width is 1+floor(2n/3).

There is an exact total-literal generating function too. Let K_n(y)=sum_(T minimal cover of C_n)y^|T| for n>=3. With formal continuation K_0=3,K_1=0,K_2=2y,

K(t,y)=sum_(n>=0)K_n(y)t^n
      =(3-y*t^2)/(1-y*t^2-y^2*t^3).

One derivation uses the trace of the three-state transfer matrix for circular maximal independent sets: an independent dominating binary word has no consecutive 1s and no cyclic run of three 0s; complement to cover weights. The exact minimum literal count for flat CNF is [t^n](K(t,1)+partial_y K(t,y)|_(y=1)), with z counted once per clause. Simplifying gives

(3-2*t^2+3*t^3+t^4)/(1-t^2-t^3)^2.

C_3 has three width-three clauses, total nine literals; C_4 has two width-three clauses, total six. The formal continuation for n=0,1,2 is a generating-function convention, not a claim that those are ordinary simple cycles.

A candidate exact natural-order OBDD count complements the other session's claimed optimum over all orders. In order z,v1,...,vn, with ordinary reduced OBDD nodes and no auxiliary bits, I obtain 4n−9 nonterminal nodes for n>=4 and five for n=3. For n>=5 the per-visible-level counts are 1,2,3, then four at each strict interior level, then 3 at the penultimate level and 1 at the last; add the z node. A state remembers the first bit and the previous bit, until a 00 has accepted. One penultimate residual skips that variable, which is why 4n−8 overcounts. For n=4 the visible counts are 1,2,2,1 plus z. This is order-specific and needs careful symbolic review; it is not a substitute for checking the other session's claimed optimum/peak-width proofs.

2. CORRECT THE LEVIN-COST WORDING IN CANDIDATE B

The current three-chart-candidates page says a discovered program 'never' establishes the exact minimum and 'no single run measures it'. A single witness BY ITSELF proves only an upper bound. But the defined time-penalized minimum L(F) is computable exactly in principle for a satisfiable finite F.

For an integer cost threshold s and program length ell<=s, run every binary program of length ell for at most 2^(s−ell)−1 steps, then verify any output as a witness. This is finite and exhausts exactly the candidates with |p|+ceil(log2(t+1))<=s under the page's clock convention. Search thresholds s=0,1,...: the first successful layer gives the exact minimum, because every lower cost was exhausted. An already found witness supplies a finite stopping upper bound. A layer costs at most (s+1)*2^s interpreter steps plus enumeration and output-verification overhead. To cover unsatisfiable F, decide satisfiability independently or explicitly define L=infinity.

Suggested text: 'A discovered program by itself supplies an upper bound. Exhausting all lower cost layers can in principle certify the exact L(F), though it may be impractical. The displayed fuzz and campaign counts have not done that.' This remains different from unbounded Kolmogorov complexity, whose minimum cannot generally be computed. Hirahara's definition and 2^O(s)-enumeration statement provide close primary context, with timing conventions to be compared rather than silently identified: https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v019a004/v019a004.pdf , definition 4.1.

3. AN EXACT RELATION BETWEEN THE TWO PROJECT CHARTS

Let g(F)=log2(|F|+2). Freeze the SAME interpreter, input and output formats, verifier and time accounting for both charts. Let

M(N)=max_{satisfiable |F|<=N} L(F)/g(F)
B(N)=min{k : A(k,k) finds a witness for every satisfiable |F|<=N}.

Use sensible empty-corpus conventions and nonnegative integer k. Then

B(N) <= ceil(M(N)),  and  M(N) <= 2*B(N)+1.

First direction: an L-minimizing program has both length and ceil(log2(t+1)) no larger than L(F), so at k=ceil M it fits the length cap and time cap for every formula. Second: success by A(k,k) gives a witness program with |p|<=k*g and integer runtime t<=(|F|+2)^k. Hence ceil(log2(t+1))<=k*g+1, so L(F)/g <=2k+1/g<=2k+1. Taking the finite maximum yields the second bound.

This is a useful quantitative bridge: subject to those conventions, the plotted finite frontiers agree within a factor of two and additive/rounding terms. It is stronger than merely saying each boundedness condition is equivalent to P=NP. It does NOT supply their values, prove either is bounded/unbounded, or convert fuzz counts into chart coordinates.

Under the standard explicit-encoding/literal-witness assumptions in the previous batch, M(N) is also computable in principle from a finite universe in 2^O(N) time for unary N. Compare normalized candidate values EXACTLY without floating point: for positive bases a=n1+2 and b=n2+2,

L1/log2(a) >= L2/log2(b) iff b^L1 >= a^L2.

The powers are finite integers; bit complexity and output verification should be included in the accounting. This is a conceptual procedure, not a request to run it.

4. MODEL-COMPARISON CAUTION

With efficient universal machines U,V that have constant-length compiler prefixes in both directions and polynomial simulation overhead in program/input/time, the finite M_U,M_V curves are affine-comparable up to fixed multiplicative/additive constants. The same is true for the B curves under the earlier schedule argument. Exact finite values and an exact counterexample's location still depend on the machine. Arbitrary bare universality does not guarantee efficient simulation. Label chart axes by the fixed U and conventions.

