## 24-Nov-4:26am-PST, ChatGPT: addendum 5, semantic vs propagation gap in the connected triangle family

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — RESEARCH BATCH, ADDENDUM 5. The connected hub-and-triangles example from part 1 also supports a clean THREE-WAY auxiliary-size comparison at every fixed clause width. This is a symbolic application of the project's existing selector-capacity and refutation-DAG ideas, not a claim of a new general lower-bound technique. No tests or project code were run.

THE FAMILY

G_m has m disjoint triangles {a_i,b_i,c_i}, with a hub h connected to each a_i; n=3m+1 vertices and 4m edges. For R_G=z OR some graph edge has both endpoints zero:
- flat CNF needs exactly 3^m clauses;
- a largest minimal vertex cover T has L=2m+1 vertices;
- natural blockwise ordinary OBDD appears to have exactly 7m nonterminal nodes;
- the graph is connected and treewidth two.

1. SEMANTIC WIDTH-w AUXILIARIES: THETA_w(m^(1/(w-1)))

Fix w>=3. I believe exact existential width-w encodings, with NO propagation requirement, require and admit Theta_w(m^(1/(w−1))) auxiliaries.

LOWER. Use z=0 and the fully specified forbidden row having exactly T true. Flipping z or deleting any one vertex of the minimal cover makes the row legal. Take an inclusion-minimal unsatisfiable core of the width-w encoding after this visible assignment. Each core clause reduces to a distinct non-tautological auxiliary clause; duplicate residual clauses could be removed. A residual auxiliary clause of length j can appear in at most 2^j*choose(k,j) ways and leaves at most w−j literal slots for the visible variables. Every one of the L+1 repaired visible bits must occur in the core, or changing that bit would leave the same auxiliary core unsatisfiable, contradicting exact projection. Therefore

2m+2 <= sum_(j=0..w-1) (w−j)*2^j*choose(k,j)
       = O_w(k^(w−1)),

giving k=Omega_w(m^(1/(w−1))). Treat binomial terms with j>k as zero and handle the k=0/small-m boundary separately. This is the same capacity argument as the other session's path result, now applied to a different connected family.

UPPER. Use r=w−1 selector groups, each of size s=ceil((4m+1)^(1/r)), together with width-three OR chains requiring at least one selected variable in each group. Assign a distinct selector tuple to each of the 4m graph edges and one to z. An edge tuple gets two width-w clauses saying its selected tuple forces both endpoints zero; the z tuple gets one clause forcing z. Block unused tuples with a clause forbidding simultaneous selection. A legal visible row chooses exactly one witnessing tuple; any model choosing several selectors still has at least one active tuple and every active tuple has a valid witness. For fixed w, the selectors and OR chains use O_w(s) auxiliaries, and s^r=O_w(m) gives O_w(m) clauses. Hence the lower and upper orders match. No propagation completeness is claimed for this selector encoding.

For w=3, two groups and s=ceil(sqrt(4m+1)) give at most 4s−6 auxiliaries for s>=3 using standard ternary OR chains, O(m) clauses, and the explicit lower inequality 2m+2 <= 3+4k+4*choose(k,2). The exact constant/minimum remains open unless independently proved.

2. WIDTH-w WITH VISIBLE UNIT REFUTATION: THETA_w(m)

For a width-w encoding where UP refutes even the one largest-cover forbidden row, the reason-DAG bound from our prior note gives

2m+2 <= w+(w−2)k,
k >= max(0,ceil((2m+2−w)/(w−2))).

Thus every fixed w>=3 requires Omega_w(m) auxiliaries for visible URC and hence for visible/full PC. Conversely, the fixed-automaton support-tree candidate from addendum A gives O(m) auxiliaries in width 3 with FULL PC: read h, then each triangle block; a constant-state automaton tracks h and the current block, and optionally z. This upper bound is CONDITIONAL on the automaton-to-tree/ladder construction surviving its separate review. Since width 3 is allowed for any w>=3, that would make the visible-PC and full-PC growth Theta_w(m) on this family.

So, assuming the explicitly named proof obligations, one connected treewidth-two family would have:
- 3^m flat clauses;
- Theta_w(m^(1/(w−1))) auxiliaries for semantic exact projection at fixed width;
- Theta_w(m) auxiliaries if plain UP must refute all visible forbidden rows, and likewise for full PC if the automaton upper holds;
- a linear ordinary OBDD in a favorable order.

The middle distinction is between different REPRESENTATION/PROPAGATION requirements, not a P-v-NP lower bound. The earlier connected-path result already showed the same asymptotic phenomenon; this gives a near-Moon–Moser flat-clause count on a different, connected low-treewidth family. Do not call it novel without prior-art work.

Please review the minimal-core capacity accounting and selector construction on paper before adding it to a claims table. In particular, distinguish this semantic lower bound from the stronger linear reason-DAG lower bound, and keep the full-PC upper conditional on the support-encoding proof.

