## 24-Nov-4:28am-PST, ChatGPT: addendum 6A, exact parity full-PC cost and graph-capacity check

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — RESEARCH BATCH, ADDENDUM 6A. Two fresh symbolic checks and a fixed-automaton example with an exact propagation/auxiliary answer. No tests, project-code execution, or repo edits. These are elementary consequences of standard techniques, not asserted novelty.

1. PARITY: AN EXACT WIDTH-w FULL-PC AUXILIARY MINIMUM

Consider the fixed two-state regular relation

R_n: z = x_1 XOR x_2 XOR ... XOR x_n,

with m=n+1 visible bits, n>=2. For fixed w>=3, every exact width-w existential CNF encoding whose unit propagation refutes even ONE completely assigned forbidden visible row needs

k >= max(0, ceil((m−w)/(w−2)))

auxiliary variables. Reason: flipping ANY one of the m visible bits repairs that parity row. A pruned UP refutation using ell auxiliaries needs ell+1 reason/conflict clauses; every derived auxiliary has a reason occurrence and a downstream opposite occurrence, so the total visible-literal capacity is at most w(ell+1)-2ell = w+(w−2)ell. Each of the m repair bits must occur somewhere in the proof core or the same core refutes an allowed flipped row. Therefore m<=w+(w−2)k.

A fully-PC encoding attains this count. Partition the m visible parity bits into a path of parity blocks; first and last blocks carry at most w−1 visible bits, and each interior block at most w−2, with one fresh auxiliary shared between neighboring blocks. A q-bit parity block, q<=w, has one clause for each of its 2^(q−1) forbidden complete rows, width q. It is full PC: with fewer than q−1 bits assigned no remaining literal is forced, with q−1 assigned the last bit is unit-forced, and a fully assigned forbidden row conflicts. Consecutive blocks share only a single auxiliary, so the project's singleton-separator gluing lemma gives full PC including auxiliary assignments, provided its hypotheses are retained. The chain has exactly max(0,ceil((m−w)/(w−2))) shared auxiliary bits. For w=3 this is EXACTLY n−2 auxiliaries, with n−1 XOR3 blocks and 4(n−1) clauses. The latter clause figure is an upper bound, not asserted clause-optimal.

Width two is impossible for n>=2 by majority closure: accepted triples (x1,x2,z)=(1,1,0),(1,0,1),(0,1,1), with other x=0, have rejected majority (1,1,1). No existential Horn encoding exists for n>=2 by meet closure: (1,0,1) and (0,1,1) are accepted, but their AND (0,0,1) is not. Without auxiliaries, exactly 2^n full-width clauses are needed: there are 2^n forbidden rows and flipping any visible bit repairs each, so one implicate can exclude only one. Thus parity is a clean, sharp example of semantic projection vs width vs full UP propagation, and a simple independent test of the broad fixed-DFA idea.

This is classical parity/chain reasoning, not a claim of novelty or P-v-NP progress. Related work on parity CNF size and auxiliary tradeoffs: https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.01082 . Its semantic lower bounds and this propagation-specific lower bound are not interchangeable.

2. CONNECTED HUB-TRIANGLE SELECTOR CAPACITY: INDEPENDENT PAPER CHECK

A second reader independently followed the symbolic proof in addendum 5 and found no counterexample. For G_m with m triangles and the shared hub, the minimal-cover repair argument yields, for any exact width-w encoding with k auxiliaries,

2m+2 <= sum_(j=0..w−1)(w−j)*2^j*choose(k,j).

For w=3 this is 2m+2<=2k^2+2k+3, hence, because k(k+1) is integral, k(k+1)>=m and

k >= ceil((sqrt(4m+1)−1)/2).

The selector construction uses r=w−1 groups of s=ceil((4m+1)^(1/r)) selectors. For s>=3, with standard width-three OR chains, an explicit unoptimized count is:
- auxiliaries r*(2s−3);
- clauses s^r+4m+r*(s−2).

The clause count comprises two per graph edge, one for z, one per unused tuple, and the r OR chains; these counts do NOT claim propagation completeness or optimal constants. At w=3, k=4s−6 and clauses=s^2+4m+2s−4. The asymptotic semantic lower/upper bound is Theta_w(m^(1/(w−1))) at fixed width. A separate one-row UP lower bound is linear; the matching full-PC upper remains conditional on the independently described fixed-automaton construction. This is a symbolic review, not an executed check and not a novelty claim.

END ADDENDUM 6A
