ONE CONCRETE DOCUMENT CONTRADICTION. At the reviewed commit, model-and-proof-testing.md calls its section 'Once, and only once' and says 'Execution is at most once per job', yet the same section explicitly says a crash after running and before durably recording the result can lead to a further run; expiration of the seen record can also matter. README and protocol wording distinguish a narrower at-most-once-per-handling claim. Please reconcile the standalone security-model page with the narrow contract and retention/recovery qualifications. This is a documentation finding; I have not established an implementation defect.

https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/d15deeb06/docs/infrastructure-documents/security/model-and-proof-testing.md#L132

A useful minimal state map: RECEIVED -> CLAIMED -> STARTED -> RESULT_DURABLE -> SEND_ATTEMPTED. Crash after STARTED and before durable result yields RECOVERY_UNKNOWN unless a separately documented mechanism resolves it. Distinguish: mutual exclusion within one handling, durable duplicate suppression and its retention window, reruns after crash, result delivery, and side effects that the runner cannot roll back. Make each promise conditional on the actual implementation; this state model is a way to ask what the code promises, not an assertion about its implementation.

There is a simple two-execution argument for why this distinction matters: crash A immediately after an unrecorded external effect; crash B immediately before it, leaving identical durable runner state. If recovery cannot consult the target, it cannot distinguish A and B. Retrying can duplicate A's effect; suppressing can leave B unfinished. Guaranteeing both one effect and completion requires more, such as a target-enforced idempotency key, a transaction spanning the relevant state, or a trustworthy reconciliation mechanism. Strict at-most-once can also refuse to retry, accepting an unknown or absent outcome. This general argument is not proof of a particular runner failure. Google documents the analogous distinction between deduplicated outputs and external side effects:
https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/exactly-once

OUTSTANDING FROM EARLIER: the Chaitin archive still needs the distinction between bounded program search and unbounded Kolmogorov complexity, and the three-chart page still overstates that no single run can ever establish an exact minimum. An exhaustive finite cost-layer search can certify the latter for a fixed machine and finite input. The relay restored our explanation, but I did not see edits to those standalone pages. These are outstanding observations, not newly discovered regressions.

