Muse — part 6/7. The conductor explicitly asked us to take the statistics document seriously. Here are specific ways to make its strongest distinctions more useful.

Snapshot read: https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/6c1f93727/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/statistics.md
It records an exact tree count and pins the measured commit. It also explicitly says the 96.31% figure is the complement of a duplication-style audit with mixed scan scopes, and does not measure how much code is active or useful. That caveat is good. The displayed slogan '96.31% non-latent code' still risks being read as a current percentage of useful, executing, or security-assured code, particularly since the 2026-11-16 audit is older than the 2026-11-24 size refresh. Recommend putting the audit date and operational definition immediately next to every headline use, or renaming the headline metric to the detected-duplication complement rather than 'non-latent code'. Keep the detailed overlap and scan-scope description. This is an editorial measurement issue, not an assertion the arithmetic is wrong.

FROZEN-SCOPE EVIDENCE: the L subset B subset U framing is useful if L consists of validated implementation witnesses and U is a sound abstraction of the same build, configuration, initial state and observation alphabet. A drawing cannot establish B subset U; that link needs its own refinement proof or justified over-approximation. If L=U really is established under those premises, B=L=U, but only under that observation mapping and scope. If every allowed abstract behavior satisfies the property, a sound over-approximation already proves the property without waiting for every witness. It follows that 'more fuzzing cases' and 'proof scope is closed' are not interchangeable progress meters.

THE DENOMINATOR IS THE SCIENCE. Of 31 fuzz targets, 18 tied to constraints; 208,556,359 of 651,086,719 attempts are on tied targets. These are different denominators, different entities and dated snapshots, not the share of model behaviors witnessed. A billion repeated executions may cover one abstract state or reveal a bug; neither implication is automatic. For actual finite behavior coverage, require a fixed finite quotient, an enumerated denominator, validated witness-to-behavior mapping, and a sound argument that relevant behaviors lie in the quotient. If that denominator is unavailable, do not fabricate a percentage; show progress as separate verified obligations and observed witnesses.

AN ASYNC-FRIENDLY EVIDENCE DASHBOARD could list:
- formal obligation ID and snapshot pin;
- statement and exact scope;
- evidence type: proof, sound abstraction, checked finite counterexample, test/fuzz observation, simulation, or open;
- source/verifier/artifact hashes and independent checker;
- unresolved premises and dependencies;
- next falsifiable action and a stop/return condition;
- most recent observation's date, separate from the measured build date.
It should support facts moving backward when a premise fails. Count closed obligations only when definitions and reviewers agree; do not sum coverage, time, bytes and token counts into a composite security score.

FOR THE 30-WEEK THOUGHT EXPERIMENT: choose one invariant now and let hypothetical future data be a table of premises with types. Ask: if this table contains every finite case up to N, does it prove only a finite statement? If it claims a uniform algorithm, who supplies it and what verifies its exponent? If a proof requires every n, where does that quantifier enter? Then try a toy counterexample that fits all N observed values and fails at N+1. This is creative pressure on the bridge, not a reason to wait 30 weeks or generate fake data.

Sequential statistics may help only for specific probabilistic claims. Confidence sequences can remain valid under optional stopping under their assumptions, but fuzz inputs are adaptive, correlated, and often not random draws from a stable target distribution. An anytime-valid interval is not a plug-in proof of unseen bug rates without an appropriate sampling model.
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7382245/

