k=1: at most 109 nodes;
k=2: at most 39;
k=3: at most 29;
k=4: at most 28.

General construction: use q nonempty blocks, where q<=min(2^k,m), with a complete prefix selector tree of q leaves, at most k selector levels, and q-1 internal nodes. Write m=q*t+h, 0<=h<q. Put z in one block of size t; use h ordinary blocks of size t+1 and the remaining ordinary blocks of size t. At each leaf, compute the grouped-order OBDD for its block; existential projection over the selector chooses any block. The projected OR is exactly F_m.

Using the earlier node-count formulas, the construction costs at most

(q-1) + (3*2^t-2) + h*(2*2^(t+1)-2)
+ (q-1-h)*(2*2^t-2)

= (2q+1+2h)*2^t-q-1.

For m=9 take q=1,2,4,8,9 respectively to get the five values. Note q need not be a power of two: a full prefix tree with q leaves and max depth <=ceil(log2 q) is available. For q=9, use seven leaves at depth three and two at depth four. The 28-node upper can also be counted directly: eight selector nodes; nine x nodes; nine y nodes; and two z nodes in one special leaf that computes z OR (x_j AND y_j). Every other leaf computes only x_i AND y_i. The union of all leaves is F_9.

Only the zero-selector lower bound of 1534 follows from the residual-count argument already discussed. The 109 bound with one selector is an upper bound unless the remaining normalization and finite lower proof are independently verified. Likewise 39, 29 and 28 are upper bounds; do not label them exact. An ordinary reduced OBDD may merge nodes and be smaller, so sharing does not harm an upper count.

A new general LOWER bound on the post-z suffix node budget N gives a cheap tradeoff check. Suppose at most k selector bits can be chosen before all x,z decisions, and put q=2^k. For each subset S of the m first-endpoint bits, after x is fixed and z=0 the projected function is OR_{i in S} y_i. It is the union of at most q residual suffix functions, each rooted at a nonterminal or the false terminal. The true terminal cannot occur, because all these target functions are false on the all-zero y assignment. Distinct S require distinct unions, hence distinct subsets of at most q of the N suffix nodes. Therefore

sum_{i=0}^{min(q,N)} binom(N,i) >= 2^m.

For m=9,q=2 this elementary counting gives N>=32; stronger combinatorial arguments may improve it. It does not count selector, x or z nodes and thus is not a 109-node proof. Check assumptions on skipped variables and the placement of selectors, and use the bound as a sanity check against any purported smaller diagram.

