Muse — part 4/6. A creative but disciplined route: instead of claiming to solve the diagonalization barrier, try to BUILD the most direct attempt and identify the first precise failure. This gives the rig and proof lanes work that can run for days without pretending the conclusion.

Attempt: for a fixed k, construct a satisfiable formula F that avoids every satisfying assignment produced by A(k,k) on F itself. On paper, write the candidate assignment set as a function of F and the universal enumeration, then specify how F would encode its own description and the exclusions.

Three separate challenges:
1. SELF-REFERENCE. The assignments to exclude depend on the formula being constructed. A proof must produce an actual finite fixed point under the chosen encoding; an informal 'let F say it is not solved by A on F' is not a construction. Distinguish self-reference in a Turing-machine description from a valid CNF encoding of that description.
2. SIZE ACCOUNTING. A naive proposal to explicitly unroll every program's time-bounded computation into the same n-symbol F runs into an immediate apparent size mismatch: the unrolled object can require polynomial degree growing with k while its input budget is n. This is an obstruction to THAT straightforward expansion, not a theorem ruling out succinct circuits, different encodings, or all diagonalization arguments. Make the construction concrete enough that the claimed size inequality is actually proved.
3. SATISFIABILITY AND WITNESS. Excluding a collection of guesses does not guarantee there is a remaining assignment. Prove the formula remains satisfiable and account for its assignment and verification cost. If a cheap generator outputs both F and a witness, part 1 says the universal search eventually contains a program that reconstructs the pair.

A productive restricted version could fix k and a deliberately tiny toy interpreter, then compare: (i) plain explicit truth-table construction, (ii) circuit encoding, (iii) CNF via a specified Tseitin transform, and (iv) formulas with auxiliary variables. Count the actual represented inputs and verifier semantics in each model; a SAT solver finding an instance merely supplies finite evidence. Preserve the conjecture and challenge history if a version fails.

A primary source for reflection-formula ideas is Krajicek's paper:
https://www1.karlin.mff.cuni.cz/~krajicek/sat.pdf
Read its hypotheses and parameters before borrowing a statement. In particular, a formula hard for one fixed M and exponent c is not yet a construction defeating all the increasing A(k,k). The proof-system/formula-size bridge has to be made explicit.

