Bogdanov, Talwar and Wan, 'Hard instances for satisfiability and quasi-one-way functions', Theorem 1: assuming P!=NP, given a FIXED polynomial-time SAT search algorithm A and its fixed time bound, a polynomial-time procedure produces a satisfiable formula and witness on which A fails for infinitely many input lengths. Their Theorem 2 gives a randomized analogue under the assumption printed there, NP not subset BPP. Their abstract mentions NP not subset RP, so when quoting cite the particular theorem/version and its explicit assumption rather than smoothing the difference.
https://andrejb.net/pubs/quasicrypto-ics.pdf

This does NOT conflict with the previous obstruction for a single cheap generator producing witnessed defeats of the GROWING family A(k,k). The fixed-A construction's code, exponent and costs can depend on the chosen A and its time bound. A sequence of separate algorithms, each polynomial for fixed k but with a degree growing with k, need not be a single polynomial-time generator in k. The good lengths are also only infinitely many and need not be efficiently recognizable.

A precise stronger version of our obstruction: fix G and an efficient U simulation bounded by C*(t+k+n+1)^a when G(1^k) runs for t and outputs (F,w), |F|=n. The candidate program p_k encodes k in O(log k) bits and runs G. Once p_k fits the length budget, if

C*(t+k+n+1)^a <= (n+2)^k,

then A(k,k) must succeed on F whenever w is valid. Therefore a witnessed defeat forces that upper bound to exceed the search clock. In particular a fixed generator whose running time is polynomial in k+n cannot produce witnessed defeats for arbitrarily large k under these conventions. This is a necessary cost condition, not a lower bound for formula-only generation or a P-vs-NP result.

Earlier nearby work also matters: Maly and Müller, 'A remark on pseudo proof systems and hard instances of the satisfiability problem', studies hard sequences, optimal SAT search, and when randomized/invertible sampling can yield hard instances. Our observation sits in established territory; do not present the idea of a generator being reproducible by a solver as new.
https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/malq.201700009

CONCRETE MULTI-DAY PROOF TRACK:
1. Extract Bogdanov–Talwar–Wan's construction and explicitly parameterize dependence on |A|, its time exponent k, and output length n.
2. Substitute the actual A(k,k) or an agreed clocked representation; account for the universal search's full running time, not merely each candidate clock.
3. Compare generation cost with the per-program budget (n+2)^k. Identify the earliest lemma requiring a simulation degree or hidden constant that depends on k.
4. Independently challenge whether a uniform encoding, padding, or alternate parameter n could remove that dependence. If someone claims it can, run the witnessed-generator obstruction against the claim.
5. Record the exact open assertion left over and whether it is equivalent to, weaker than, or stronger than the original P-vs-NP statement. No claim of separation follows just from finding a growing exponent.

This is the sort of 'where exactly does the trick fail' work where a long parallel lane could find something the finite charts alone cannot.

END DEEP 4/7

