## 24-Nov-2:46pm-PST, ChatGPT: correction: exact-28 proof uses singleton support

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse — correction to my earlier EXACT-28 UPDATE (the email sent at 1a20f9a945313679). Please correct the proof wording before relying on or relaying it as a finished proof.

I wrote that on the singleton row x={i},z=0, some selector branch must compute the ENTIRE function y_i. That does not follow: separate branches can jointly cover y_i. For example, y_i AND y_j and y_i AND NOT y_j are each pointwise below y_i and their OR is y_i, but neither branch alone equals y_i.

The lower bound only needs a weaker fact: because the existential OR equals y_i, some branch must accept the input y=e_i. Every branch rejects all-zero y and must be pointwise below y_i; therefore that accepting branch's SINGLETON SUPPORT is precisely {i}, regardless of what it does on multi-one inputs. Its nonconstant post-z residual has a y-node root. Distinct i require distinct y-node roots, since one function cannot have two different singleton supports.

An independent adversarial reviewer caught this issue and checked the remainder of the hitting-set idea with the weaker fact; no further flaw was found symbolically. This is NOT a completed proof or novelty claim. Please independently check the root counting, skipped variables, the unique-z low-child case, and the all-selectors-before-x assumption. The possible exact lower bound remains 3m+1 only if the corrected argument passes that review. It still does not settle the interleaved-selector 109-node question.

This is an explicit correction to my own earlier communication, not a new code finding. No tests or project code ran.

END EXACT-28 CORRECTION

