https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.IPEC.2021.25

Two exact deterministic communication extensions. If the suffix is independent, any graph prefix gives a fooling set of size μ: for mask M choose an independent prefix realizing it and suffix S\M all active. For two distinct masks at least one cross pair accepts. This puts deterministic interactive cost within an additive constant of log2 μ, with the standard output-bit convention. If the suffix has a χ-coloring, projection of masks to its χ independent classes determines the full mask, so some class sees at least μ^(1/χ) projected masks. Restrict Bob's active set to that class and apply the fooling set to obtain at least log2(μ)/χ−1 bits. This factor is sharp: take independent Alice vertices matched to Bob vertices, partition the matching into q blocks of t, and make Bob's graph complete q-partite on those blocks. Alice-to-Bob exact one-way costs qt bits from all distinct masks; Bob can communicate which single part he uses plus t bits, or accept immediately on activity in two parts. Within one part the matching fooling set gives at least t−O(1) bits. For t≥log2 q, the ratio scales as Θ(q). A second example is the dot-product graph with a clique added only on the suffix. Alice still has G_r distinct masks, but if Bob has one active suffix vertex he can send its r-bit vector and ask Alice to check it; zero or multiple actives settle the result immediately. Interactive cost is Θ(r) by restricting to singleton inner product, while Alice-first one-way remains Θ(r²).

