Three finite propagation results came out of the same proof pass. These are exact auxiliary counts under width-three full PC, with a URC result in the bipartite case. They are proofs by finite algebraic case analysis.

1. Width-three full PC on C_5 uses exactly two auxiliaries

The URC theorem already gives the two-auxiliary lower bound. Number edges e1=12, e2=23, e3=34, e4=45, e5=51. Use bits s,t with code 00 allowing e1 or e2, code 10 allowing e3, code 01 allowing e5, and code 11 allowing e4 or z. An edge is allowed as a witness when both its y values are zero. The base clauses, writing v_i for y_i, are

    (s∨t∨¬v2)
    (t∨¬v1∨¬v3), (s∨¬v1∨¬v3)
    (¬s∨t∨¬v3), (¬s∨t∨¬v4)
    (s∨¬t∨¬v1), (s∨¬t∨¬v5)
    (¬s∨z∨¬v4), (¬t∨z∨¬v5).

Add the prime cover clauses of path e5,e1,e2 guarded with s, and the prime cover clauses of path e1,e2,e3 guarded with t. Each path has max minimal cover size two, so each guarded clause has width at most three. The four slices of the displayed clauses give precisely the claimed legal fibers.

For full-PC inspection, fixed s,t leave exact flat prime-clause formulas on the visible variables. With s=0 and t unassigned, the path covers propagate every implied visible literal. The two-edge group is blocked if v2=1 or both v1 and v3 are 1, which forces t=1 by a displayed clause. The e5 alternative blocks at either endpoint, forcing t=0. Reflect for t=0. With s=1, the two possible no-z edge witnesses meet at v4, and z∨¬v4 explicitly handles that common consequence. A blocker of e3 forces t=1. Losing the e4-or-z alternative forces t=0 via its endpoint v5 when z=0, or leads straight to a clash on the common v4. Reflect for t=1.

With neither bit fixed, a blocker of the s=0 or t=0 slice is seen by its guarded covers. To force s=0, e3 and e4 must both be blocked and z=0. If v4=1 the shared clause gives it immediately. Otherwise both v3 and v5 are true, so t=0 first and then s=0 by the e3 guard. Reflect for t=0. Every pair of incident graph edges shares a bit value in the code table. Thus forcing a visible vertex false at z=0 implies a bit value and reduces to a previously checked slice. A missing feasible code forces a clash by the slice tests. If z is free and all edges blocked, s=t=1 follow and z is then forced. This covers presets and consequences of both auxiliary variables.

2. Width-three full PC on C_6 uses exactly three auxiliaries

The two-bit impossibility is the earlier C6 addendum. For the upper bound subdivide e1 of the C5 construction to get cycle order 1,x,2,3,4,5. Keep 10→34, 01→51, and 11→45 or z. Let 00 now mean the path group G with edges 1x,x2,23. Introduce u↔(s=t=0) by two binary clauses and a ternary. The group has minimal cover pairs {x,2}, {1,2}, {x,3}; guard their negative clauses by ¬u. Keep the 10/01 edge guards and the two 11 shared clauses from above. Add shared slice guards (s∨¬v1∨¬v2) and (t∨¬v_x∨¬v3). Add four ternary clauses saying that u=0 in the s=0 slice requires the e51 endpoints, and u=0 in the t=0 slice requires the e34 endpoints, to be zero. For example the former clauses are (u∨s∨¬v1) and (u∨s∨¬v5).

A blocked 00 group propagates ¬u from its cover clauses. An unavailable zero slice then forces the corresponding bit through its blocked single-edge alternative. With one bit fixed to zero and both alternatives feasible, the only possible shared visible consequence comes from the common endpoint, v1 in the s=0 slice or v3 in the t=0 slice. For instance if every surviving group edge uses v1, 1x is viable, so v_x is free or zero. Blocking x2 then requires v2=1 and the shared slice guard forces ¬v1. The reflected case uses the other guard. If either alternative blocks, derive the other bit and reduce to a fixed fiber. The 1-slice arguments are unchanged. With neither bit known, the code pairs at each vertex still share a bit, so forced visible consequences reduce to a slice. The gate forces u=1 when both bits are zero and u=0 when either is one; a group blockage forces u=0 directly. If u=0 is preset the two zero slices simply have their singleton alternatives, explicitly covered by the new guards. If u=1 is preset the gate gives the PC path-group fiber. The z consequence is proved as for C5. All clauses retain width three.

3. URC on K_(p,3) also has an exact additive count

For K_(p,q), p≥q≥3, saturation at p−2 URC auxiliaries is impossible. Use the p-side cover and a complete visible conflict assignment with the other side zero. The unique code at a selected private facet now has the following consequence. Fix z=0 and exactly one p-side vertex to zero. The saturated reason tree only uses z and p-side facet literals, so every legal assignment to the q-side has the same auxiliary code. After fixing the code and the p-side, the residual CNF on the q-side must represent a single clause containing all q negative literals. If q>3, width three cannot do this. If q=3, a residual clause of width three containing exactly those literals must already have appeared in the original width-three formula, contradicting the fact that z=1 permits the q-side to be all true. Hence k_URC≥p−1. For q=3 the separate group chains attain p−1. This proves exact URC as well as exact PC for K_(p,3). For larger q the general URC gap remains open.

The finite rows C5 and C6 are now small, checkable examples to include next to the general linear cycle-PC construction. In each, display the code table and the steps for auxiliary consequences under partial assignments. The exact general PC minimum remains open. No tests or project code were run.

