One generalization reveals a wall. An affine map T(x)=M x+t is polynomially checkable as a candidate: verify by linear feasibility that all source points land in V_ε, and that no source point satisfying any given literal of C can map to an all-false assignment of that C. But deciding if some clause-preserving affine map exists is NP-complete even with width-three clauses. Reduce an arbitrary CNF H to width-three gate definitions D for h=H(x), and add (¬p∨h). A known source point exists with p=0 and consistent gates. Every feasible map to p=1 sends it to a model of H. Conversely if H is satisfiable, a constant map to a verified model with p=1 preserves all clauses on every source point. The matrices and their verification are polynomial in input size. The restriction to translations is doing real work in the algorithm.

