For trees, minimum crossing matching width equals pathwidth (vertex separation convention). Put k equal to the minimum crossing matching width. No vertex can have three components of T−v each of matching width ≥k. In an order of width k, choose a k-matching at a cut of each component; at the middle cut there is a path through v from a processed vertex of the earliest component to an unprocessed vertex of the latest. A crossing edge of that path is disjoint from the k-matching in the middle component. Mark each direction toward a component of width at least k. Bidirectionally marked edges form a path if any exist: all intervening edges are bidirectional by inclusion of high components and no vertex has three high directions. Off-path components are low. In the absence of a bidirectional edge a sink has no high direction and serves as a singleton spine. Order the spine from one end, then each vertex’s off-path components contiguously before the next spine vertex. Inductively their own frontier is ≤k−1, with at most one additional spine vertex active. This proves pathwidth≤k; the reverse inequality counts the distinct left endpoints of a crossing matching. Consequently the optimized forest peak is ≤4 exactly when pathwidth≤2.

