Two lower bounds from the landed relay check. If no selector occurs before z, the projected residual depends on each next x bit and distinct prefix sets have distinct residuals. All original continuation roots before x_j must be at that x label, since skipping it cannot reintroduce the dependency through future quantification. The same reasoning at z gives 512 distinct z nodes. Distinct nonempty x sets give 511 distinct nonterminal continuations after z=0. Thus there are at least 3·2^9−2=1534 nodes even allowing later selectors. With a single selector after r x labels and before z, the first r labels contribute at least 2^r−1. There are 2^r distinct nonterminal roots following those labels. This gives 2^(r+1)−1, excluding r≥6 at size at most 109.

The key refinement is to locate those further roots. Each projected residual after the first r x labels still depends on the next x bit x_(r+1); use a suffix with that own y coordinate 1 and other y coordinates and z zero. A continuation whose root follows that bit has no way to depend on it. The only labels available between x_r and x_(r+1), inclusive at the right, are the selector and x_(r+1). Hence at r=5, at least 31 nodes precede the selector and at least 32 distinct nodes belong to the selector/x6 layer. They are disjoint from x7 through y9.

