Count S selector nodes, q actual continuation roots after selectors (include terminal zero if reachable), X and Y first- and second-half visible nodes, and Z override nodes. The selector prefix is a rooted DAG with at most 2S arcs, so S≥q−1. At x all ones, every x label with just one node must be visited on an accepting y=e_i,z=0 branch. One root cannot serve two such unique x labels i<j on that row. If it did, set x_i=0 and retain all other x bits. The unique x_j node must still be reached for y=e_j. The tail at x_j, now fed y=e_i, would reproduce the old accepting path even though x_i is zero. Thus u_x≤q and X≥2m−q.

Count unique y labels similarly. A unique y_i node has its low child zero: reach it on an x=e_i,z=0 witness for y=e_i and set y_i=0, varying the later y bits freely, to see that the low child is identically zero. On x all ones a continuation cannot supply two unique singleton probes. It must test the earlier y_i to accept its own singleton, and feeding a later singleton hits its zero low child. Hence u_y≤q and Y≥2m−q. This also corrects a sentence in the earlier 28 relay. A particular branch need not compute exactly y_i on all y assignments. The single accepted singleton support and the unique-node low argument are sufficient.

If Z=1 its low child is zero, using x=0,y=0,z=1 acceptance and x=0,z=0 rejection for every y. More generally at least one z node has low child zero for that reason. With Z=1, every x row has at least one branch visiting it, because y=0 needs the override. This branch supplies no singleton witness at z=0 and, on x all ones, it cannot go through a unique x node. Thus both unique-label bounds improve to q−1. For Z≥2 combining S,X,Y gives total≥4m−q+1. For Z=1 the improved bound is 4m−q+2.

For upper bounds partition into h disjoint blocks, as equal as possible, and give each block its own selector leaf. A block of size a computing its edge OR has 2^a−1 x nodes and the same y count. A designated block with z additionally uses 2^a z nodes. When m≤2h−1 a singleton block is available to tag, all remaining blocks having at most two pairs. Counting the selector tree gives 4m−h+1. When m=2h, all blocks have size two so the tag costs four z nodes, giving 4m−h+3. The remainder proves the latter is best possible.

Suffix supports and rigidity. At x-prefix set A,z=0 the union of ≤q actual suffix singleton-support sets must be A. These supports are closed under dropping their earliest labels, by following a low edge and skipping labels with zero contribution. More formally normalize each zero-rejecting suffix node to the OR of its singleton probes; low children recursively implement tail supports and high is terminal one when the label belongs to support. Already-present zero-rejecting branches suffice for correctness. There is at most one new node per old node, and each distinct nonempty support costs a node. Every singleton is required. The graph of actual two-element supports has independence number ≤q because a (q+1)-set cannot be covered by q singletons and any larger support brings its own tail pair. By Turán on the complement its minimum number of edges e_T is the sum of binom(a,2) over balanced q part sizes. Moreover when the q supports cover all m labels, at most 2q distinct labels are among their final two positions. Each remaining label is the smallest of a distinct support of size≥3, by tail closure. Thus

