This suffix bound is exact throughout q≤m≤3q as a suffix-only problem by using all subsets of balanced blocks of sizes 1/2 or 2/3. When m=2q and Y=3q the actual nonsingleton supports are exactly a perfect matching by Turán equality. Then Z≥4. To see this, choose independently a nonempty selection from each pair for the x row. Each pair consumes one branch, so a branch providing z must have a nonzero low support equal to its entire chosen pair subset. Every pair offers three subset choices. If there were at most two available nonzero tagged supports, avoid them independently. There is already one tag with low zero. Thus three total z nodes cannot suffice.

To finish m=2h, assume a circuit of size≤4m−h+2. If q≤h−2 the generic bound excludes it. If q=h−1, Z=1 excludes it too, and Z≥2 would require Y=2m−q. The suffix bound is stronger since m>2q. At q=h, Z≥4 is too large. Z=3 requires the rigid Y=3q case and hence Z≥4. With Z=1 every row has at most q−1 witness-producing slots; the suffix bound with that smaller budget excludes the necessary Y≤3q+1. With Z=2 there is one distinguished low-zero node and at most one tag with nonempty singleton support. Fix x_j=y_j=0 for a j in that support, or choose any j if it is empty. On those x rows a branch may not use a tag with j in its low support, by projection soundness, so one branch slot is spent on the zero-support override. Removing the pair leaves 2q−1 pairs and q−1 witness slots. The suffix bound now gives at least 3q+1 y nodes for q≥2 (including q=2). At least one y_j node was removed and the assumed original budget allowed at most 3q+1 y nodes, contradiction. If q=1 the same restriction leaves an essential pair with no witness slot. This completes the boundary.

