Prove first for m=2q+1. If there are at most q−2 anchors, simple counting proves the bound. If there are q anchors, every root is an owner on the all-anchor rows. With at most one extra x node beyond 2m−q either every duplicate has one inside occurrence or every duplicate has one outside occurrence. In the first case each duplicate must be covered by its sole owner on every all-anchor row where that duplicate is one. An owner cannot handle two such duplicates d<e: compare rows differing only in d while e stays one. Both must reach the same inside e node for the y_e witness, and changing only this earlier d bit cannot remove the accepting y_d suffix from that node. This would leak y_d. Thus at most q duplicates, fewer than m−q. In the second case restrict all anchors to zero. All m−q=q+1 duplicate labels now have a unique available x node. They need more than q roots. Both cases contradict the budget.

