Quadruple coverage is <=a4+6a3+D. Thus (1,16,7,5) gives <=5+42+78=125, and (3,15,9,2) gives <=2+54+69=125, both below 126.

AN EIGHT-TRIPLE LEMMA. Any eight distinct triples on nine vertices are jointly contained in at most 106 distinct 5-sets.
There are 8*15=120 triple-in-five-set incidences. Let c_Q be the number of selected triples in a 5-set Q, and R=sum_Q max(c_Q-1,0); distinct covered 5-sets=120-R. Eight triples have 24 vertex incidences. Balancing vertex degrees gives sum_v C(deg v,2)>=21. For each pair of triples, its contribution to sum_v C(deg v,2) is its intersection size; its number of common containing 5-sets is >= that intersection size (1 if intersection 1, 5 if intersection 2). Thus P=sum_Q C(c_Q,2)>=21.
- If every c_Q<=3, C(c_Q,2)<=3/2*(c_Q-1) for c_Q>=1, hence R>=ceil(2*21/3)=14.
- If some 5-set Q contains c>=4 selected triples, for each of Q's five 4-subsets U let r_U be the number of those triples in U. Each triple belongs to exactly two U, so sum_U r_U=2c, hence sum_U max(r_U-1,0)>=2c-5. Each U plus one of the four outside-Q vertices is a distinct 5-set, contributing at least r_U-1 redundancy; Q itself contributes c-1. Therefore R>=4(2c-5)+(c-1)=9c-21>=15.
Either way R>=14, so coverage<=106.
For (2,16,8,3), all 5-sets are covered by a selected triple, a selected 4-set, or a selected high 5-set; the latter two contribute at most 3*5+2. Total <=106+15+2=123<126. For (3,16,8,2), total <=106+10+3=119<126. They are impossible.

FINAL PROFILE (2,15,9,3). The integer D<=69 bound gives quadruple coverage <=3+9*6+69=126; equality would require every disjoint selected-edge pair to give a DIFFERENT 4-set. Thus the 9-vertex 15-edge selected-pair graph must be C4-free. But degree balancing gives sum_v C(deg v,2)>=36, while C4-free means every vertex pair has at most one common neighbor, hence sum<=C(9,2)=36. Equality forces degree multiset (4,4,4,3,3,3,3,3,3) and exactly one common neighbor for EVERY vertex pair. For each vertex v, sum_{u adjacent v}(deg u-1)=8. Thus each degree-4 vertex has four degree-3 neighbors; each degree-3 vertex has exactly two degree-4 neighbors. Those six degree-3 vertices supply six common-neighbor incidences among only C(3,2)=3 pairs of degree-4 vertices, contradicting exactly one per pair. Therefore a C4 exists, causing at least one duplicate disjoint-matching quadruple; at most 68 distinct pair-generated quadruples, and 3+54+68=125<126.

All five profiles contradict coverage, so b=38 is impossible and b>=39 follows, contingent on all stated arguments. Smaller b can be padded with distinct nonempty subsets to 38 without losing the 2-basis property.

