Let B be a two-basis of P([10]), b=|B|, and let a_{>=j} count members of size at least j. All ten singletons are forced.

Counting targets:
- There are C(10,5)=252 five-sets. Each needs a basis component of size >=3, and one component of size >=3 lies in at most C(7,2)=21 five-sets. Thus a_{>=3}>=12.
- There are C(10,7)=120 seven-sets. Each needs a component size >=4, each covering at most C(6,3)=20 such targets; hence a_{>=4}>=6.
- There are C(10,9)=10 nine-sets. Each needs a component size >=5, each covering at most C(5,4)=5 such targets; hence a_{>=5}>=2.

Let I=sum_{T in B}|T| count point-set incidences. Since the ten singleton sets are in B and all others have size at least two,

I >= 2b-10+a_{>=3}+a_{>=4}+a_{>=5} >=2b+10.

For every point i, the members of B that AVOID i form a valid 9-point two-basis: any set not containing i cannot be represented using a set that contains i. The candidate 9-point bound gives at least 39 members avoiding i. Thus at most b-39 members contain i, and summing over ten points gives

I <= 10(b-39).

Combine: 2b+10 <=10b-390, hence b>=50.

