Monotone shared-variable gluing. Suppose two full-PC factors share one Boolean selector s and any number of visible variables W that occur only negatively in both factors. All other variables are private. Their conjunction is URC and unit propagation derives every forced literal outside W. To see this, saturate UP. Setting all still-free W to zero relaxes both factors. Neither local factor can then be inconsistent without its own PC already detecting it under the original partial assignment. The feasible domains of s must intersect: if opposed singletons, local PC would already propagate the conflict. A model on each side with a common value of s combines after this same shared W assignment.

For each private literal not yet forced, take its opposite in a local model. After setting free W=0, its s value can be matched by the other side, whose s domain is both values unless s is fixed at saturation. Thus the opposite extends globally. A free w in W can fail only in the negative direction. If fixing w=1 prevents every global model, after fixing the other free shared bits to zero either one local domain is empty (which full PC would already catch) or the two s domains are opposite singletons. This localizes the missed implication to a pair of opposing selector requirements. It also gives a sufficient width-three bridge: if these requirements are witnessed by single residual clauses (¬w∨s) and (¬w∨¬s), each source clause of width at most three with at most one other already-false literal, resolve on s. The common ¬w means the resolvent stays width≤3, and it propagates the missing literal. The condition on single residual clauses matters; a multi-step derivation need not yield a width-three bridge.

Standalone binary override lemma. For a nonconstant downward visible predicate P, among width-w fully PC encodings unate in visible vertices, requiring the only positive z occurrence to be (z∨a) with a an auxiliary literal costs exactly one additional auxiliary. Upper: replace the old z by a fresh p and add z∨¬p, preserving PC by singleton overlap. Lower: eliminate z by Davis–Putnam. Resolving the one positive binary with every negative-z clause preserves width and PC. Interpret the former auxiliary literal ¬a as the new guard. On a=1 any former z=1 model can lower z to zero, since the positive z clause remains satisfied and negative z occurrences relax. Projection on that side is exactly P. On a=0 there is a model at the all-blocked visible row with z=1, since there P fails. Visible unateness lets the same auxiliary assignment extend to every less-blocked row. Thus the resulting guard relation has one fewer auxiliary. The companion being an auxiliary and the one-positive-occurrence hypotheses are substantive.

P10 local architecture exclusion. In the compressed interval approach with its four retained interval bits, fix the two later flags and the later visible values as in the interval reduction. One resulting local projected relation uses visible state bits α,β and x1…x4. Its fibers are z1/00 unrestricted; z0/00 requires e1∨e2∨e3; z0/10 requires x4; z0/11 unrestricted, with the other states disallowed. Here x_i=1 means usable and e_i=x_i x_(i+1). This small relation itself has no width-three fully PC encoding with one extra bit p.

Under z=0,β=0,x2=0, the sole new visible consequence is x4=1, and all three premises are essential. The 00 fiber needs edge e3, the 10 fiber needs x4 directly. Unit propagation must therefore first derive a polarity of p from two premises, then x4 from p and the remaining premise. Orient p true on this derivation. The three possible required pairs of clauses are

    (z∨x2∨p), (β∨¬p∨x4)
    (z∨β∨p), (x2∨¬p∨x4)
    (β∨x2∨p), (z∨¬p∨x4).

Take the forbidden row z0/00,x2=1,x1=x3=x4=0. Flipping x1 or x3 makes it legal. In the first or third case, the second clause forces p=0 in all three rows. Thus any clause rejecting the forbidden full assignment must include x1 and x3. In case one, the legal bypass z1/00 with the same x values forces p=0 and requires the third literal to be z. That rejects the legal later z0/11 row. In case three, legal z0/11 forces p=0 and requires α or β in the third position, which rejects legal bypass z1/00. In case two, the first clause forces p=1 on the forbidden row and both flips, so the same x1,x3 pair is mandatory. Both legal bypass and legal later rows could satisfy a third literal only by sharing ¬p across those alternative visible changes. But z0/10 with x4=1 and x1=x3=0 is legal and forces p=1, so it violates that clause. No case works.

