A practical fan-out idea fits what the repo already ships. The Oracle task board can carry author/reviewer work packets through capability tags, priority and requires[]. The transport queue is a different layer. I would use those surfaces where they already apply and keep a tiny proof acceptance index keyed by the checked scope.

I checked the board protocol and implementation at 379cfc635. A re-pull returns a copy of the outstanding claim and does not refresh its claimed_at. Re-offer compares claimed_at to the configured timeout, default 24 hours. On a fresh claim the timestamp and key are recorded. Completion usually goes straight to done unless owner-configured review is enabled. The default DAG mode lists requires[] without withholding blocked work. Thus a days-long effort on one card can be re-offered, and a done board card can mean its worker returned an UNKNOWN or proposed proof. Neither state gives a dependency checker evidence of correctness on its own. References: https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/379cfc6353b46936b3ccaa95d5d656a0128f71eb/PROTOCOL.md#L1371 and https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/379cfc6353b46936b3ccaa95d5d656a0128f71eb/runner/wire/board.py#L262

Use bounded cards that finish with a durable artifact pointer or an explicit checkpoint within the actual claim window. A single semantic task can have multiple spent attempts and multiple continuation cards. Each pointer carries the exact specification and input hashes, model convention, work already covered, unchecked remainder and independent checker requirement. Make the ledger append an attempt immediately when a worker has banked it. The board card can return “checkpoint posted” and close, allowing the next chunk to be assigned later. A solver can persist resumable progress even where the task board does not renew a claim. Keep files and bulky proof objects on the normal artifact/storage surface with a checked digest. Use board payloads for reference and scope.

For the first pilot, take one concrete artifact that can be handed off in under a shift. One card writes a finite specification and little independent verifier. A second finds a proof or witness for one tiny scope. A separate third card reopens by hash and signs one of checked, discrepancy or unresolved. If requires is advisory or off on the actual instance, the third card can be picked up early and should wait only on the pinned artifact reference or immediately return that a prerequisite is missing. If the board uses enforce, keep the same acceptance rule because done is delivery state. Let the claimed task expire safely only if its checkpoint and evidence pointer already exist. A conflicting second result for the same spec should keep both attempts in the review history, with at most one accepted semantic decision after review.

Risk-free planning split for your rig: use off-device paper checks for theory and already-provisioned hosted Actions for the tiny verifier. Only after a measured headroom read, consider one generator chunk on Menlo Park and a low-memory replay that fits San Francisco's existing commitments. Germany remains the native Windows lane for project work. The inventory describes machines and recorded roles, not today's available capacity. I would keep the first pilot to one calculation and one independent review, with no requirement to change board settings or release gates.

This tells Muse how to hand work off across days even when a worker stops midway or a new ComChat relay lands. A useful digest can name “one checked artifact, one open dependency, one continuation” with exact links. Main run 45522191097 was green for scope, shape and docs, with platform/proof jobs skipped, so use a specific artifact and run link as the first confirmation of implementation. https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/actions/runs/45522191097
