1. Prefix lower bound, without assuming closure.

Use the existing rank layers P(v)∪{i} at x_i. Their d-subsets hit every ((d−1)h+1)-set. Count 4h+1 singletons, ≥6h+4 pairs by Turán, ≥4h+4 triples, ≥h+3 fours, and ≥1 higher-layer node. The last uses the existing cumulative count. For triples, τ≥2h+1 and 4τ≤n+t by Chvátal–McDiarmid. If the smallest possible t=4h+3 were attained, t>n=4h+1 implies degree≥4 at some v. Taking v into the transversal and applying the same bound to the remaining edges decreases its numerator by at least one after the cost of v. Contradiction. The four bound uses 6τ≤n+2e for the four-uniform layer and τ≥h+1. This gives X≥15h+13 using only rank-layer coverage, no tail-closure assumption on the prefix.

2. The marked suffix gives ≥15h+28.

It is an ordered-tail h-basis on n=4h+2, with ≥6h+8 pairs, ≥h+5 fours and ≥2 high members. For the last count, a ≥6-member has a distinct size-five tail; with at most one high member of size five, h sets cover at most 4h+1 points. Its triple bound improves from 4h+6 to 4h+8. To prove that, τ≥2h+2. At t=4h+6, degree≥4 and the single-vertex argument forbids equality. At t=4h+7, degree≥5 would suffice after one deletion. Otherwise maximum degree is four, and 3t−3n=15 forces at least 15 vertices of degree four. Such a vertex shares a triple with at most eight different vertices. Pick two degree-four vertices sharing no triple. Taking both into the transversal and deleting their eight incident edges gives 4τ≤n+t−2<8h+8. This excludes the other possibility. Thus the suffix sum before pair excess is at least 15h+25. Pair excess p≥3 already gives the desired 15h+28.

For p≤2, Brouwer stability partitions the pair graph into h internal cliques, since its complement is K_(h+1)-free and within two edges of Turán. Let d be the imbalance excess in internal pair count, and p−d the number of cross edges. Balanced has two size-five blocks, others size four. Write b_i for sizes, so d=(Σ(b_i−4)²−2)/2.

Use the same missing-pure-triple argument as at m=4h. Add one independently chosen representative per outside part to each missing pure triple T. Trim pools to prevent cross pairs. A target cannot be covered by h supports of size at most two, since its three points in the base save at most one support and all others are isolated in the target pair graph. It must contain a triple support, including the tail of a larger support if used. Some outside pool must be exhausted by cross triples using two points of T. A cross triple charges at most b_i−2 missing pure T in its unique two-point base.

At p=0 the pure layer has 4h+12 triples and the charge from pools of at least four recovers any missing ones. At p=1, d=0 gives one cross edge; pools of at least three recover the pure capacity. For d=1 with no cross edge, the non-four sizes are (6) or (3,5,5,5), with pure capacities 4h+16 and 4h+15 respectively. The same charge suffices for each. At p=2 we only need t≥4h+9. For d=2 and no cross edges, the non-four sizes are (3,5,6) or (3,3,5,5,5,5); capacities are 4h+19 and 4h+18, and a size-six block can lose at most five net from 20 triples when three incidences are charged per missing one. At d=1 one cross edge, the (6) profile keeps t≥4h+11 by that same loss bound. In the (3,5,5,5) profile, a size-five base can have a pool of size two only from a cross edge into the size-three block. If the edge is wholly outside the base, trim the larger endpoint's pool when needed. At most one size-five base can lose, and can lose at most three net from its ten triples under the two-vs-three charge. Either profile suffices.

At d=0 with two cross edges, size-four bases need only two choices to recover each missing triple. A size-five base needs a pool of size two only when both edges from T hit distinct vertices in one outside size-four class. To see this, exclude the outside endpoints of edges incident with T, and for any edge wholly outside the base choose the endpoint so as not to make a second deletion in a part if possible. With two edges the only forced double deletion is the stated case. At most one of the two size-five bases can have that exception. It can lose at most three net from its ten pure triples under the two-vs-three charge. So balanced p=2 retains at least 4h+9 as well. These bounds yield suffix ≥15h+28 in every case. Summing gives (h−1)+(15h+13)+(15h+28)=31h+40.

