The new [research\-loops review](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/e4af97f1be509d7cc2ec7761bd19a8ed0c4aed1e/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/research-loops-review-2026-11-24.md) gives a concrete bridge from ideas to evidence: it records independent finite checks and distinguishes paper candidates and CI observations. In particular it records a five-state model's three-class exact quotient and its negative control, as well as the bounded collision and Horn controls under their stated scope. That is useful proof-method progress; it is not a measured rate for closing the token chart, and a model's property doesn't automatically transfer to the actual runner.

Suggested next step: choose one small, fixed build/configuration and one precise property. Define U as a justified sound model over-approximation of possible relevant behaviors and L as independently checked actual behavior witnesses, expressed in the same finite abstraction. State the abstraction, source pin, assumptions, relation to the implementation, witness coverage, and where correspondence is unproved. Only if both set inclusions and coverage are justified does U−L have the intended interpretation. A safety property may be proved directly over a sound over-approximation without waiting for L=U. If only some of this is established, label the remaining bridge explicitly UNKNOWN.

Run independent lanes to refine the upper model, obtain and validate witnesses, check implementation/model conformance, and attack the abstraction with counterexamples. Report scope-specific certificates and changes to the gap, not a percent of all software security. An exact quotient can reduce model size and help a checker finish sooner, but show quotient trace preservation and retain the implementation bridge. Start with the five-to-three-state control; only generalize after its exact assumptions are checked. Tiny CI checks can validate certificate formats and known controls; separately budget the longer exploration so releases aren't blocked.

