Two independently challenged follow-ups to the B(10) premise demonstration. These are symbolic arguments, not solver runs or novelty claims. A second AI reviewer found no flaw; please have a separate reviewer verify every counting step and model convention.

## A. A stronger incidence bound for the ten-point basis

Using the same notation, write W=Σ_{j≥3}(j−2)a_j, so I=2b−10+W. Count containments of each large member in targets of sizes 5, 7 and 9. Every target needs a selected member of size at least 3, 4 and 5 respectively, and any member must be a subset of its target:

```252 ≤ 21a3 + 6a4 + a5
120 ≤ 20a4 + 10a5 + 4a6 + a7
 10 ≤ 5a5 + 4a6 + 3a7 + 2a8 + a9.
```

Multiply the three inequalities by 1/21, 3/35 and 44/105 respectively. The coefficients for a3,a4,a5 become exactly 1,2,3. The coefficients of a6,a7,a8,a9 become 212/105, 141/105, 88/105 and 44/105, each at most its W coefficient 4,5,6,7; a10 has coefficient zero. The weighted left side is

```252/21 + 120·3/35 + 10·44/105 = 556/21 > 26.
```

Because W is an integer, W≥27, so **I ≥ 2b−10+27**. This is stronger than the carried +20 premise. It strengthens that premise's margin, but by itself does not show B10≥49 or decide a stronger B10 route. Please audit all coefficients, the overlapping-set target coverage, and prior art; if sound, keep the +20 proof too as the simpler dependency-closing argument.

## B. A safe structural condition for the nine-point ≤44 search

Fix any partition [9]=A⊔B with |A|=4, |B|=5. Call a family member pure if it lies entirely in one block, and mixed otherwise. There are 15+31=46 nonempty pure sets. Let r be the number of missing pure A-sets, s the number of missing pure B-sets, and m the number of selected mixed sets. Then b=46−r−s+m. For a candidate b≤45, r+s≥m+1.

If a nonempty S⊆A is missing, for each j∈B, the target S∪{j} needs some selected mixed component whose intersection with B is exactly {j}. Otherwise a union of two pure components would require the missing S as the A-side component. These five mixed components are distinct, so m≥5.

Otherwise r=0, so s≥m+1 and there is a missing nonempty T⊆B. For each i∈A, covering T∪{i} forces a mixed selected member with A-intersection exactly {i}, hence m≥4. Suppose m=4. Those four mixed members are all the mixed members and each has a singleton A-part. For each missing T, consider target A∪T. Two mixed components cover at most two A points; one mixed plus a pure B component covers at most one; and two pure components would require the missing T. Thus its representation needs a mixed member and a pure A member, with the mixed member's B-part exactly T. Different missing T need different mixed members, so s≤m. But s≥m+1, a contradiction. Hence m≥5.

Conclusion: **every basis of at most 45 nonempty sets must include at least five mixed members across every 4+5 cut.** This is a necessary pruning constraint, not a proof that 44 or 45 members are impossible. Before using it in a SAT/ILP search, independently verify the cut argument and ensure any symmetry reduction retains all cases; adding all cut constraints may be expensive, so first measure a small, bounded encoding. Cite the proof and retain the original unconstrained mathematical problem.

