Here is the paper lane Andrew authorized. The previous review left Candidate C padding as a conditional shape waiting for an exact encoding. I pinned a transparent codec and wrote the transfer with both budget constants explicit. It is a scoped paper lemma, pending the independent interpreter/encoding check below.

Let E(F) be a fixed efficient canonical encoding of a SAT formula and use legal inputs x=0^q 1 E(F), q≥0. The parser removes zeros up to the delimiter and decodes E(F). Use the same canonical active-variable witness representation for every padding of a given formula. For legal x of length n and N≥n, Pad(x,N)=0^(N−n)x is a legal exact-length string, with the same witnesses and a polynomial-time validator. Define A_U(c,d) using length cap floor(c log₂(n+2)) and clock (n+2)^d as in Candidate C. U must support a compiler for a fixed wrapper with O(payload length + 1) description cost and polynomial simulation overhead. Just semantic universality, without the description or time bound, does not license this lemma.

Theorem. For fixed weak parameters c,d≥0 and fixed integer r≥1, there are fixed C,D with the following property. For every legal satisfiable input x of length n, if A_U(C,D) is proved to fail on x, then A_U(c,d) fails on Pad(x,N) for each n≤N≤(n+2)^r−2.

Proof. Suppose p succeeds on one of these padded inputs at the weak budget. On input x, a fixed wrapper carries N, the length of p, and p as a self-delimiting payload. It constructs Pad(x,N), simulates U(p,Pad(x,N)), and returns the same verified witness. A code of length O(log(N+2)) suffices for N. Framing p contributes O(log(|p|+2)). Total pre-compiler size is |p|+O(log(N+2)+log(|p|+2))=O(log(n+2)), using |p|≤c log₂(N+2) and the window. The linear-length compiler keeps that within C log₂(n+2) for a fixed C. The wrapper materializes N symbols, then simulates at most (N+2)^d steps for this successful p and validates its output. With fixed polynomial overhead that all fits in (n+2)^D for a fixed D. Constants can be absorbed by increasing C,D. Neither parameter needs to vary by input. The purported stronger failure is contradicted. The proof establishes only the indicated direction because the wrapper pays both description and time.

Corollary A. Under P≠NP, this yields arbitrarily large windows of legal lengths containing a weak-budget defeat at every length in the window. A bounded-length set of satisfiable misses of the stronger fixed procedure could be patched by a finite table and give a polynomial solver. Each large stronger failure then witnesses a whole window via the proof. The failure may be a different padded input at each length. The lemma yields no defeat against a larger budget from a smaller one.

Corollary B. If every sufficiently large window has at least one fully passing legal length for the weak pair, SAT is in P. On any x, try the weak algorithm on all polynomially many lengths in its window, validating each output. A satisfiable formula gets a witness at the fully passing length, and an unsatisfiable one never does. A finite lower range can be handled separately. Finding out which length is fully good is unnecessary.

