## Notation and previously sent ingredients

Let b=|F|, p=a2 be the number of selected pairs, G their graph, S=Σ_v C(deg(v),2), t its triangle count, and D=S−t. Ten singleton sets are forced. Let q=a3, and let A4 denote the number of selected members of size at least four. From the weighted incidence note, W=Σ_{j≥3}(j−2)a_j≥27, so I−10=2b−20+W≥2b+7.

Each triple with no selected pair is forced as a family member. Inclusion-exclusion gives q≥120−8p+D. Also A4≥7: the 120 seven-element targets each need a component of size at least four, and such a component lies in at most C(6,3)=20 seven-targets. If only six such components existed, equality would force all six to be four-sets with disjoint families of seven-targets. Any two intersecting four-sets have union of size at most seven and so lie together in a seven-target; the six would have to be pairwise disjoint, impossible on ten points. Thus b≥10+p+q+A4 gives

```b ≥ 137 − 7p + D.
```

Every triangle contains three wedges, so 3t≤S and D≥2S/3. As C(d,2)≥2d−3 for each degree d, S≥4p−30. Hence b≥117−13p/3; b≤49 forces p≥16.

## Eliminate p=16

The degree sum is 32. Convexity gives S≥36, attained only by two vertices of degree four and eight of degree three. Thus D≥24, so b≥137−112+24=49. With b≤49, equality forces D=24. Then D≥2S/3 and S≥36 force S=36 and t=12=S/3.

Equality 3t=S means every wedge is closed, which implies each connected component of G is a clique. But a degree-four vertex would belong to a K5 and hence force at least five degree-four vertices, contradicting the degree multiset. Therefore p=16 is impossible.

## Eliminate p≥18

The degree sum is at least 36. Convexity gives S≥48 and hence D≥32. The previously sent four-class collision count gives

```1023 ≤ b(b+1)/2 − (I−10) − p − D − S.
```

If b≤49 and p≥18, substituting I−10≥2b+7 gives at most

```49·50/2 − (98+7) − 18 − 32 − 48
= 1225 − 105 − 98
= 1022,
```

a contradiction. The expression b(b+1)/2−2b−7 increases over the relevant range, so the b=49 substitution is the largest possible.

## What remains for p=17

This leaves p=17. If the prior ≥49 argument is valid, b=49. Degree sum 34 gives S≥42; b≥137−119+D gives D≤31; and D≥2S/3 gives S≤46. Thus

```42 ≤ S ≤ 46; 28 ≤ D ≤ 31; t=S−D.
```

Since b=49 and p=17, q+A4=22. The forced edge-free triples give q≥120−136+D=D−16, while A4≥7 gives q≤15; in particular 12≤q≤15. A5≥2 from the nine-target count. Consequently

```W ≥ q + 2A4 + A5 ≥ q + 2(22−q) + 2 = 46−q ≥31.
