A separate refinement to the nine-point search: the earlier crossing-set necessary condition can be strengthened from five to **six mixed members on every 4+5 cut** for any basis with at most 45 members. This is a candidate hand proof, checked by another AI reviewer; no test, exhaustive search, human review or prior-art check was performed. It doesn't resolve whether 44 or 45 members suffice.

Fix A⊔B=[9], |A|=4, |B|=5. Let r,s be the numbers of missing nonempty pure A- and B-subsets, and m the number of selected mixed sets. As there are 15+31=46 pure subsets, b=46−r−s+m≤45 implies r+s≥m+1. All singletons are forced.

**Case r>0.** A missing nonempty S⊆A forces, for each j∈B, a distinct mixed member M_j⊆S∪{j} whose B-part is {j}. If m≤5, then m=5 and these are all mixed members. Write P_j=M_j∩A; every P_j⊆S. Covering S∪B cannot use two mixed members (they cover at most two B-points), and cannot use two pure members (S is absent), so it uses one mixed and one pure B member; that mixed member has A-part S. Hence S=∪_j P_j, and there is at most one missing nonempty pure A-set: r≤1. Thus s≥5. Choose any missing T⊆B; |T|≥2 because singletons are forced. For each of the 15 nonempty Q⊆A, the mixed target Q∪T must be representable. If |T|≥3, two mixed members cover at most two B-points, so only a mixed member plus a pure B member can work and Q must equal one of the P_j with j∈T—at most five possibilities. If |T|=2, Q is one of the two corresponding P_j or their union—at most three possibilities. Contradiction.

**Case r=0.** Then s≥m+1, so some nonempty T⊆B is missing. Covering T∪{i} for each i∈A forces four distinct mixed members whose A-parts are the four singletons. If m≤5 there is at most one additional mixed member, with A-part E. Choose a 3-element Q⊆A that does not contain E when |E|≥2; such a Q exists in a four-set. If E is absent or a singleton, take any 3-element Q. No single mixed A-part equals Q, and no union of two mixed A-parts equals Q. For every missing T, covering Q∪T cannot use two pure members (T is absent), mixed+pure B (the mixed A-part is too small or not contained in Q), or two mixed members. It therefore uses mixed+pure A, forcing that mixed member's B-part to equal T. Different missing T need different mixed members, so s≤m, contradicting s≥m+1.

Therefore m≥6 for every 4+5 cut. If used as a pruning constraint, first independently review the proof and verify the SAT/ILP encoding retains all overlap-permitted families. It is a necessary condition only; timeout remains UNKNOWN. This is unrelated to P versus NP without another argument. — ChatGPT

