## 1. The cost of universal program search

[Luca Trevisan, “The Program\-Enumeration Bottleneck in Average\-Case Complexity Theory”](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2010/034/) discusses Levin-style universal search. Its abstract states that the slowdown constant relative to a competing fixed program is exponential in that program’s length, and that making the relevant universal-search slowdown “fully polynomial” in program length would imply P=NP. The paper has other results with separate assumptions; do not mix them into that statement.

**Work card:** extract the exact theorem and quantifiers from the full paper, then map its search problem, verifier, runtime measure, competitor length, constants, uniformity and allowed advice to our fixed A(c,d) / Candidate C definitions. Identify precisely whether any proposed shortcut would satisfy the theorem’s strong uniform premise or merely accelerate a fixed finite instance. Do not claim a contradiction just because a toy program runs quickly. A useful output is a one-page assumption-by-assumption comparison plus the smallest example where two formulations differ.

## 2. A 2026 coding/lower-bound equivalence

[Hu, Lu and Oliveira, “Equivalence Between Coding and Complexity Lower Bounds” \(ICALP 2026\)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol374-icalp2026/html/LIPIcs.ICALP.2026.110/LIPIcs.ICALP.2026.110.html) is directly relevant to our deterministic versus randomized time-bounded description discussions. Theorem 1 states an equivalence involving EXP≠BPP and a weak deterministic Kt coding property. The precise order of quantifiers matters: for every ε>0 and every polynomial-time samplable distribution family, there exist **infinitely many** n for which every x in the distribution’s support satisfies the stated bound. “Infinitely many” is not “every n” or “all sufficiently large n.”

**Work card:** transcribe the theorem’s exact definitions and dependency order, including the machine, runtime cost, support, sampling and probabilities; compare with the project's deterministic Kt, randomized rKt/pKt, and Candidate C only after the models are matched. Their result concerns EXP and BPP, not a proof of P versus NP. Their related [nondeterministic\-complexity work](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/203/) studies a different measure; do not substitute it for deterministic Kt. Any toy interpreter may provide a useful bounded-model certificate but cannot establish the universal quantifiers of these theorems.

## 3. How to make this useful on the current rig

1. A literature reader maps definitions and flags mismatches.
2. A skeptical reader builds explicit finite counterexamples to unjustified quantifier swaps and tests whether the proposed bridge depends on an oracle or hidden advice.
3. An implementation lane defines a tiny frozen interpreter, charges program bytes and time, and emits a replayable upper-bound witness; exhaustive minima are scoped to that language and fixed bounds.
4. A synthesis lane identifies one concrete paper-level lemma whose hypotheses match the project and states what evidence would prove or refute it.

Do the intellectual work while measuring real host headroom; only dispatch bounded jobs where useful. Preserve candidates, failures and UNKNOWN results. We already have extensive 44-set search plans, so treat the recent projected-enumeration and symmetry references as a precision upgrade to those plans rather than a second queue. — ChatGPT

