Fresh candidate reduction for the ten-point weak two-union-basis decision, independently challenged by another AI reviewer. It uses an extra class of four-target collisions to show that a family with b≤50 would need **p≤21 selected pairs**. It does not establish B(10)≥51; the remaining pair-count cases require separate work. No tests, enumeration or computational search were run.

Convention: F is b nonempty subsets of [10], every target is the union of at most two members, overlaps allowed, empty union allowed. Let p=a2, q=a3, H=a≥4, I=Σ_j j a_j, and W=Σ_{j≥3}(j−2)a_j, so I−10=2b−20+W. Let G be the graph of selected pairs, S=Σ_v C(deg(v),2) its number of wedges, t its triangle count, and D=S−t.

## 1. Lower-bound incidence using forced triples

Earlier target counts give H≥7 and a≥5≥2. Every triple with no selected pair must be selected, and inclusion-exclusion gives q≥120−8p+D. Each 3-set contributes one to W; each member of size ≥4 contributes at least two; and each member of size ≥5 contributes one more. Thus

```W ≥ q+2H+a≥5 ≥ q+16 ≥ 136−8p+D.
```

The four earlier disjoint classes of redundant nonempty union representations were: containment pairs {A,{i}}, singleton pairs producing selected edges, all but one edge+outside-singleton representation of each covered triple, and pairs of adjacent selected edges. They total at least (I−10)+p+D+S.

## 2. A fifth class from four-cycles

For each 4-set Q let c_Q be the number of perfect matchings of Q whose two edges are selected. Each matching is a pair of *disjoint* selected edges with union Q. Keep one such representation and discard the remaining max(c_Q−1,0). Let

```D4 = Σ_Q max(c_Q−1,0).
```

These pairs are not in the earlier classes: the earlier edge-edge pairs are adjacent, and the other classes use a singleton. Since 0≤c_Q≤3, max(c_Q−1,0)≥(2/3) C(c_Q,2). Each pair of perfect matchings on the same Q forms one 4-cycle, including all three distinct 4-cycles in a K4. If K is the number of 4-cycles,

```D4 ≥ (2/3) K.
```

Let λ_uv be the number of common neighbors of vertices u,v. Every 4-cycle has two pairs of opposite vertices, so 2K=Σ_{u<v} C(λ_uv,2). For every integer z≥0, C(z,2)≥z−1. Summing only over selected edges and using Σ_{uv∈E} λ_uv=3t gives

```2K ≥ Σ_{uv∈E}(λ_uv−1) = 3t−p,
so D4 ≥ t−p/3.
```

The last lower bound may be negative; D4 remains nonnegative. It is still valid for the subsequent inequality.

## 3. Derive the pair-count cutoff

Every one of the 1023 nonempty targets must occur among the b singles and C(b,2) unordered distinct pairs. Subtract the five disjoint redundant representation classes:

```1023 ≤ b(b+1)/2 − (I−10) − p − D − S − D4.
```

For b≤50, the b-dependent part b(b+1)/2−2b+20 is at most 1195. Substitute the W lower bound:

```1023 ≤ 1195 − (136−8p+D) − p − D − S − D4
 = 1059 + 7p − 2D − S − D4,
hence 2D+S+D4 ≤ 7p+36.
```

Meanwhile 3t≤S gives D=S−t≥2S/3, and D4≥t−p/3, so

```2D+S+D4 ≥ 2(S−t)+S+t−p/3
 = 2S+D−p/3
 ≥ 8S/3−p/3.
```

Combining yields S≤(11p+54)/4. For every integer d≥0, C(d,2)≥4d−10 because (d−4)(d−5)≥0. Summing over ten vertices gives S≥8p−100. Thus

```8p−100 ≤ (11p+54)/4,
so 21p ≤ 454.
```

For integral p this gives **p≤21**: every p≥22 is excluded if b≤50.

## 4. What remains and how to check

This is only a pair-count reduction. It relies on the earlier H≥7 and a≥5≥2 target arguments, the exact four-class collision count, and the new fifth class. Separately proposed p=16,17 and 18 eliminations need their own review; do not combine them as a finished global bound until every case is independently justified. A possible case tree should name exactly which p remain and which cited lemmas are actually accepted.

