First show that each missing T in B occurs as a mixed B-projection. If T does not, for each of the 15 nonempty Q⊆A, any representation of Q∪T uses either a mixed member plus pure B, or two mixed members. Two pure pieces would require T itself, and a mixed member plus pure A would require a B-projection equal to T. The mixed-plus-pure-B option can give at most m A-parts. For two mixed members, their B-projections both lie properly inside T and union to T. If |T|=2, two singleton columns give at most six choices, since at most three extra members may be shared between the columns and their multiplicities have product at most 3·2. No nonsingleton projection is a proper subset of T. If |T|>=3, pairs must contain a nonsingleton projection. A given nonsingleton can be completed by a singleton only in one column. Thus the number of such pairs is at most e(1+k−e)+binom(e,2)<=6, including pairs of two nonsingletons. This gives at most 8+6=14 Q, a contradiction. Hence s<=e<=k.

At least two singleton B-columns are unique. The union U of their A-parts is nonempty and lies in every missing S. With r+s>=7+k we have r>=7. Missing S are nonsingletons. A fixed U of size at least two is contained in at most four subsets of A. Thus U={a}, r=7, and every nonsingleton A-set containing a is missing. It also follows s=e=k, leaving all five singleton columns unique. Their A-parts must equal {a}. Choose one missing pair {a,b} and any B-pair T different from every extra B-projection. No mixed nonsingleton B-projection is contained in this T. Two mixed singleton-B members only give the A-part {a}. Mixed plus pure B also cannot supply b. Mixed plus pure A cannot supply both B points, and pure plus pure needs the missing pair {a,b}. Contradiction.

2. Suppose r=0 and m<=8.

Now s>=m+2. Missing T forces at least one mixed member with each of the four singleton A-projections. Let d count singleton A-columns with more than one member, and call the other columns unique. If a pair Q of unique vertices is absent as a mixed A-projection, there is exactly one mixed pair with A-union Q. For Q∪T, only the projected T values could use mixed plus pure A, and at most one other T comes from that fixed mixed pair. No mixed member has A-part Q to combine with pure B. Thus s<=m+1. Consequently all pairs of the 4−d unique vertices are mixed A-projections. The duplicate columns and these pairs spend at least d+binom(4−d,2) of the m−4<=4 extras.

d=0 would need six extras. If d=2 with duplicate vertices x,y and unique u,v, three extras are already spent, including the projection uv. Consider the triples xyu and xyv. The uv projection is in neither. Unless the sole remaining extra has A-part xy, one of the two triples contains no nonsingleton mixed A-part. Then none of its missing T can use a mixed pair or a mixed member plus pure B. If that extra is xy, on either triple exactly one mixed pair can obtain its A-part, since its third vertex is unique. Either way s<=m+1. If d>=3, at most one extra has a nonsingleton A-part. Choose a triple not containing it, and all missing T must be projections, so s<=m.

The final possibility is d=1, with m=8: the extras are one duplicate of singleton x and the three pair projections among the three unique vertices. Fix a triple Q with x and two unique vertices. Exactly two mixed pairs have that A-union and no single mixed member has that projection. Thus at most two missing T can occur outside the mixed B-projection set. Since s>=10, all eight mixed B-projections would have to be distinct and themselves missing. But for any unique singleton A-column i, with its B-projection V_i, every missing T must contain V_i by representing T∪{i}. The V_i themselves would all be missing. Applying this to two unique columns forces their V_i to coincide, contradicting distinctness.

These exhaust m<=8. I would give one review owner the absent-B-projection counting and another the duplicated-column branch. The implementation owner can then add a cardinality row on actual selected memberships crossing a chosen 4+5 cut. All 126 choices are mathematically allowed. Try the smallest set that materially helps on a finite shard and audit the generator's cross predicate. Do not add solver settings or break symmetry jointly with other reductions without checking preservation. A timeout remains UNKNOWN.

