```b = 46 − r − s + 6 ≤ 44, hence r+s ≥ 8.
```

## 1. Both sides have missing pure sets

The previously emailed six-mixed-cut lemma gives r>0. For completeness, s>0 also follows directly: suppose s=0, hence r≥8. Pick a missing S⊆A. For every j∈B, target S∪{j} requires a selected mixed member whose B-part is {j} and whose A-part lies inside S. The five singleton-B support buckets are nonempty. With only six mixed members, at least four of those buckets contain exactly one member. Pick one unique bucket; its fixed nonempty A-part P lies in *every* missing A-set S. If |P|=1, that singleton is itself forced in the basis, so at most 2^3−1=7 supersets can be missing; if |P|≥2, at most 2^(4−|P|)≤4 are available. Both contradict r≥8. Therefore s>0.

## 2. First show r≤4

Let P be the union of the A-parts of all uniquely occupied singleton-B buckets. Each missing S⊆A must contain P. If |P|≥2, there are at most 2^(4−|P|)≤4 possible missing S. If |P|=1, all at least four uniquely occupied mixed members have that same singleton A-part {a}. But one missing T⊆B forces, for each i∈A, a distinct mixed member whose A-part is {i}; the two mixed members outside those four unique buckets cannot supply the three singleton A-parts outside a. Contradiction. Thus |P|≥2 and r≤4.

