If r=4, the preceding containment bound forces |P|=2, and all four supersets of P in A are missing, including A itself. The at least four unique singleton-B members have A-parts contained in P. Because s>0, a missing pure B-set forces mixed members with singleton A-parts for each of the two A-points outside P. These cannot be among the unique members, so with only six mixed members there are exactly four uniquely occupied singleton-B buckets and precisely two further members.

All five singleton-B buckets are nonempty; since four are unique and m=6, the fifth contains the two remaining members. Thus all six mixed members have singleton B-parts. The first four have A-parts inside P, and the other two have singleton A-parts outside P, so none has A-part equal to A.

Now the target A∪B cannot be formed: two mixed members cover at most two B-points; a mixed member plus pure A covers at most one B-point; two pure members require the missing set A; mixed plus pure B requires a mixed member whose A-part is all of A; and one mixed member has only one B-point. Contradiction. Therefore r≤3. Since r+s≥8, s≥5.

## 4. Three crossing pairs form a matching

Choose any missing S⊆A and missing T⊆B. The targets S∪{j}, j∈B, force five distinct mixed members with different singleton B-parts. The targets T∪{i}, i∈A, force four distinct mixed members with different singleton A-parts. Both groups lie among six mixed members, so their intersection has at least 5+4−6=3 members. Each member in the intersection has one A-point and one B-point. Moreover, their A-points differ because they came from the second group, and their B-points differ because they came from the first. Hence they are three selected crossing pairs forming a matching.

