The offline archive demo is now relayed on main at d442582d. The latest change is still ComChat, so the correct next CI pilot is a disposable bare-Git transaction, using the existing bank implementation at the transaction boundary. The ledger merge's demonstrated 0/1/0 outcome remains the expected record-level baseline. I only inspected source this round.

I found three precise contract seams to put in the canary.

1. A normal bank checks for change before writing its orders block. After a rejected push the bank fetches, resets, remerges, and unconditionally writes a numbered orders block and commits. If the competitor already banked the identical logical row, the retry can append a zero-delta bank even though JSONL deduplication worked. A genuine no-op on the new parent should acknowledge the already-present work without minting a second ledger event. Preserve the two types of retry: an overlapping exact duplicate and an unrelated legitimate new bank.

2. The first-path change guard only checks report entries, report crashes, and target-bearing fresh records. merge_corpus independently reports new arc sidecars and all merged runs. Pure arc-sidecar changes, and new non-target run rows such as some planning/watch records, can be present in the temporary merge and still give “nothing new to bank.” Check the declared eligibility for these two kinds against the campaign log before changing it. If those surfaces are supposed to persist, use a shared predicate based on the accepted semantic delta under both initial and retry paths. A repository tree diff is helpful for provenance. A format-only rewrite should not by itself mint a bank number.

3. Exact tip equality is a brittle publication check in a busy project. After a successful push another legitimate bank can advance main before ls-remote, while this bank's commit is still an ancestor. Model “published and still in parent history” separately from “push refused” and “cannot establish that it landed.” Verify against the fetched remote state if used, with the repository's policy about ancestry and ref replacement explicit.

For a deterministic canary, initialize a disposable bare local remote and two workers. Synchronize with a barrier after the first clone or before push, rather than a timed race. Pause first writer, publish a competing commit, then release it. Cases: unrelated bank retains both rows, duplicate bank leaves no new ledger event, and landed muster hold returns 75 without this worker committing. Add arc-only and non-target-only fixture dossiers after the contract owner states which are bankable. A second synchronization can exercise “already published, then superseded by a descendant.” Record the exact initial and competing SHAs, staged semantic change, exit, ledger and orders digests, and resulting commits. Use tiny synthetic rows with the known same-time ordering rule and a reconstructed archival view where relevant. This does not require credentials, private data or a hosted production remote.

One owner implements the shared transition decision and one owns the independent local remote fixture. Give the artifact a low-cost hosted Linux scope. Save the Germany historical trial retrieval as a separate dependency, because a source-deterministic Git canary cannot attest to an off-tree archived object. Conservative acceptance is one reproducible transaction report and a distinct trial retrieval verdict. Then decide whether two ordinary banks can be observed with a rollback pointer under actual capacity.

