As before, r,s count missing nonempty pure A,B sets and r+s>=m+2. Every singleton is present. An orphan below means a missing B-set that does not occur as the B-projection of a mixed member.

Case r>0. Each missing A-set forces a mixed row in every singleton B-column. Put k=m−5<=4, e the number of extras with nonsingleton B-projection, and d=k−e the number of extra singleton rows.

For an orphan T, all 15 nonempty A-sets Q must be representable in Q∪T. At most m values of Q could come from mixed plus pure B, and the others must come from two mixed rows whose B-parts union to T. Mixed plus pure A needs a B-projection equal to T, and two pure sets need T itself. Each orphan of size at least three therefore demands at least 15−m>=6 row pairs. Across all such orphans the number of pairs is at most e(3+d)+binom(e,2), since a nonsingleton B-part can add a singleton in at most three distinct outside columns, plus duplicate rows, and each mixed pair yields only one T. This total is at most 18. An orphan of size two permits only singleton B-rows, and the two eligible columns with multiplicities a,b give at most a+b+a·b Q-values. This can reach 15 only when a=b=3, which spends all four extras as duplicates on these two columns and supplies at most one such orphan.

If d>=3, at least one singleton B-column is still unique, forcing its nonempty A-part into every missing A-set. Thus r<=7. If d=3, e<=1 and the >=3 orphan count is at most one, so s<=2. If d=4, e=0 and only the single pair orphan is possible, so s<=1. In either case r+s<=9, below m+2>=10. If d<=2, let U be the union of the A-parts of the unique singleton B-columns. There are at least 5−d such columns, so choose a B-pair of them different from all e extra projections: binom(5−d,2)>e. Every missing A-set S contains U. If S strictly contains U, use it with that B-pair. Only the unique singleton mixed parts can participate. They cannot supply anything outside U, or both B points when combined with pure A. The pure-pure representation needs S itself. Thus r<=1. There are no size-two orphans, and s<=e+3, so r+s<=e+4<m+2. This closes r>0.

Case r=0. Since a missing B-set T exists, covering T∪{i} forces a mixed row in each singleton A-column. Call a vertex clean if its column has exactly one row. Let c be the number of clean vertices and d=4−c. For every clean i, its row's B-part V_i lies in every missing B-set. At most one distinct missing B-projection can come from the c clean rows: two such missing V_i,V_j would have to contain each other. If c>=1, at most m−c+1 distinct missing T can be mixed B-projections. If c=0, the bound is m.

For an A-set Q absent as a mixed A-projection, let W(Q) count mixed row pairs whose A-parts union to Q. For Q∪T, every orphan T needs one of those pairs and hence s<=m−c+1+W(Q) for c>=1, or s<=m+W(Q) if c=0. It suffices to exhibit an absent Q with W(Q)<=c or <=1 respectively. In particular, all clean pairs must be mixed A-projections. An absent clean pair has W=1. The d duplicate columns and the clean pairs spend at least d+binom(c,2) of at most five extras beyond the four singleton rows.

- c=4 is impossible: six clean pairs are needed.
- c=3: with duplicate vertex x, the duplicate and three clean pairs spend four extras. Consider Q formed by x and two clean vertices. The sole leftover nonsingleton projection can be avoided by choice of Q, leaving only the clean pair and two x rows, so W=2. A leftover singleton x increases that count to 3, still feasible for the argument.
