A size-two orphan can occur only if the column multiplicities a,b satisfy a+b+ab>=15. Neither can equal one (largest other multiplicity is six). A qualifying pair uses at least six rows. Two disjoint qualifying pairs are impossible with ten rows. Two sharing a column have three total masses <=8 after reserving the other two required columns. For shared mass >=5, two others >=2 exceed eight. Shared mass 4 would force both others to be 2, which yields only 14. Shared mass 3 needs both others >=3, also too many; lower shared mass cannot help. Thus at most one pair orphan, and it needs d>=4.

