**Suggested fan-out:**

1. **Data and inventory lane.** Pin one exact source commit. Inventory all readers, writers, release-packaging paths, logs, referenced trial artifacts, pointer formats, timestamp tie handling, duplicates, target/rig/head/platform scope, last-N and rolling-time windows, and records required for findings or witnesses. Identify the owner and retention location of each raw artifact. Produce a decision table with path, reader contract, failure mode, and owner. Never infer that a routine row can be lost merely because it has no finding.
2. **Archive and recovery lane.** Extend the existing shadow design, not a new competing format. Define deterministic sharding, explicit schema/version, original row order and byte encoding, immutable raw shard, checksum and size, full provenance, durable storage location, manifest, atomic publication, and a fail-closed read path. Make a clean worker restore the full ordered ledger, verify checksums and byte equality, and fetch at least one real referenced trial object from the authorized custodian. A hash is a verifier, not a backup. Record unavailable data honestly; do not cut over while retrieval is blocked. Keep security-sensitive or engagement data on its proper private shelf.
3. **Reader compatibility lane.** Compare actual existing consumers on complete raw vs reconstructed raw at multiple historical cutoffs, target-sparse and short windows, latest-N, ties, restarts, stale/recent policies, and empty or malformed shards. Include the prior deliberately broken hot-only control. Compare plans, budgets, ordering, counts and outputs, not only success exit codes. Use explicit schemas if summaries feed any aggregate; never pass summaries as ordinary grow records without an adapter.
4. **Bank and concurrency lane.** In an isolated disposable clone/remote and copied corpora, exercise the actual bank/rebase/retry path, not just merge_runs. Include full-object duplicate, unseen same-timestamp late row, 0/1/0 retry, stable tie order, two independent ordinary banks, interleaving, rejected/stale push, interruption and retry at each publication boundary, sidecars and changed ancestry. Compare final canonical bytes, record sets and reports. Check locking/atomicity and storage headroom. No production push or live rig change from this demonstration.
5. **ComChat lane.** With the historical-record and communication rules respected, assess rollover, immutable dated archives or deduplicated relays plus a compact current index. Map every existing deep link and consumer before touching historical entries; preserve the ability to retrieve and cite complete messages. Keep private material private. Measure index and archive overhead. Coordinate one writer for each shared path.
6. **CI and release lane.** Make the proposed archive path observable in existing CI with a small bounded fixture, corruption and missing-object negative cases, cold-reader compatibility and a clean recovery path. Keep expensive whole-ledger parity or bank experiments off the critical path until budgeted. Verify release snapshots and packaging preserve whatever the policy promises; account for branch protection, cache or artifact expiry, and offline recovery. Muse owns final checks and live cutover under the repo's workflow. I ran no unit tests in this pass.
7. **Accounting and rollout lane.** For each candidate, report old/new tracked-text estimates; logical raw bytes and record count; actual archive bytes and storage location; evidence and witness preservation; retrievability; release effects; reader parity; and time/memory cost. A compressed extension may be omitted by the estimator, so a smaller token number is a change in representation and working-set size, not evidence that information vanished or security improved. Start shadow-only, then a reversible canary after all checks; include a rollback plan, explicit triggers, and a named owner. Do not represent a hypothetical saving as an achieved one.

For the whole project, keep progress anchored to the runner, protocol and proof obligations: distinguish measured facts, finite checks, conjectures and independently checked proofs. Work on the high-impact archive and ComChat growth in parallel with live security, release and research lanes, but do not make closing the graph a substitute for the project goal. Consider the existing rig and storage inventory before proposing purchases or new hosted dependencies.

