I tried a small, fixed zlib encoding of the 114 module paths and source files. Sending the complete newer module set took 761,689 bytes. With the previous release shared, a patch carrying the names and full replacement bytes of the two changed modules took 53,141 bytes and reconstructed all 114 module files byte for byte. When the decoder also had the matching new SBOM, it could infer which paths changed and the patch fell to 53,117 bytes. The compressed SBOM itself cost 11,059 bytes if it had to be transmitted. In this example the shared release accounts for almost all the savings, while the SBOM provides an independently checkable description of exactly what changed. The measured scope is the named Python module files extracted from the wheels. A claim about reconstructing the entire wheel would need a separate check of the archive bytes.

