```HOMING PIGEON — HIGH-LEVEL WORK PLAN FOR MUSE
Get it working; make it work better over time.
25 November 2026, Pacific. Planning source: main 2a5207fcd77a27d8cc916b1d186473aeed96570e.

MISSION

Build a repeatable improvement loop for the existing product: small verifiable change → larger, budgeted exploration → small, independently readable result → next change. Deliver at least one week of meaningful work, with a second-week continuation and checkpointable tasks that can safely run longer. The conductor sets direction; Muse coordinates the live project. Start from GOAL.md, the current standing orders and existing work, and refresh before changing anything. The owner’s goal is a secure-by-default multi-platform runner, documented protocol drafts and v4 security model, and traceable evidence for each v4 security constraint. Token counts, chart convergence and commit volume are measurements, not the objective.

THIS IS ONE CAMPAIGN, NOT A REQUEST TO DO ALL IDEAS AT ONCE

Reconcile existing ComChat asks and merge this plan into them; the attached brain dump is background, not an additional backlog. Keep working on any already-authorized higher-priority incident, red release, or running job. Create disjoint lane cards with paths, owners, source commit, dependencies, budget, measurable output, and next decision. Use existing worktrees and desk practices; integrate serially and keep unfinished features dark where appropriate. Production changes, releases and project checks belong to the existing live seats. I ran no tests in preparing this plan.

CORE RULES

1. Use the rigs and accounts the conductor owns and has authorized. First obtain an up-to-date read-only capacity/health picture using an approved path. The fleet inventory is historical and Nuremberg was marked retired; do not assume machines or their earlier capacities are available. A spending ceiling is not permission for new paid resources. Preserve headroom for normal work and release tasks.
2. Each job has a pinned source SHA, owner, scope, input/seed or shard, CPU/wall/memory/disk budget, checkpoint, output location and stop conditions. For missing prerequisites, lost isolation, stale capacity, resource saturation or invalid evidence: stop or checkpoint, report why, and use UNKNOWN or BLOCKED where appropriate. Do not send test traffic to unowned services or publish private data.
3. Preserve crash data, findings, witnesses, original records and existing links under their correct handling rules. No live archive cutover until actual recovery, reader compatibility, full bank behavior and rollback have evidence. No history rewrite for space savings.
4. Keep statuses honest: planned, running, completed within scope, failed, unknown, blocked, skipped, superseded or deferred. A relay is not implementation, a docs-only green run is not a full release check, a hash is not storage, a signed claim is not proof it is true, and finite experiments do not settle P versus NP.
5. Record actions and evidence so another agent can take over without asking the conductor routine questions. Keep project-wide decisions and behavior changes within existing policy; do not silently change owner-chosen defaults such as the documented Windows SYSTEM setup. No automatic new release gate without the applicable authorization.

DAY 1 — BASELINE, DEDUPLICATE, DISPATCH

Refresh main and pin one SHA. Read the desk, standing orders, GOAL and active board. Triage existing red or release-blocking signals first. Reconcile recent ComChat into a small number of work families: archive and bank, CI/proof routing, per-platform isolation and recovery, release evidence, proof/math research, charts/docs, and long-run scheduling. Merge the overlapping B(9)/B(10) refinements and my prior archive emails rather than filing them repeatedly. For each family record landed / partial / active / blocked / unknown and evidence link; mark recommendations as optional until selected. Obtain a present-tense inventory of authorized resources and existing workloads, with resource ceilings and alert path.

Deliverable: one concise baseline and lane board, including exact source, inventory timestamp and unknowns. No fabricated completion percentage.

DAYS 2–3 — THREE INDEPENDENT LANES, ONE SHARED EVIDENCE CONTRACT

LANE A: EFFECTIVE SECURITY AND RECOVERY. On available disposable platforms, inspect actual install posture: OS version, principal, sandbox mechanism, effective file/network reach, authentication/policy/disclosure state and failure behavior. Choose one real security boundary and one benign synthetic canary, with a denial control; document the exact config and whether the mechanism is enforce, audit or absent. Investigate signed-job revocation, restarts and result durability with bounded fault injection in a local lab. Clearly separate unknown external side effects from exactly-once execution claims. On unavailable systems report UNKNOWN. Produce a per-platform matrix and one reproducible case; do not assume a dated posture note still reflects the current source.

LANE B: RECOVERABLE FUZZ EVIDENCE. Continue the existing Nov 19 shadow-shard pilot; do not invent a new competing one. Independently retrieve and verify one referenced trial object through its authorized custodian. In parallel, demonstrate original full ordered ledger reconstruction, checksums, missing/corrupt-object refusal and the reader/scheduler parity cases already identified. Keep a broken hot-only negative control. Preserve each raw record and any private material. The prior 899-of-974 pilot and selected 0/1/0 merge demonstration are partial work, not a production migration.

LANE C: PROPERTY-TO-CODE EVIDENCE. Select one v4 property and one supported backend. Write the narrow proposition, source paths, effective config, platform, assumptions, model bounds, and how each model transition corresponds to a real execution. Record a passing example, a rejection/negative control and one deliberately corrupted trace. Run model checks only where the project has a supported checker and report actual scope. A proof of a model under assumptions does not automatically prove the whole product.

All lanes use a minimal common evidence envelope: source SHA and artifact digests; what was intended; what actually ran; inputs, environment and resource bounds; real outcome (including skipped/failed/unknown); evidence location and retrieval; and independent reviewer. Reuse existing tools before adding infrastructure.

DAYS 4–5 — COMPATIBILITY, CI AND REAL RESUMABILITY

1. Archive/bank: create a deterministic, disposable local bare-remote scenario with two workers and a barrier. Exercise an exact duplicate, an unseen equal-timestamp record, 0/1/0 retry behavior, unrelated competing bank, no-op competing bank, and a successful publish followed by a later descendant. Check actual transaction-level final records, order, sidecars, reports, commits and idempotency. Review the contract for arc-only and non-target-only data before deciding whether they should cause a bank. These are source-level concerns, not confirmed bugs. Recheck current implementation first.
2. CI routing: inspect the previously raised possible cross-scope rename and formal-model-as-docs issues. Reproduce or refute with small local fixtures in the project’s normal authorized checking process. If real, choose a minimal fix preserving legitimate docs-only speed and safe fallbacks; show which checks ran and skipped at the exact SHA. Do not call a docs-scoped result a full proof or platform run.
3. Long-run work: implement or extend a small manifest/checkpoint contract for one existing job type. Pin target, toolchain, partition, input, resource cap and output. Demonstrate interruption, resume and a stale/mismatched checkpoint refusal on a disposable example. Show PARTIAL or UNKNOWN rather than fabricating completion. Keep raw data durable and private where required.

Deliverable: a reviewable evidence bundle and a short before/after report for each lane. One writer per shared path, serial integration, independent fresh-context review.

DAYS 6–7 — ONE BOUNDED RESEARCH EXPERIMENT AND RELEASE VIEW

Research: start from the existing finite-map fixture or one reviewed weak-basis subcase. Pin its exact encoding, universe and checker. For the finite map, independently validate the listed universe (including its 2,952-formula three-variable scope), SAT witnesses and a small baseline; for a negative bounded-search claim check completeness of the relevant search, not just a satisfying witness. A deliberately insufficient run must return UNKNOWN. Preserve shard manifest, missing work, negative controls and tool versions. Reconcile later B(9) mixed-cut and B(10) bound refinements as evolving claims and independently challenge the strongest selected statement; do not treat a ComChat relay as a proof or claim P versus NP solved.

Extend the already-discussed SBOM / historical-wheel pilot only if resources permit: use a chronological held-out cohort, reproducible input hashes, full and delta baselines, and total costs including decoder/model/shared-reference bytes. A neutral or worse result is useful. State conclusions only for the specified corpus and representation.

Release and integration: report exact commit-to-workflow-to-artifact links, relevant successful/failed/skipped jobs, artifact hashes and expiry/retrieval plan. Distinguish a docs-only build from a release matrix. If the existing authorized release process reaches its normal verified gate, follow it; otherwise leave a clear handoff and no implied release. Review restore/rollback and any owner decision separately. Do not misrepresent a future mathematical chart line as an observed count.

WEEK 2 AND CONTINUOUS OPERATION

Expand only lanes whose controls and outputs are working. Grow one bounded fuzz/research cohort at a time with checkpoint IDs and an independently checked sample; extend archive coverage only after recovery and compatibility; cover more platform/posture cells as actual rigs permit; and integrate the property ledger with change-driven evidence invalidation. Review results at least daily in compact form: what changed, what actually ran, what failed, what is waiting, budget consumed, next safe action and where raw evidence can be recovered. Weekly, independently challenge one important claim and retire, revise or expand a lane based on measured results.

HANDOFF: WHEN CAN THE CONDUCTOR WALK AWAY?

Give a factual status card: exact main/release SHA; each active job and actual owner; machine and resource budget; next checkpoint and deadline; how to stop and recover; current blockers; alert route; release status and what was skipped; and whether work really continues in a durable executor. If live job state is not observable, say so. An intentionally paused, recoverable system is a valid safe state. Do not claim continuous background operation merely because this email was sent.

Please post or send a concise first checkpoint after baseline and continue the lanes using the normal desk. Preserve the longer background text as optional context; do not dump it into ComChat as hundreds of independent tasks.
```
