## 25-Nov-1:38am-PST, ChatGPT: For Muse — Homing Pigeon security evidence research and eight-day plan

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse,

Andrew asked for at least a week of focused work on his release, SBOM, and security evidence idea. The full eight-day plan is below, with two stretch days. The ZIP bundles this plan with the research background, prior measured results, and the controls and source references.

Start in parallel. Deliver the first independently checked release receipt or exact blocker by day two using the evidence already available for v3.18.35.5. The later milestones cover exact reconstruction, historical forecasting, scoped evidence reuse, and a retention check. Please send Andrew the brief readout described in the plan.

Homing Pigeon high-level technical plan
Prepared for Muse and Andrew, November 25, 2026
Eight working days, with two focused stretch days

Muse,

Andrew wants you to take the lead on his idea. Use the release, its SBOM,
the CI history, and the fuzz ledger as complementary records. Make one
security verdict easier for an owner to verify, then measure which part
of the release history does real work in shortening or explaining the
evidence. background.txt is the research brief for this plan.

The first milestone is a two-day result using existing artifacts. Start
the technical lanes together. End the eighth working day with the
strongest reproducible result in each viable lane, an explicit record
of what remains open, and one recommendation to Andrew.

Starting point

The baseline reviewed was 2a5207fcd77a27d8cc916b1d186473aeed96570e,
and the latest published release observed at that baseline was
v3.18.35.5. Existing relays have the original question and numbers.
At kickoff, reconcile the current head and any work already in flight
against this baseline. Use any finished equivalent instead of duplicating
it. Record the resolved source SHA and artifact hashes in each result.
The published history, retained CI records, and local scratch analysis
are the inputs for this pass. Preserve the normal release train and
use the existing unit-test results.

The work has three primary lanes and two bounded companions

A. Release test receipts and actual execution provenance.
 Owner builds a shadow evidence index; verifier checks it independently.
B. Release reconstruction and Chaitin-style cost accounting.
 Owner builds an encoder; verifier compares decoded output with the release.
C. Historical fuzz forecasting.
 Owner curates and scores the cohort; verifier audits the time split,
 features and eligibility independently.
D. One v4 claim and an exact evidence reuse rule.
 A separate proof reader writes the observation contract and checks
 controls against the verifier.
E. Retention and owner-facing audit.
 A reader independently validates the lossless-ledger contract and
 tries to recover one real evidence chain from a detached release.

If reviewer capacity is tight, prioritize A, B and the small D check.
Keep C's data audit useful even if forecasting cannot proceed. Give
E's archive extension to a spare reader only after pinning the earlier
shadow result. These lanes can complete without waiting for each other.
They share source identities and reporting conventions.

Day 1 — Pin the evidence and open the lanes

1. Pin the main SHA, published tags and digests, reader/checker versions,
 and any known release or proof changes since the baseline.
2. Open release v3.18.35.5 as the first receipt example. The public
 Python build provenance names 45581902934/1. The publisher is
 45583185804/1. Both point to source commit
 d0a933e2f5451b5c1369a5fa47ccee8bca88c404. Resolve the publisher,
 builder, and each suite producer as separate records.
3. Freeze the release reconstruction sample before viewing delta costs.
 Choose twenty eligible historical transitions of one wheel flavor
 if available. Include v3.18.35.2 to v3.18.35.4 as a calibration
 case outside the held-out score. Record each exclusion and its reason.
4. Freeze an initial fuzz target, outcome, historical availability
 rules, and time split. Several banks from one stable coverage profile
 are a better start than folding incomparable targets together.
5. Pick one small replay predicate. For a real release check, archive
 inventory is a good start. For a v4-labelled exercise, a synthetic
 P11 result-size receipt is precise. Select the scope before setting
 expected outcomes.
6. Assign a second reader to the first deliverable. Ask them to try a
 wrong-attempt or wrong-artifact match and document what it would take
 for the system to accept it.

Day 2 — Send Andrew the first result

Build a shadow receipt for the pinned v3.18.35.5 sample. A read-only
metadata check identified four distinct suite jobs in the builder.
Linux is 139093924114, Windows parts are 139094520406 and
139094520325, and macOS is 139097152273. Treat these as starting
references. Verify actual job/attempt metadata and artifact linkage.
Record the difference between a GitHub artifact-archive digest and a
digest of the log bytes after reading it. If a log is unavailable,
retain the verified metadata with an explicit availability status.

Produce a small reviewable packet. Include one exact chain from
release tag to suite and evidence, one plausible join rejected by the
checker, and a second reader's status. Supply the original observed
release verdict and the roles of any advisory suites. If no retained
log can be verified, report precisely which link is missing. A clear
missing link is a valid day-two result.

Days 3–4 — Build the small instruments

Lane A implements or sketches the additive suite_evidence fields using
the existing snapshot/provenance shapes. Keep each platform and Windows
matrix part distinct. Carry the actual producer attempt even if another
attempt is now latest. Test the checker with frozen receipt fixtures
for wrong source, wrong log/attempt, duplicate candidate, pending
advisory macOS, and expired or unavailable evidence. Use a real
historical partial retry if evidence is accessible and a marked
synthetic fixture otherwise. Hand the prototype and schema to a reader.

Lane B reproduces the original wheel-module result. Use the earlier
release alone as the main delta baseline. Add the newer authenticated
SBOM as an explicitly shared input. Include a stale manifest or
names-only arm where feasible. Record the full standalone code, both
conditional delta sizes, the paid SBOM cost, the cost of the base when
it has to travel, and the decoder/metadata overhead when material.
Check every named byte using a separate comparison with the wheel.
For extra rigor, re-render deterministic SBOM fields from checked source
and count the release-specific metadata required to match exact bytes.
Keep that measurement a separate target.

Lane C produces the join census before scoring anything. Resolve the
actual source head, release equality where demonstrable, and historical
first availability. A bank may land well after the timestamp in a row.
Do not fit a historical predictor using records that had yet to land.
Record what is observed, what is reconstructed from exact source, and
what remains uncertain. Freeze an eligibility table before outcomes are
scored. Show the Germany checkout case as a worked example of how the
joiner keeps deployment identity distinct from experiment identity.

Lane D specifies the full observation interface and verifies one scope
independently. A known-positive and known-negative example are both
required for the check to mean anything. Include a changed declared
input whose fresh result still passes. The reuse decision must still
detect the changed input. Keep verifier failures and missing reads
visible. Use the byte equality rule described in the brief and name
any collision-resistance assumption when using fingerprints.

Days 5–6 — Score held-out evidence and make the audit portable

Run Lane B's frozen exact decoder on every eligible held-out transition
with the target release bytes reserved for the oracle. Every mismatch
ends the affected exact-reconstruction claim and becomes an example
to investigate. Publish the number of transitions decoded exactly,
the excluded count, paired sizes, and runtime.

Run Lane C's time-ordered historical forecasts. Compare a smoothed
history baseline, additional pre-run context, and that same context
plus module-change inputs. Score all arms on the same records and use
one comparable shuffled/stale control. Predeclare simple choices and
retain the 2.8 percent historical base rate only as a starting
reference, not the held-out base rate. Report paired log loss in bits,
especially rare positive outcomes by block. Capture one context-equal
pair with different outcomes if found, or a representative prediction
error. Record the unresolved cohort statistic even when a fit is too
weak or a join too incomplete to score.

Lane A hands the shadow output to a reader starting from the detached
release. Have them distinguish what is verifiable with the pinned
trust inputs from what requires raw log retrieval. Check release
attestations against the expected source and verified artifact bytes
where access allows. Include one exact link to a v4 evidence record
only when its scope supports the association.

Lane E independently rechecks the earlier ledger shadow claim. Select
a second bounded cohort if the same fixture can be reproduced.
Preserve findings, unique witnesses, header/order/counts and the raw
locator contract. Confirm one missing or mismatch state with a small
local fixture. Any actual rig-local object retrieval uses the current
custodian's existing access and is a separate, recorded check.

Days 7–8 — Independent review, decision and handoff

Perform the acceptance review with readers who did not produce the
initial conclusion. For the deterministic replay rule, compare every
transported verdict with a fresh result of the small independent
verifier on the held-out set. Report which query differences forced
fresh checking even when the eventual verdict remained the same.
Review both exact compression and forecasting accounting for any
future-data, implicit dictionary or identity-lookup advantage.
Retain the smallest disagreement as a named case with its input
identities and expected/observed answers.

Deliver these in the day-eight packet.

1. One short plain-text summary for Andrew. Lead with a verified
 result or exact blocker, then give counts, scope, and his next
 decision. Include the claim, source version, and one easy-to-open
 piece of evidence for each completed lane.
2. A shadow release-evidence manifest and compatibility notes. Include
 an additive schema or draft patch only where the first receipt
 demonstrates a need, existing data can populate it, and a reader can
 verify the result. Record publication and future gate use as distinct
 project decisions.
3. A release reconstruction table with cold and shared-context accounts,
 a replayable example, and both favorable and unfavorable results.
4. A historical join and prediction report with exact denominators,
 the pre-run feature contract, and a later interval held out.
5. A one-page scoped proof or a verified counterexample to the reuse
 contract, with fixtures and the independent checker's result.
6. A retention status card distinguishing byte restoration, receipt
 metadata, raw object retrieval, and any proposed rollout. Include
 a rollback outline if a rollout is ever separately selected.

Acceptance and stopping conditions

Release receipts. Every verified row has a unique exact source, job
and actual attempt; it binds to the identified evidence bytes or is
explicitly metadata-only. A nonpassing or missing origin cannot be
turned into a verified passing receipt. Distinct matrix parts stay
distinct. The shadow report leaves the recorded release verdict intact.

Byte reconstruction. Exact agreement on every named path and byte in
the chosen module set. Name the target format and both charged and
shared context in every total. A smaller delta is demonstrated only
against the stated baseline. A zero SBOM gain still completes the
experiment.

Forecasting. Each scored predictor sees only its declared pre-result
inputs. Align scored populations and document exclusions. Any recorded
prediction gain must clear the frozen baseline and controls on the
held-out set to be called a gain. An inconclusive forecast still yields
a useful data census and a scoped result for the declared window.

Evidence reuse. Every required control behaves as declared and every
eligible reused verdict agrees with fresh checking on the held-out
pairs. A disagreement invalidates this reuse rule until understood.
A shadow architecture that lacks complete observations stays
unresolved for that check. Report input bytes, verifier work, cache
cost and retrieval work before making a savings claim.

Retention. Exact ledger bytes, timestamp order, counts, duplicate/retry
results and reader/scheduler outputs at the declared checkpoints.
Keep original data authoritative through the proposed shadow work.
Pause advancement at a retrieval, identity, completeness, or restore
mismatch, and retain the minimum repro and original data for review.

Record the measured status at day eight, including outcomes that
contradict the hypothesis. If a lane requires inaccessible evidence,
close the available part, state the missing link, and keep the other
lanes running. A small finite proof, a named counterexample, and an
unresolved result are different deliverables with valid stopping rules.

Days 9–10 — Stretch, if the first packet is complete

Choose at most one of these first.

* Broaden the evidence manifest to an existing direct release or an
 actual partial retry, and independently reproduce the exact
 producer-attempt binding with explicit unavailable states.
* Check the P6 clause 7 model bridge. Freeze the same-path,
 same-candidate-mtime retry sequence. An unreadable observation
 retains the old config and marker. When the same unacknowledged
 candidate becomes readable on a later invocation, it applies once
 and advances the marker. A third identical observation is a no-op.
 This is a bounded model and source bridge, conditional on that
 subsequent invocation. Use the existing conformance evidence and
 independently reason about the same-path fixture.
* Scope one off-box restoration through existing authorized access and
 record a verified underlying object. If the archive lane is ready,
 design a disposable two-writer canary around ref rejection, retry,
 exact order and muster behavior. A successful scratch restore
 remains separate from an everyday operational cutover.
* Use a finite interpreter or a recorded tiny SAT candidate as a
 calibration for certificate checking and exact stopping. Name the
 clock, scope, independent witness verifier, and what a scoped pass
 says. Across-size complexity claims need their own theorem.

Quick update format for Andrew

What we checked.
What the evidence showed, with count or exact example.
What an independent reader could reproduce.
What remains open and why.
The one next choice worth making.

