## 25-Nov-05:26PM-PST, Muse: The queues Worker job gate takes the results route's treatment: encrypted jobs are accepted

The finding the Anna pipe build lane brought back this afternoon is fixed, at the owner's direct order to push the backlog. The shipped queues Worker's job posting gate refused the protocol's default encrypted job: it compared the signed payload's job_id member against the subject id through a reader that returned undefined when the member was absent, and an hp_enc envelope carries no job_id member at the payload level, so every encrypted job was refused with a 400 naming a job_id mismatch between subject and body, and the pipe had to compose signed cleartext jobs instead.

Supposition first, as this project does it. The supposition was written in the repository and pushed before any measurement (docs/infrastructure-documents/project/queues-worker-job-gate-supposition-2026-11-25.md, committed at 04:48 PM PST today): a correctly signed encrypted job would measure 400 with the mismatch error and store nothing, its signed cleartext twin would measure 202 and store, and giving the job gate the results route's treatment would flip exactly the first answer and no other. The measurement matched exactly, and the conclusions section of the same document now records both measurements, before and after.

The change itself is small, in runner/cloud/queues-worker/src/index.js. The job gate now reads the payload's job_id claim with payloadId, the one reader the results route already used, and refuses only when a claim is present and differs from the subject id; a present claim that is not a string reads as null and still refuses. The superseded reader, bodyJobId, is removed, so one reader now serves both routes and the two gates cannot drift apart on this question again. A comment at the gate states the reason the check exists at all: a job stored under the subject with a different id inside could never be replaced by the real job for that id, while an encrypted envelope carries its id in the subject only and the runner binds it by decrypting under that id.

Proof of work. A new family in the Worker's fuzz suite, shotgun41, adds 24 tests pinning the gate's answers: the encrypted envelope accepted and leased back to the runner under the subject id byte for byte, the encrypted shape beside a differing claim refused and beside a matching claim stored, the absent claim accepted with and without encryption, a nested claim treated as no claim, the present claims that are not strings refused, case and padding and length differences refused, bookend and run length sweeps over the claimed id, injection shaped claims refused, a duplicate encrypted submit acknowledged with the first envelope staying, and a refusal leaving the way clear for the real job. Ten older tests moved with the rule, each recorded rather than quietly rewritten: the shotgun35 family and one shotgun40 test had pinned the old refusal for absent, nested only, misspelled key and unreadable claims, and those assertions now pin the acceptance under the subject id, with dated correction notes in both family headers explaining that the reading those families exist to pin is unchanged and every present claim keeps its answer. The full Worker suite stands at 899 tests, all passing, none skipped. The Python queue batteries (transport queues, queue pipe edges, send job queue edges, hosting queue edges, platinum queues, query worker, skip registry) stand at 733 tests, OK, with the 4 standing registered skips and no new skip added. The shrink guard is clean.

What this unblocks, stated with its limit. The Anna bridge's signed cleartext composition was a staging posture forced by this gate; it is forced no longer. Pointing the bridge at the protocol's default encrypted composition, and the real runner verification the owner requires before any of the pipe is called working, still stand ahead and are unchanged by this landing. The Anna design document's build state section carries a dated update saying the same.

