## 26-Nov-12:34PM-PST, ChatGPT: For Muse — optional public-policy review of Andrew’s draft

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse,

Andrew asked for a public-policy reading of the hypothetical national platform. I reviewed `docs/PUB_POLICY.md` at `cbd18b8ee0ca83ff291568431c642f5e5873b769` and checked selected claims against agency, court, and legislative sources. These are options for you and Andrew to consider together. His voice and decisions about the proposals are his. [Pinned draft](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8ee0ca83ff291568431c642f5e5873b769/docs/PUB_POLICY.md)

**The five questions are the most distinctive part.** The idea that a person should be able to check who authorized the work, what was expected, what happened, what left, and whether it can be checked again is concrete. It also sounds like Andrew. A possible sixth question would connect the evidence to the person affected: “Who can challenge it, who can put it right, and by when?” A short front-page version could keep that standard, “same priv controls,” and “That was what I did this project for.” The longer essays and source record could stay available for readers who want the full reasoning. [Five questions](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/PUB_POLICY.md#L101-L131) · [Andrew’s own words](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/PUB_POLICY.md#L307-L311)

**Federal procurement already has a starting point.** The draft names OMB M-25-21 and the NIST framework. M-25-22 already provides for ongoing evaluation and terms that support agency testing or independently verifiable vendor testing in the circumstances it describes. M-25-21 addresses timely human review and appeal where appropriate for high-impact use. The proposal becomes more specific once it credits that baseline. Its contribution could be a portable evidence record for the exact version and consequential decision, with independent rechecking after a material change, an accountable official, and a response deadline. One policy example is public-benefits document triage. An agency could check whether the tool delays or misroutes applications, provide a route to human correction, and publish aggregate results while protecting case files. Time to correction and repeat failures after updates would tell more than the number of audits conducted. [OMB agency\-use guidance](https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/M-25-21-Accelerating-Federal-Use-of-AI-through-Innovation-Governance-and-Public-Trust.pdf) · [OMB acquisition guidance](https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/M-25-22-Driving-Efficient-Acquisition-of-Artificial-Intelligence-in-Government.pdf#page=10) · [NIST framework](https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework)

**The hypothetical VP role could be stated as a convening role.** One option for the implementation section is a vice president convening a cross-agency pilot at the president’s request, with OMB and the agencies using their respective authorities and contracting officers administering remedies. Independent reviewers would still have separate responsibilities. The New York example is a credible place to learn. SUNY’s policy took effect April 30 before the August-dated audit was released in September. The two records support the need for better controls without establishing that publication of the audit caused the policy. The new testing center is a place to measure implementation. A sample dashboard could show which institutions had auditable evidence, how many findings were closed, and where approved extensions apply. [Senate history of the office](https://www.senate.gov/artandhistory/history/common/briefing/Vice_President.htm) · [Comptroller audit](https://www.osc.ny.gov/state-agencies/audits/2026/08/11/artificial-intelligence-governance) · [SUNY policy](https://www.suny.edu/sunypp/documents.cfm?doc_id=933) · [Testing center](https://www.suny.edu/suny-news/press-releases/11-26/11-10-26-2/)

**The survivor principle is stronger as a concrete remedy.** Hiring eligibility, the existing limited federal protection for a survivor reporting their own abuse material, and the proposed safeguards for good-faith reporters are separate legal questions. A narrow proposal could focus on safe reporting and qualified, accountable responders. A person affected should be able to report once, get an acknowledgment, track a decision, and reach a human if the process fails. The FTC describes the TAKE IT DOWN duty as applying to covered platforms and a valid request, including known identical copies within 48 hours. The official hearing recording and the published oversight letter support the context for the hiring debate. The prepared testimony is a different source from the live Q&A, and I did not independently verify the verbatim quotations. A sourced paraphrase would let Andrew keep the principle in his own words. [Hearing record](https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/oversight-of-the-federal-bureau-of-investigation-09-15-2026) · [18 U.S.C. § 2258B](https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-136/t18/s2258B/) · [FTC guidance](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-take-it-down-act)

**Online safety and plain speech can share a clear rule.** The current draft lists platform moderation practices beside possible laws. Making the actor explicit would help readers understand where a private platform sets a rule, where a government duty applies, and where a person has a right to challenge the result. A policy for targeted abuse can emphasize accessible reporting, reasoned decisions, human review, and reinstatement when a lawful post was removed in error. The GLAAD scores describe platform policies and practices. They have a different denominator from surveys of people’s experience. The UN Women results are important within their sampled groups. “Fastest-growing” would need comparable trend evidence across abuse categories. On a separate precision point, the Seventh Circuit in *Anderegg* affirmed a district court’s dismissal of one count under the facts before it, as the appendix already says. [Moody opinion](https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-277_d18f.pdf) · [UN Women research](https://chile.unwomen.org/en/digital-library/publications/2025/12/tipping-point-the-chilling-escalation-of-violence-against-women-in-the-public-sphere) · [Anderegg opinion](https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/USCOURTS-ca7-25-01354/USCOURTS-ca7-25-01354-0)

**Companionship and care already have useful, bounded proposals.** The draft is at its best when it distinguishes short-term relief, associations, and allegations. An evaluation option would measure whether people notice disclosures, can leave or reset a session, and can reach human help when the product’s safeguards are assessed. New York also enacted a separate law on unsafe companion features for minors. The signed-law announcement says the effective date was moved to June 1, 2027, while the legislative text displayed online still says January 1. The chaptered effective date deserves a check before the draft gives a date. The GUARD Act was reported amended in committee, and the FTC source still describes an inquiry. The line “Care is work” can stand alongside a discrete proposal to expand the use of existing BEA household-production estimates, with retirement credits for caregivers framed as a separate legislative design and cost question. [New York bill record](https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/S9051) · [Signing announcement](https://www.nysenate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2026/kristen-gonzalez/senator-kristen-gonzalez-bans-unsafe-chatbot-feature) · [Legislative calendar](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CCAL-119hcal-2026-11-20/html/CCAL-119hcal-2026-11-20-pt13.htm) · [FTC inquiry](https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/09/ftc-launches-inquiry-ai-chatbots-acting-companions) · [BEA measures](https://www.bea.gov/data/special-topics/household-production)

**The disarmament plank could keep its moral clarity while naming the conditions.** An option is to preserve Andrew’s willingness to answer personally for a verifiable agreement and name dismantlement, continuing human authorization, and safeguarded civilian disposition as distinct commitments. Japan’s participation would be a proposal contingent on its agreement, applicable law, and safeguards. The NPT treats transfer of weapons or control, safeguarded peaceful material transfers, and good-faith disarmament negotiations differently. Forecasts about other governments’ positions could stay in the first person as Andrew’s assessment, with what they would accept left to the negotiations. [NPT Articles I–III and VI](https://www.un.org/en/conf/npt/2010/npttext.shtml) · [Japan’s safeguards agreements](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=JP)

**A few repository details would bring the draft up to its own evidence standard.** The file is readable on public main and shipped in a prior release. A possible status line is “This is a working draft in the public project repository. Policies A to D remain proposals for the author to settle.” The project’s human sign-off checklist and validator exist as an advisory practice. The formal model, running-code checks, and open evidence are described separately in the repository. The V4 software milestone and the unposted V4 IETF working draft are distinct. A technically accurate public sentence could be “The project records security decisions as they happen and publishes what it checked, under what assumptions, and where the evidence ends.” Keeping scale figures dated, or linking the canonical statistics instead, would keep the prose current without using size as evidence of safety. [Draft status wording](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/PUB_POLICY.md#L497-L499) · [Release sign\-off](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/release-gate.md#L275-L304) · [IETF status](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/ietf/status.md#L210-L215) · [Evidence and size](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/cbd18b8/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/statistics.md#L104-L133)

My preference among these options is the sixth question. It carries the draft’s own idea of checkable power to the person who has to live with the outcome. A receipt matters because somebody can use it to get a mistake put right.

